window.DECKS=[{"id": "t1", "code": "T1", "name": "FCC rules", "exam": 6, "G": {"T1A": "Purpose, definitions, coordinators, RACES", "T1B": "Frequencies, modes and power", "T1C": "Licenses, call signs and renewal", "T1D": "Authorized and prohibited transmissions", "T1E": "Control operators and control types", "T1F": "Identification, repeaters, third party, clubs"}, "Q": [["T1A01", "Which of the following is part of the Basis and Purpose of the Amateur Radio Service?", ["Providing personal radio communications for as many citizens as possible", "Providing communications for international contesting", "Advancing skills in the technical and communication phases of the radio art", "All these choices are correct"], 2, "Part 97.1 lists five purposes. Advancing technical and communication skills is one. Personal radio for everyone describes CB, and contesting isn't listed."], ["T1A02", "Which agency regulates and enforces the rules for the Amateur Radio Service in the United States?", ["ARRL", "Homeland Security", "The FCC", "All these choices are correct"], 2, "The FCC writes and enforces the rules. The ARRL is a membership organization, not a regulator."], ["T1A03", "What do the FCC rules state regarding the use of a phonetic alphabet for station identification in the Amateur Radio Service?", ["It is required when transmitting emergency messages", "It is encouraged when using phone emissions", "It is required when in contact with foreign stations", "All these choices are correct"], 1, "Phonetics are encouraged on phone, never required. Not even emergencies or foreign contacts make them mandatory."], ["T1A04", "How do you receive official notification of your new license and call sign after passing the exam?", ["Email from the FCC with a link to download the license grant", "USPS First-Class Mail from the FCC", "Email from the lead volunteer examiner", "USPS First-Class Mail from the lead volunteer examiner"], 0, "The FCC emails you a link to download your license. Nothing comes by mail anymore."], ["T1A05", "What proves that the FCC has issued an operator/primary license grant?", ["A printed copy of the certificate of successful completion of examination", "An email notification from the NCVEC granting the license", "The license appears in the FCC ULS database", "All these choices are correct"], 2, "Your license exists when it shows up in the FCC's ULS database. The CSCE paper is only proof you passed."], ["T1A06", "On which of the following HF frequencies can automatically controlled amateur propagation beacons be found?", ["Anywhere in the Technician portion of 10 meters", "Anywhere, if they are registered with the FCC", "In a specific portion of each of the HF Technician bands", "On ten meters, between 28.200 MHz and 28.300 MHz"], 3, "Automatic beacons on HF live in one slice: 28.200 to 28.300 MHz on 10 meters."], ["T1A07", "What is the FCC Part 97 definition of a space station?", ["Any satellite orbiting Earth", "A manned satellite orbiting Earth", "An amateur station located more than 50 km above Earth's surface", "An amateur station using amateur radio satellites for relay of signals"], 2, "Space station means an amateur station more than 50 km above the Earth's surface. It doesn't have to be a satellite."], ["T1A08", "Who recommends transmit/receive channels for repeater and auxiliary stations?", ["A Frequency Spectrum Manager appointed by the FCC", "A Volunteer Frequency Coordinator recognized by local amateurs", "An FCC Regional Field Office", "The International Telecommunication Union"], 1, "A Volunteer Frequency Coordinator recommends repeater channels. Volunteer, chosen by local hams, not the FCC."], ["T1A09", "Who selects a Frequency Coordinator?", ["The FCC Office of Spectrum Management and Coordination Policy", "The local chapter of the Office of National Council of Independent Frequency Coordinators", "Amateur operators in a local or regional area whose stations are eligible to be repeater or auxiliary stations", "The FCC Regional Field Office"], 2, "The local amateurs who run or could run repeaters pick their own coordinator."], ["T1A10", "Besides an FCC-issued amateur operator license, what is required to be the control operator of a Radio Amateur Civil Emergency Service (RACES) Station?", ["A written recommendation by the local ARRL Emergency Coordinator", "Membership in the Amateur Radio Emergency Service (ARES)", "Certification of current enrollment by a civil defense organization", "Nothing"], 2, "To operate a RACES station you need your license plus current enrollment certified by a civil defense organization."], ["T1A11", "Which of the following is prohibited?", ["International communications on VHF bands and higher frequencies", "Willful or malicious interference", "Third-party traffic using digital modes", "All these choices are correct"], 1, "Willful or malicious interference is prohibited, full stop. International VHF contacts and digital third-party traffic are allowed."], ["T1B01", "Which of the following frequency ranges are available for phone operation by Technician licensees?", ["28.050 MHz to 28.150 MHz", "28.100 MHz to 28.300 MHz", "28.300 MHz to 28.500 MHz", "28.500 MHz to 28.600 MHz"], 2, "Technician phone on 10 meters runs 28.300 to 28.500 MHz."], ["T1B02", "Which of the following U.S. amateur radio operators are allowed to contact the International Space Station (ISS) on VHF bands?", ["Only amateurs with a General class or higher license", "Any amateur with a Technician class or higher license", "Only amateurs with a General class or higher license, and NASA approval", "Any amateurs with a Technician class or higher license, and NASA approval"], 1, "Any Technician or higher may talk to the ISS. No NASA approval needed."], ["T1B03", "Which frequency is in the 6-meter amateur band?", ["49.00 MHz", "52.525 MHz", "28.50 MHz", "222.15 MHz"], 1, "Six meters is 50 to 54 MHz, so 52.525 MHz is the one inside it."], ["T1B04", "Which amateur band includes 146.52 MHz?", ["6 meters", "20 meters", "70 centimeters", "2 meters"], 3, "146.52 MHz is the national 2-meter FM simplex calling frequency. Two meters is 144 to 148 MHz."], ["T1B05", "Which of the following bands include frequencies where Technicians are authorized to use digital modes such as FT8?", ["10 meters", "6 meters", "2 meters", "All these choices are correct"], 3, "Technicians can run FT8 and other digital modes on 10, 6 and 2 meters."], ["T1B06", "On which HF bands does a Technician class operator have phone privileges?", ["None", "10-meter band only", "80-meter, 40-meter, 15-meter, and 10-meter bands", "30-meter band only"], 1, "On HF, Technician phone is 10 meters only, from 28.300 to 28.500 MHz."], ["T1B07", "Which of the following VHF/UHF band segments are limited to CW only?", ["50.0 MHz to 50.1 MHz and 144.0 MHz to 144.1 MHz", "219 MHz to 220 MHz and 420.0 MHz to 420.1 MHz", "902.0 MHz to 902.1 MHz", "All these choices are correct"], 0, "The bottom 100 kHz of 6 and 2 meters is CW only: 50.0 to 50.1 and 144.0 to 144.1 MHz."], ["T1B08", "How are US amateurs restricted in segments of bands where the Amateur Radio Service is secondary?", ["U.S. amateurs may find non-amateur stations in those segments, and must avoid interfering with them", "U.S. amateurs must give foreign amateur stations priority in those segments", "International communications are not permitted in those segments", "Digital transmissions are not permitted in those segments"], 0, "Secondary means other services come first. You may hear them, and you must not interfere with them."], ["T1B09", "Why should you not set your transmit frequency to be exactly at the edge of an amateur band or sub-band?", ["To allow for calibration error in the transmitter frequency display", "So that modulation sidebands do not extend beyond the band edge", "To allow for transmitter frequency drift", "All these choices are correct"], 3, "Display error, drift and your own sidebands can all push a signal past the edge. Stay off the line."], ["T1B10", "Where may SSB phone be used in amateur bands above 50 MHz?", ["Only in sub-bands allocated to General class or higher licensees", "Only on repeaters", "In at least some segment of all these bands", "On any band if the power is limited to 25 watts"], 2, "Every amateur band above 50 MHz has at least some segment where SSB phone is allowed."], ["T1B11", "What is the maximum peak envelope power output for Technician class operators in their HF band segments?", ["200 watts", "100 watts", "50 watts", "10 watts"], 0, "Technician HF limit is 200 watts PEP."], ["T1B12", "Except for some specific restrictions, what is the maximum peak envelope power output for Technician class operators using frequencies above 30 MHz?", ["50 watts", "100 watts", "500 watts", "1500 watts"], 3, "Above 30 MHz, Technicians get the full legal limit of 1,500 watts PEP."], ["T1C01", "For which classes of amateur radio licenses does the FCC currently issue new licenses?", ["Novice, Technician, General, Amateur Extra", "Technician, Technician Plus, General, Amateur Extra", "Novice, Technician Plus, General, Advanced", "Technician, General, Amateur Extra"], 3, "The FCC issues only three classes now: Technician, General, Amateur Extra. Novice and Advanced are old classes that can be renewed but not newly issued; Technician Plus licenses are now renewed as Technician."], ["T1C02", "Who may select a desired call sign under the vanity call sign rules?", ["Only a licensed amateur with a General or Amateur Extra Class license", "Only a licensed amateur with an Amateur Extra Class license", "Only a licensed amateur who has been licensed continuously for more than 10 years", "Any licensed amateur"], 3, "Any licensed amateur can request a vanity call sign. License class limits which formats you can pick, not whether you can pick one."], ["T1C03", "What types of international communications are FCC-licensed amateur radio stations permitted to make?", ["Communications incidental to the purposes of the Amateur Radio Service and remarks of a personal character", "Communications incidental to conducting business or remarks of a personal nature", "Only communications incidental to contest exchanges; all other communications are prohibited", "Any communications that would be permitted by an international broadcast station"], 0, "International contacts must relate to amateur radio or be personal remarks. No business."], ["T1C04", "What may happen if the FCC is unable to reach you by email?", ["Fine and suspension of operator license", "Revocation of the station license or suspension of the operator license", "Revocation of access to the license record in the FCC system", "Nothing; there is no such requirement"], 1, "You must keep a working email on file with the FCC. If they can't reach you, they can revoke the station license or suspend the operator license."], ["T1C05", "Which of the following is a valid Group D call sign format for Technician class?", ["KF1XXX", "KA1X", "W1XX", "All these choices are correct"], 0, "Group D, the format Technicians get, is two letters, one digit, three letters, like KF1XXX."], ["T1C06", "Which of the following statements is true about using your amateur radio license when operating aboard a vessel or craft in international waters?", ["Amateur operation is prohibited in international waters", "You may only operate during maritime emergencies", "You need special FCC authorization for maritime mobile operation", "You may operate from a US-documented vessel with the master's permission"], 3, "You can operate from a US-documented vessel in international waters with the master's permission."], ["T1C07", "How long before the expiration date may an amateur radio license renewal be requested?", ["30 days", "90 days", "6 months", "1 year"], 1, "You can renew up to 90 days before expiration."], ["T1C08", "What is the normal term for an FCC-issued amateur radio license?", ["Five years", "Eight years", "Ten years", "Life"], 2, "Licenses run ten years."], ["T1C09", "What is the grace period for renewal if an amateur license expires?", ["Two years", "Three years", "Five years", "Ten years"], 0, "After expiration you get a two-year grace period to renew."], ["T1C10", "How soon after passing the examination for your first amateur radio license may you transmit on the amateur radio bands?", ["Immediately on receiving your Certificate of Successful Completion of Examination (CSCE)", "As soon as your operator/station license grant appears on the ARRL website", "As soon as your operator/station license grant appears in the FCC’s license database", "As soon as you receive your license in the mail from the FCC"], 2, "You may transmit the moment your grant appears in the FCC database, not when you leave the exam room."], ["T1C11", "If your license has expired and is still within the allowable grace period, may you continue to transmit on the amateur radio bands?", ["Yes, for up to two years", "Yes, as soon as you apply for renewal", "Yes, for up to one year", "No, you must wait until the license has been renewed"], 3, "During the grace period you can renew but cannot transmit until the renewal goes through."], ["T1D01", "With which countries are FCC-licensed amateur radio stations prohibited from exchanging communications?", ["Any country whose administration has notified the International Telecommunication Union (ITU) that it objects to such communications", "Any country whose administration has notified the American Radio Relay League (ARRL) that it objects to such communications", "Any country banned from such communications by the International Amateur Radio Union (IARU)", "Any country banned from making such communications by the American Radio Relay League (ARRL)"], 0, "A country blocks contact by notifying the ITU, the international telecom body. The ARRL and IARU are amateur organizations with no such power."], ["T1D02", "Under which of the following circumstances are one-way transmissions by an amateur station prohibited?", ["Announcements of upcoming ham radio operating events", "Broadcasting", "International Morse Code Practice", "Telecommand or transmissions of telemetry"], 1, "Broadcasting, meaning transmissions meant for the general public, is prohibited. Code practice, telemetry and event announcements are fine."], ["T1D03", "When is it permissible to transmit messages encoded to obscure their meaning?", ["Only when using a remote station during a contest", "Only when transmitting certain approved digital codes", "Only when transmitting control commands to space stations or model craft", "Never"], 2, "Coded messages are allowed only for control commands to space stations or model craft."], ["T1D04", "Under what conditions is an amateur station authorized to transmit music using a phone emission?", ["When incidental to an authorized retransmission of manned spacecraft communications", "When the music produces no spurious emissions", "When transmissions are limited to less than three minutes per hour", "When the music is transmitted above 1280 MHz"], 0, "Music is allowed only as part of an authorized retransmission of manned spacecraft communications."], ["T1D05", "When may amateur radio operators use their stations to notify other amateurs of the availability of equipment for sale or trade?", ["Never", "When the equipment is not the personal property of either the station licensee, or the control operator, or their close relatives", "When no profit is made on the sale", "When selling amateur radio equipment and not on a regular basis"], 3, "You can mention ham gear for sale as long as it is amateur equipment and you don't do it on a regular basis."], ["T1D06", "What, if any, are the restrictions concerning transmission of language that may be considered indecent or obscene?", ["The FCC maintains a list of words that are not permitted to be used on amateur frequencies", "Any such language is prohibited", "The International Telecommunications Union (ITU) maintains a list of words that are not permitted to be used on amateur frequencies", "There is no such prohibition"], 1, "Indecent or obscene language is prohibited. There is no official word list."], ["T1D07", "Which of the following is an example of an auxiliary station?", ["A station sending one-way transmissions between a remote repeater receiver and the main repeater transmitter", "A backup radio for emergency use in case the main station radio fails", "A station used in the Military Auxiliary Radio System for linking government and amateur stations in a digital network", "A second station utilized in multi-operator contest stations"], 0, "An auxiliary station handles one-way links, such as from a remote repeater receiver to the main repeater transmitter."], ["T1D08", "In which of the following circumstances may the control operator of an amateur station receive compensation for operating that station?", ["When the communication is related to the sale of amateur equipment by the control operator's employer", "When the communication is a part of classroom instruction at an educational institution", "When the communication is made to obtain emergency information for a local broadcast station", "All these choices are correct"], 1, "You may be paid when the operating is part of classroom instruction at a school."], ["T1D09", "When may amateur stations transmit information in support of broadcasting, program production, or news gathering, assuming no other means is available?", ["When such communications are directly related to the immediate safety of human life or protection of property", "When broadcasting communications to or from the space shuttle", "Where non-commercial programming is gathered and supplied exclusively to the National Public Radio network", "Never"], 0, "Helping broadcasters or news gathering is allowed only for immediate safety of life or protection of property, and only when no other means is available."], ["T1D10", "How does the FCC define broadcasting for the Amateur Radio Service?", ["Two-way transmissions by amateur stations", "Any transmission made by the licensed station", "Transmission of messages directed only to amateur operators", "Transmissions intended for reception by the general public"], 3, "Broadcasting means transmissions intended for the general public."], ["T1D11", "When may an amateur station transmit without identifying on the air?", ["When the transmissions are of a brief nature to make station adjustments", "When the transmissions are unmodulated", "When the transmitted power level is below 0.1 watt", "When transmitting signals to control model craft"], 3, "Of these choices, only signals controlling a model craft skip on-air ID; the transmitter must carry a label with the call sign and the licensee's name and address instead. Brief tests, unmodulated carriers and low power all still need ID."], ["T1D12", "Which of the following is required when making on-the-air test transmissions?", ["Identify the transmitting station", "Conduct tests only between 10 p.m. and 6 a.m. local time", "Notify the FCC of the transmissions", "All these choices are correct"], 0, "Test transmissions still require station identification."], ["T1E01", "When may an amateur station transmit without a control operator?", ["When using automatic control, such as in the case of a repeater", "When the station licensee is away and another licensed amateur is using the station", "When the transmitting station is an auxiliary station", "Never"], 3, "There must always be a control operator. Even a repeater on automatic control has one responsible for it."], ["T1E02", "Who may be the control operator of a station communicating through an amateur satellite or space station?", ["Only an Amateur Extra Class operator", "A General class or higher licensee with a satellite operator certification", "Only an Amateur Extra Class operator who is also an AMSAT member", "Any amateur allowed to transmit on the satellite uplink frequency"], 3, "Anyone allowed to transmit on the satellite's uplink frequency can be its control operator. Technicians included."], ["T1E03", "Who must designate the station control operator?", ["The station licensee", "The FCC", "The frequency coordinator", "Any licensed operator"], 0, "The station licensee designates the control operator."], ["T1E04", "What determines the transmitting frequency privileges of an amateur station?", ["The frequency authorized by the frequency coordinator", "The frequencies printed on the license grant", "The highest class of operator license held by anyone on the premises", "The class of operator license held by the control operator"], 3, "Privileges follow the control operator's license class, not the station owner's."], ["T1E05", "What is an amateur station’s control point?", ["The location of the station’s transmitting antenna", "The location of the station’s transmitting apparatus", "The location at which the control operator function is performed", "The mailing address of the station licensee"], 2, "The control point is where the control operator does the controlling, which may not be where the radio is."], ["T1E06", "When, other than during an emergency, may a Technician class licensee be the control operator of a station operating in an Amateur Extra Class band segment?", ["At no time", "When designated as the control operator by an Amateur Extra Class licensee", "As part of a multi-operator contest team", "When using a club station whose trustee holds an Amateur Extra Class license"], 0, "Outside an emergency, a Technician can never be control operator in an Extra-only segment."], ["T1E07", "When the control operator is not the station licensee, who is responsible for the proper operation of the station?", ["All licensed amateurs who are present at the operation", "Only the station licensee", "Only the control operator", "The control operator and the station licensee"], 3, "When they are different people, the control operator and the station licensee are both responsible."], ["T1E08", "Which of the following is an example of automatic control?", ["Repeater operation", "Controlling a station over the internet", "Using a computer or other device to send CW automatically", "Using a computer or other device to identify automatically"], 0, "A repeater runs on automatic control: no operator at the controls while it transmits."], ["T1E09", "Which amateur stations may be remotely controlled?", ["Only repeater stations", "Only automatically controlled stations", "Only digital stations", "Any station"], 3, "Any amateur station may be remotely controlled."], ["T1E10", "Which of the following is an example of remote control as defined in Part 97?", ["A software defined radio (SDR)", "Operating the station over the internet", "Controlling a model aircraft, boat, or car by amateur radio", "Earth-Moon-Earth (EME) communication"], 1, "Remote control means operating the station from elsewhere, such as over the internet. Model aircraft control is telecommand, a different thing."], ["T1E11", "What is a control operator as defined in Part 97?", ["The person speaking or otherwise communicating messages over an amateur station", "The person who is the licensee of an amateur station", "An amateur operator identified in the FCC database as responsible for transmissions and FCC rules compliance at a station license location", "An amateur operator designated by the licensee of a station to be responsible for transmissions and FCC rules compliance at that station"], 3, "The control operator is designated by the station licensee and is responsible for transmissions and rule compliance at that station."], ["T1F01", "When must the station licensee make the station and the station records available for inspection?", ["At any time ten days after notification by the FCC of such an inspection", "At any time upon request by an FCC representative", "At any time after a written notification by the FCC of such inspection", "Only when presented with a valid warrant by an FCC official or government agent"], 1, "Station and records must be available at any time on request by an FCC representative. No advance notice, no warrant."], ["T1F02", "How often must you identify with your FCC-assigned call sign when using tactical call signs such as “Race Headquarters”?", ["Never, the tactical call is sufficient", "Once every hour", "At least every 10 minutes during and at the end of a communication", "At the end of every transmission"], 2, "Tactical calls are fine, but give your FCC call at least every 10 minutes and at the end."], ["T1F03", "When are you required to transmit your assigned call sign?", ["At the beginning of each contact, and every 10 minutes thereafter", "At least once during each transmission", "At least every 15 minutes during and at the end of a communication", "At least every 10 minutes during and at the end of a communication"], 3, "Identify at least every 10 minutes during a contact and at the end. Not at the start."], ["T1F04", "What language must you use for identification when using a phone emission?", ["Any language recognized by the United Nations", "Any language recognized by the ITU", "English", "English, French, or Spanish"], 2, "Phone identification must be in English."], ["T1F05", "What method of call sign identification is required for a station transmitting phone signals?", ["Send the call sign followed by the indicator RPT", "Send the call sign using a CW or phone emission", "Send the call sign followed by the indicator R", "Send the call sign using only a phone emission"], 1, "On phone you may identify by voice or by CW."], ["T1F06", "Which of the following self-assigned indicators are acceptable when using a phone transmission?", ["KL7CC stroke W3", "KL7CC slant W3", "KL7CC slash W3", "All these choices are correct"], 3, "Stroke, slant and slash all work for an indicator like KL7CC/W3."], ["T1F07", "Which of the following restrictions apply when a non-licensed person speaks to a foreign amateur radio station via a station under the control of an FCC-licensed amateur radio operator?", ["The person must be a U.S. citizen", "The foreign station must be in a country with which the U.S. has a third-party agreement", "The licensed control operator must do the station identification", "All these choices are correct"], 1, "An unlicensed person can talk to a foreign station only if that country has a third-party agreement with the US."], ["T1F08", "What is the definition of third-party communications?", ["A message from a control operator to another amateur station control operator on behalf of another person", "Amateur radio communications where three stations are in communications with one another", "Operation when the transmitting equipment is licensed to a person other than the control operator", "Temporary authorization for an unlicensed person to transmit on the amateur bands for technical experiments"], 0, "Third-party communication is a message passed between control operators on behalf of someone else."], ["T1F09", "What type of amateur station simultaneously retransmits the signal of another amateur station on a different channel or channels?", ["Beacon station", "Remote control station", "Repeater station", "Message forwarding station"], 2, "A repeater retransmits another station's signal at the same time on a different channel."], ["T1F10", "Who is accountable if a repeater inadvertently retransmits communications that violate the FCC rules?", ["The control operator of the originating station", "The control operator of the repeater", "The owner of the repeater", "Both the originating station and the repeater owner"], 0, "The originating station's control operator is responsible, not the repeater's operator or owner."], ["T1F11", "Which of the following is a requirement for the issuance of a club station license grant?", ["The trustee must have an Amateur Extra Class operator license grant", "The club must have at least four members", "The club must be registered with the American Radio Relay League (ARRL)", "All these choices are correct"], 1, "A club license needs at least four members. The trustee can hold any class, and ARRL registration isn't required."]], "P": {"purpose": ["Who runs amateur radio", "<p>The FCC regulates and enforces the Amateur Radio Service under Part 97 of its rules. The ARRL is a membership group, not a regulator.</p><p>The stated purposes include emergency communications, advancing technical and communication skills, and international goodwill. Willful or malicious interference is always prohibited.</p>"], "license": ["Getting your license", "<p>After you pass, the FCC emails you a link to download your license. Nothing arrives by mail.</p><p>The license is real once it appears in the FCC's ULS database, and that is the moment you may transmit. Your exam certificate (CSCE) only proves you passed.</p>"], "terms": ["Terms and definitions", "<p>Phonetics are encouraged on phone, not required. A space station is any amateur station more than 50 km above the Earth. Automatic HF beacons sit on 10 meters from 28.200 to 28.300 MHz.</p>"], "coord": ["Frequency coordinators and RACES", "<p>A Volunteer Frequency Coordinator recommends repeater and auxiliary channels. Local hams whose stations could be repeaters choose the coordinator; the FCC doesn't.</p><p>RACES is the civil defense radio service. To be a RACES control operator you need your license plus certified current enrollment in a civil defense organization.</p>"], "bands": ["Technician bands", "<p>Six meters is 50 to 54 MHz. Two meters is 144 to 148 MHz, and 146.52 MHz is the national FM calling frequency there.</p><p>On HF, Technician phone is 10 meters only, 28.300 to 28.500 MHz. Digital modes like FT8 are allowed on 10, 6 and 2 meters.</p><p>The bottom 100 kHz of 6 and 2 meters, 50.0 to 50.1 and 144.0 to 144.1 MHz, is CW only. Every band above 50 MHz has some SSB phone segment.</p>"], "power": ["Power limits", "<p>Technician HF segments: 200 watts PEP. Above 30 MHz: 1,500 watts PEP, the full legal limit, with a few specific exceptions.</p>"], "sharing": ["Sharing the spectrum", "<p>Where amateurs are secondary, other services have priority. You may hear them, and you must not interfere.</p><p>Don't park exactly on a band edge: display error, drift and your own sidebands can carry you over. Any Technician may contact the ISS; no NASA approval is needed.</p>"], "classes": ["License classes and call signs", "<p>The FCC issues three classes now: Technician, General and Amateur Extra. Older classes can be renewed but not newly issued.</p><p>Any licensee may request a vanity call. Technicians get Group D sequential calls: two letters, a digit, three letters, like KF1XXX.</p>"], "renewal": ["License term and renewal", "<p>A license runs 10 years. You can renew up to 90 days early. After expiration there's a two-year grace period to renew, but you can't transmit until it's renewed.</p><p>Keep a working email on file. If the FCC can't reach you, it can revoke the station license or suspend the operator license.</p>"], "intl": ["International and third party", "<p>International contacts must relate to amateur radio or be personal remarks. A country can bar contact by notifying the ITU. You may operate from a US-documented vessel in international waters with the master's permission.</p><p>Third-party communication is a message between control operators on behalf of someone else. An unlicensed person may talk to a foreign station only if the US has a third-party agreement with that country.</p>"], "prohibited": ["What you can't transmit", "<p>Broadcasting, meaning transmissions intended for the general public, is prohibited. So are indecent or obscene language and codes that hide meaning.</p><p>The exceptions: codes for controlling space stations or model craft; music only within an authorized retransmission of manned spacecraft communications; help for news gathering only for immediate safety of life or property when no other means exists.</p>"], "money": ["Money and sales", "<p>You may mention amateur gear for sale if it isn't on a regular basis. A control operator may be paid when operating as part of classroom instruction.</p>"], "id": ["Identifying your station", "<p>Give your call at least every 10 minutes during a contact and at the end. That holds when using tactical calls too. Test transmissions need ID too. Signals sent only to control a model craft are exempt, if the transmitter carries a label with the call sign and the licensee's name and address.</p><p>On phone, identify in English, by voice or CW. Indicators may be said as stroke, slant or slash.</p>"], "control": ["Control operators", "<p>Every transmitting station needs a control operator. The station licensee designates that person, who is responsible for transmissions and rule compliance. When they're different people, both are responsible.</p><p>Privileges come from the control operator's license class. A Technician can never control a station in an Extra-only segment outside an emergency. Any amateur allowed on a satellite's uplink can control a station using it. The control point is where the control operator actually controls the station.</p>"], "ctltypes": ["Control types and station types", "<p>Automatic control: the station runs itself, like a repeater. Remote control: you operate from elsewhere, like over the internet. Any station may be remotely controlled.</p><p>A repeater retransmits another station simultaneously on a different channel; if it relays a rule violation, the originating station's control operator is responsible. An auxiliary station carries one-way links, like a remote receiver feeding the main repeater.</p>"], "club": ["Inspections and club stations", "<p>Your station and records must be available any time an FCC representative asks. A club license needs at least four members.</p>"]}, "CON": {"T1A01": "purpose", "T1A02": "purpose", "T1A11": "purpose", "T1A04": "license", "T1A05": "license", "T1C10": "license", "T1A03": "terms", "T1A07": "terms", "T1A06": "terms", "T1A08": "coord", "T1A09": "coord", "T1A10": "coord", "T1B01": "bands", "T1B03": "bands", "T1B04": "bands", "T1B05": "bands", "T1B06": "bands", "T1B07": "bands", "T1B10": "bands", "T1B11": "power", "T1B12": "power", "T1B08": "sharing", "T1B09": "sharing", "T1B02": "sharing", "T1C01": "classes", "T1C02": "classes", "T1C05": "classes", "T1C08": "renewal", "T1C07": "renewal", "T1C09": "renewal", "T1C11": "renewal", "T1C04": "renewal", "T1C03": "intl", "T1C06": "intl", "T1D01": "intl", "T1F08": "intl", "T1F07": "intl", "T1D10": "prohibited", "T1D02": "prohibited", "T1D06": "prohibited", "T1D03": "prohibited", "T1D04": "prohibited", "T1D09": "prohibited", "T1D05": "money", "T1D08": "money", "T1F03": "id", "T1F02": "id", "T1D11": "id", "T1D12": "id", "T1F04": "id", "T1F05": "id", "T1F06": "id", "T1E01": "control", "T1E03": "control", "T1E11": "control", "T1E07": "control", "T1E04": "control", "T1E06": "control", "T1E02": "control", "T1E05": "control", "T1E08": "ctltypes", "T1E10": "ctltypes", "T1E09": "ctltypes", "T1F09": "ctltypes", "T1F10": "ctltypes", "T1D07": "ctltypes", "T1F01": "club", "T1F11": "club"}}, {"id": "t2", "code": "T2", "name": "Operating procedures", "exam": 3, "G": {"T2A": "Calling, simplex and repeater basics", "T2B": "Repeater access, squelch and Q signals", "T2C": "Nets, emergencies and message handling"}, "Q": [["T2A01", "What is a common repeater frequency offset in the 2-meter band?", ["Plus or minus 5 MHz", "Plus or minus 600 kHz", "Plus or minus 500 kHz", "Plus or minus 1 MHz"], 1, "Two-meter repeaters typically use a 600 kHz offset, up or down."], ["T2A02", "What is the national calling frequency for FM simplex operations in the 2-meter band?", ["146.520 MHz", "145.000 MHz", "432.100 MHz", "446.000 MHz"], 0, "146.520 MHz is the national 2-meter FM simplex calling frequency."], ["T2A03", "What is a common repeater frequency offset in the 70-centimeter band?", ["Plus or minus 5 MHz", "Plus or minus 600 kHz", "Plus or minus 500 kHz", "Plus or minus 1 MHz"], 0, "Seventy-centimeter repeaters typically use a 5 MHz offset."], ["T2A04", "What is an appropriate way to call another station on a repeater if you know the other station's call sign?", ["Say \"break, break,\" then the other station’s call sign, followed by your call sign", "Say the station's call sign, then identify with your call sign", "Say \"CQ\" three times, then the other station's call sign, followed by your call sign", "Wait for the station to call CQ, then answer with your call sign"], 1, "On a repeater, say the other station's call, then yours. No CQ, no \"break, break.\""], ["T2A05", "How should you respond to a station calling CQ?", ["Transmit \"CQ\" followed by the other station’s call sign", "Transmit your call sign followed by the other station’s call sign", "Transmit the other station’s call sign followed by your call sign", "Transmit a signal report followed by your call sign"], 2, "Answer a CQ with their call sign first, then yours."], ["T2A06", "What is an effective way to seek a call from any phone station when not using a repeater?", ["Sign your call sign once, followed by the words “listening for a call;” if there is no answer, change the frequency and repeat", "Say “QTC,” followed by “this is” and your call sign; if there is no answer, change the frequency and repeat", "Transmit an unmodulated carrier for approximately 10 seconds, followed by “this is” and your call sign, and pause to listen; repeat as necessary", "Repeat “CQ” a few times, followed by “this is,” and your call sign, then pause to listen; repeat as necessary"], 3, "Off a repeater, call CQ a few times, say \"this is\" and your call, then listen."], ["T2A07", "What does the term \"repeater offset\" mean?", ["The difference between a repeater’s transmit and receive frequencies", "The repeater has a time delay to prevent interference", "The approximately half-second delay to allow for the “squelch tail” to be removed", "The fee charged by the repeater owner or club to provide for maintenance"], 0, "Offset is the difference between a repeater's transmit and receive frequencies."], ["T2A08", "What is the meaning of the procedural signal “CQ”?", ["A shortened form of the term “Contest QSO”", "“Communication Quality,” used to indicate transmitted audio clarity", "Only the called station should transmit", "Calling any station"], 3, "CQ means calling any station."], ["T2A09", "Which of the following is a customary way to indicate a station is listening on a repeater and looking for a contact?", ["“CQ CQ” followed by the repeater’s call sign", "The station’s call sign followed by the word “listening”", "The repeater’s call sign followed by the station’s call sign", "“QSY” followed by your call sign"], 1, "On a repeater, give your call followed by \"listening.\""], ["T2A10", "What is a band plan, beyond the privileges established by the FCC?", ["A voluntary guideline for using different modes or activities within an amateur band", "A list of operating schedules", "A list of available net frequencies", "A plan devised by a club to indicate frequency band usage"], 0, "A band plan is a voluntary guideline for which modes and activities go where within a band, on top of what the FCC requires."], ["T2A11", "What term describes an amateur station that is transmitting and receiving on the same frequency?", ["Full duplex", "Diplex", "Simplex", "Multiplex"], 2, "Simplex means transmitting and receiving on the same frequency."], ["T2B01", "What is the purpose of the reverse function on a VHF/UHF transceiver?", ["To reduce power output", "To increase power output", "To listen on a repeater’s input frequency", "To listen on a repeater’s output frequency"], 2, "Reverse swaps your radio's transmit and receive frequencies, so you listen on the repeater's input. It tells you whether you could reach the other station directly."], ["T2B02", "What term describes the use of a sub-audible tone transmitted along with normal voice audio to open the squelch of a receiver?", ["Carrier squelch", "Tone burst", "DTMF", "CTCSS"], 3, "CTCSS is a sub-audible tone sent with your voice to open a receiver's squelch, usually a repeater's."], ["T2B03", "Which of the following describes a linked repeater network?", ["A network of repeaters in which signals received by one repeater are transmitted by all the repeaters in the network", "A single repeater with more than one receiver", "Multiple repeaters with the same control operator", "A system of repeaters linked by APRS"], 0, "In a linked network, whatever one repeater receives is transmitted by all of them."], ["T2B04", "Which of the following could be the reason you are unable to access a repeater whose output you can hear?", ["Improper transceiver offset", "You are using the wrong CTCSS tone", "You are using the wrong DCS code", "All these choices are correct"], 3, "If you hear the repeater but can't get in, the offset, the CTCSS tone or the DCS code could each be wrong."], ["T2B05", "Which of the following would cause your FM transmission audio to drop out on voice peaks?", ["Your repeater offset is inverted", "Your FM deviation is too low", "You are talking too loudly", "Your transmit power is too high"], 2, "Talking too loudly overdeviates your FM signal, and the audio drops out on peaks."], ["T2B06", "What type of signaling to a repeater uses two simultaneous audio tones?", ["DTMF", "CTCSS", "GMRS", "D-STAR"], 0, "DTMF uses two tones at once, the same tones as a phone keypad."], ["T2B07", "How can you join a digital repeater’s “talkgroup”?", ["Register with the local Frequency Coordinator", "Register with the digital repeater control operator", "Program your radio with the group’s ID or code", "Program your radio with the DTMF tone used by the talkgroup"], 2, "To join a DMR talkgroup, program its ID into your radio. No sign-up with the repeater owner or coordinator needed."], ["T2B08", "Which of the following applies when two stations transmitting on the same frequency interfere with each other?", ["The stations should negotiate continued use of the frequency", "Both stations should choose another frequency to avoid conflict", "Whichever station was on the frequency first has preemptive rights to the frequency", "Use subaudible tones so both stations can share the frequency"], 0, "No one owns a frequency. The two stations should work it out between themselves."], ["T2B09", "Why are simplex channels designated in the VHF/UHF band plans?", ["So stations within range of each other can communicate without tying up a repeater", "For contest operation", "For working DX only", "So stations with simple transmitters can access the repeater without automated offset"], 0, "Simplex channels let nearby stations talk directly and keep the repeater free."], ["T2B10", "Which Q signal indicates that you are receiving interference from other stations?", ["QRM", "QRN", "QTH", "QSB"], 0, "QRM is interference from other stations. QRN is natural noise like static."], ["T2B11", "Which Q signal indicates that you are changing frequency?", ["QRU", "QSY", "QSL", "QRZ"], 1, "QSY means changing frequency."], ["T2B12", "What is the digital color code used on DMR repeater systems?", ["An access code which must be programmed into a DMR transmitter to access a specific repeater", "A code which automatically programs the repeater offset to a frequency chosen by the user", "A code which identifies the specific CODEC being used so that the repeater can properly decode the audio", "A code transmitted by the repeater to indicate health and status of the equipment"], 0, "A DMR color code is an access code your radio must send to use a specific repeater, much like a CTCSS tone on FM."], ["T2B13", "What is the purpose of a squelch function?", ["Reduce a CW transmitter's key clicks", "Mute the receiver audio when a signal is not present", "Eliminate parasitic oscillations in an RF amplifier", "Reduce interference from impulse noise"], 1, "Squelch mutes the receiver when no signal is present, cutting the hiss."], ["T2B14", "Which of the following is a “talkgroup”?", ["A group of amateur radio operators who share email on a specific amateur radio topic", "An identifier used by DMR to organize radio traffic so that those who want to hear the group aren’t bothered by other radio traffic", "The members of a net organized for amateur radio operators with a specific common interest", "A method of automatically alerting all group members to the frequency where any member is transmitting"], 1, "A DMR talkgroup is an identifier that sorts traffic so you hear only the group you've chosen."], ["T2C01", "When do the FCC Part 97 Amateur Radio Service rules NOT apply to the operation of an amateur station?", ["When operating under RACES rules", "When operating under FEMA rules", "When operating under ARES rules", "FCC rules always apply"], 3, "FCC Part 97 rules always apply, under RACES, ARES or anything else."], ["T2C02", "Which of the following are typical duties of a Net Control Station?", ["Choose the regular net meeting time and frequency", "Ensure that all stations checking into the net are properly licensed for operation on the net frequency", "Call the net to order and direct communications between stations checking in", "All these choices are correct"], 2, "Net control calls the net to order and directs who talks when."], ["T2C03", "What technique is used to ensure that voice messages containing unusual words are received correctly?", ["Send the words by voice and Morse code", "Use the Q-code “QSR” to ask the receiving station to repeat the words back", "Spell the words using a standard phonetic alphabet", "All these choices are correct"], 2, "Spell unusual words with a standard phonetic alphabet."], ["T2C04", "What is RACES?", ["An emergency organization combining amateur radio and citizens band operators and frequencies", "An international radio experimentation society", "A radio contest held in a short period, sometimes called a “sprint”", "An FCC Part 97 amateur radio service for civil defense communications during national emergencies"], 3, "RACES is the civil defense amateur service under Part 97, used during local, regional or national emergencies."], ["T2C05", "What does the term “traffic” refer to in net operation?", ["Formal messages exchanged by net stations", "The number of stations checking in and out of a net", "Operation by mobile or portable stations", "A count of the number of activations of the net each month"], 0, "Traffic means formal messages passed by net stations."], ["T2C06", "What is the Amateur Radio Emergency Service (ARES)?", ["A group of licensed amateurs who have voluntarily registered their qualifications and equipment for communications duty in the public service", "A group of licensed amateurs who are members of the military and who voluntarily agreed to provide message handling services in the case of an emergency", "A training program that provides licensing courses for those interested in obtaining an amateur license to use during emergencies", "A training program that certifies amateur operators for membership in the Radio Amateur Civil Emergency Service"], 0, "ARES is a volunteer group of hams who register their skills and equipment for public service communications."], ["T2C07", "Which of the following is standard practice when you participate in a net?", ["When first responding to the net control station, transmit your call sign, name, and address as in the FCC database", "Record the time of each of your transmissions", "Unless you are reporting an emergency, transmit only when directed by the net control station", "All these choices are correct"], 2, "In a net, transmit only when net control directs you, unless you're reporting an emergency."], ["T2C08", "Which of the following relays messages using email addresses based on amateur callsigns?", ["Winlink", "FT8", "PSK31", "AMTOR"], 0, "Winlink relays email over radio, using addresses built from call signs."], ["T2C09", "Are amateur station control operators ever permitted to operate outside the frequency privileges of their license class?", ["No", "Yes, but only when part of a FEMA emergency plan", "Yes, but only when part of a RACES emergency plan", "Yes, but only in situations involving the immediate safety of human life or protection of property"], 3, "You may go outside your privileges only for immediate safety of human life or protection of property."], ["T2C10", "What information is contained in the preamble of a formal traffic message?", ["The email address of the originating station", "The address of the intended recipient", "The telephone number of the addressee", "Information needed to track the message"], 3, "The preamble carries the information used to track the message."], ["T2C11", "What is meant by “check” in a radiogram header?", ["The number of words or word equivalents in the text portion of the message", "The call sign of the originating station", "A list of stations that have relayed the message", "A box on the message form that indicates that the message was received and/or relayed"], 0, "\"Check\" is the count of words in the message text."], ["T2C12", "Which of the following requires certification by a civil defense agency?", ["ARES", "RACES", "MARS", "SKYWARN"], 1, "RACES requires civil defense certification. ARES, MARS and SKYWARN don't."]], "P": {"calling": ["Making contacts", "<p>CQ means calling any station. Off a repeater, call CQ a few times, then \"this is\" and your call, then listen. To answer a CQ, give their call first, then yours.</p><p>On a repeater, skip CQ. To reach someone, say their call then yours. To announce yourself, say your call and \"listening.\"</p>"], "simplex": ["Simplex and band plans", "<p>Simplex means transmitting and receiving on one frequency. The 2-meter FM simplex calling frequency is 146.520 MHz. Simplex channels let nearby stations talk without tying up a repeater.</p><p>A band plan is a voluntary guideline for which modes go where, beyond what the FCC requires. Nobody owns a frequency; if two stations collide, they work it out.</p>"], "offset": ["Repeater offsets", "<p>A repeater listens on one frequency and transmits on another. The difference is the offset: 600 kHz on 2 meters, 5 MHz on 70 centimeters.</p><p>Reverse swaps your radio's frequencies so you hear the repeater's input. Linked repeaters retransmit whatever any one of them receives.</p>"], "access": ["Getting into a repeater", "<p>Many repeaters only open for a signal carrying the right tone. CTCSS is a sub-audible tone sent with your voice; DCS is a digital code that does the same job. DTMF is two tones at once, like a phone keypad, used for control commands.</p><p>If you hear a repeater but can't access it, check the offset, the tone and the code. Squelch mutes the receiver when no signal is present.</p>"], "dmr": ["DMR", "<p>A DMR color code is an access code your radio must send to use a given repeater. A talkgroup is an identifier that sorts traffic so you hear only your group. To join one, program its ID into your radio.</p>"], "audio": ["Audio and Q signals", "<p>Talking too loudly overdeviates FM and drops the audio on peaks.</p><p>QRM is interference from other stations. QSY means changing frequency.</p>"], "nets": ["Nets and traffic", "<p>Net control calls the net to order and directs who talks. Transmit only when directed, unless you're reporting an emergency.</p><p>Traffic means formal messages. A radiogram's preamble holds the tracking information, and its \"check\" is the word count of the text. Spell unusual words with a phonetic alphabet. Winlink carries email over radio using call-sign addresses.</p>"], "emerg": ["Emergency service", "<p>ARES is a volunteer group of hams registered for public service work. RACES is the Part 97 civil defense service, and it requires certification by a civil defense agency.</p><p>FCC rules always apply. You may operate outside your privileges only for immediate safety of human life or protection of property.</p>"]}, "CON": {"T2A08": "calling", "T2A06": "calling", "T2A05": "calling", "T2A04": "calling", "T2A09": "calling", "T2A11": "simplex", "T2A02": "simplex", "T2B09": "simplex", "T2A10": "simplex", "T2B08": "simplex", "T2A07": "offset", "T2A01": "offset", "T2A03": "offset", "T2B01": "offset", "T2B03": "offset", "T2B02": "access", "T2B06": "access", "T2B04": "access", "T2B13": "access", "T2B12": "dmr", "T2B14": "dmr", "T2B07": "dmr", "T2B05": "audio", "T2B10": "audio", "T2B11": "audio", "T2C02": "nets", "T2C07": "nets", "T2C05": "nets", "T2C10": "nets", "T2C11": "nets", "T2C03": "nets", "T2C08": "nets", "T2C06": "emerg", "T2C04": "emerg", "T2C12": "emerg", "T2C01": "emerg", "T2C09": "emerg"}}, {"id": "t3", "code": "T3", "name": "Radio wave propagation", "exam": 3, "G": {"T3A": "Fading, multipath, polarization and weather", "T3B": "Radio waves, wavelength and frequency ranges", "T3C": "Propagation modes beyond the horizon"}, "Q": [["T3A01", "Why do VHF signal strengths sometimes vary greatly when the antenna is moved only a few feet?", ["The signal path encounters different concentrations of water vapor", "VHF ionospheric propagation is very sensitive to path length", "Multipath propagation cancels or reinforces signals", "The Doppler effect causes slight frequency shifts which result in changes in signal strength"], 2, "Signals arriving by several paths add or cancel. Move the antenna a few feet and the mix changes."], ["T3A02", "How does vegetation affect UHF and microwave signals?", ["Causes knife-edge diffraction, distorting voice peaks", "Absorbs signals, leading to poor reception of weak signals", "Amplifies signals, improving reception of weak signals", "Has no effect"], 1, "Leaves and branches absorb UHF and microwave signals, which hurts weak ones most."], ["T3A03", "What antenna polarization is normally used for long-distance CW and SSB contacts on the VHF and UHF bands?", ["Right-hand circular", "Left-hand circular", "Horizontal", "Vertical"], 2, "Long-distance CW and SSB on VHF and UHF use horizontal polarization. FM and repeaters use vertical."], ["T3A04", "What is the effect of antenna cross-polarization over a line-of-sight VHF or UHF path?", ["Modulation sidebands might become inverted", "Received signal strength is reduced", "Signals have an echo effect", "Nothing significant will happen"], 1, "Cross-polarized antennas, one vertical and one horizontal, give a much weaker received signal."], ["T3A05", "When using a directional antenna, how might your station be able to communicate with a distant repeater if buildings or obstructions are blocking the direct line of sight path?", ["Change from vertical to horizontal polarization", "Try to find a path that reflects signals to the repeater", "Try the long path", "Increase the antenna SWR"], 1, "If buildings block the direct path, aim a directional antenna for a reflection that reaches the repeater."], ["T3A06", "What is the meaning of the term “picket fencing”?", ["Alternating transmissions during a net operation", "Rapid flutter on mobile signals due to multipath propagation", "A type of ground system used with vertical antennas", "Interference from cable TV in the form of carriers at fixed intervals across the band"], 1, "Picket fencing is the rapid flutter on a mobile signal from multipath as the car moves."], ["T3A07", "What weather condition might decrease range at microwave frequencies?", ["High winds", "Low barometric pressure", "Precipitation", "Colder temperatures"], 2, "Rain, snow and other precipitation absorb microwaves and shorten range."], ["T3A08", "What is a likely cause of irregular fading of signals propagated by the ionosphere?", ["Frequency shift due to Faraday rotation", "Interference from thunderstorms", "Intermodulation distortion", "Random combining of signals arriving via different paths"], 3, "Ionospheric signals arrive by several paths that combine at random, so they fade irregularly."], ["T3A09", "Which of the following results from the fact that signals propagated by the ionosphere are elliptically polarized?", ["Digital modes are unusable", "Either vertically or horizontally polarized antennas may be used for transmission or reception", "FM voice is unusable", "Both the transmitting and receiving antennas must be of the same polarization"], 1, "Ionospheric signals come back elliptically polarized, so a vertical or horizontal antenna works on either end."], ["T3A10", "What effect does multi-path propagation have on data transmissions?", ["Transmission rates must be increased by a factor equal to the number of separate paths observed", "Transmission rates must be decreased by a factor equal to the number of separate paths observed", "No significant changes will occur if the signals are transmitted using FM", "Error rates are likely to increase"], 3, "Multipath scrambles timing, so data error rates go up."], ["T3A11", "Which region of the atmosphere can reflect HF radio waves?", ["The stratosphere", "The troposphere", "The ionosphere", "The electrosphere"], 2, "The ionosphere, a layer of electrically charged gas high above the weather, bends HF back to Earth. The troposphere and stratosphere are lower layers that don't."], ["T3A12", "What effect does fog or rain have on 10-meter and 6-meter band signals?", ["Absorption", "Little effect", "Deflection", "Increased range"], 1, "Fog and rain have little effect on 10 and 6 meters. Absorption matters at microwave frequencies."], ["T3B01", "What is the relationship between the electric and magnetic fields of an electromagnetic wave?", ["They travel at different speeds", "They are in parallel", "They revolve in opposite directions", "They are at right angles"], 3, "The electric and magnetic fields are at right angles to each other."], ["T3B02", "What property of a radio wave defines its polarization?", ["The orientation of the electric field", "The orientation of the magnetic field", "The ratio of the energy in the magnetic field to the energy in the electric field", "The ratio of the velocity to the wavelength"], 0, "Polarization is the orientation of the electric field."], ["T3B03", "What are the two components of a radio wave?", ["Impedance and reactance", "Voltage and current", "Electric and magnetic fields", "Ionizing and non-ionizing radiation"], 2, "A radio wave is an electric field and a magnetic field together."], ["T3B04", "What is the velocity of a radio wave traveling through free space?", ["Speed of light", "Speed of sound", "0.86 times the speed of light", "1.86 times the speed of sound"], 0, "Radio waves in free space travel at the speed of light."], ["T3B05", "What is the relationship between wavelength and frequency?", ["Wavelength gets longer as frequency increases", "Wavelength gets shorter as frequency increases", "Wavelength is constant at all frequencies", "Wavelength and frequency increase as path length increases"], 1, "As frequency goes up, wavelength gets shorter."], ["T3B06", "What is the formula for converting frequency to approximate wavelength in meters?", ["Wavelength in meters equals frequency in hertz multiplied by 300", "Wavelength in meters equals frequency in hertz divided by 300", "Wavelength in meters equals frequency in megahertz divided by 300", "Wavelength in meters equals 300 divided by frequency in megahertz"], 3, "Wavelength in meters equals 300 divided by frequency in MHz."], ["T3B07", "In addition to frequency, which of the following is used to identify amateur radio bands?", ["The approximate wavelength in meters", "Traditional letter/number designators", "Channel numbers", "All these choices are correct"], 0, "Bands are also named by approximate wavelength, like 2 meters or 70 centimeters."], ["T3B08", "What frequency range is referred to as VHF?", ["30 kHz to 300 kHz", "30 MHz to 300 MHz", "300 kHz to 3000 kHz", "300 MHz to 3000 MHz"], 1, "VHF is 30 to 300 MHz."], ["T3B09", "What frequency range is referred to as UHF?", ["30 to 300 kHz", "30 to 300 MHz", "300 to 3000 kHz", "300 to 3000 MHz"], 3, "UHF is 300 to 3,000 MHz."], ["T3B10", "What frequency range is referred to as HF?", ["300 to 3000 MHz", "30 to 300 MHz", "3 to 30 MHz", "300 to 3000 kHz"], 2, "HF is 3 to 30 MHz."], ["T3B11", "What is the approximate velocity of a radio wave in free space?", ["150,000,000 meters per second", "300,000,000 meters per second", "300,000,000 miles per hour", "150,000,000 miles per hour"], 1, "The speed of light is about 300,000,000 meters per second."], ["T3B12", "Which of these frequencies travels at the highest velocity in free space?", ["Microwaves", "UHF", "VHF", "All radio frequencies travel at the same velocity"], 3, "Every radio frequency travels at the same speed in free space."], ["T3C01", "Why are simplex UHF signals rarely heard beyond their radio horizon?", ["They are too weak to go very far", "FCC regulations prohibit them from going more than 50 miles", "UHF signals are usually not propagated by the ionosphere", "UHF signals are absorbed by the ionospheric D region"], 2, "The ionosphere usually doesn't return UHF, so simplex UHF rarely goes past the radio horizon."], ["T3C02", "What is a characteristic of HF communication compared with communications on VHF and higher frequencies?", ["HF antennas are generally smaller", "HF accommodates wider bandwidth signals", "Long-distance ionospheric propagation is far more common on HF", "There is less atmospheric interference (static) on HF"], 2, "Long-distance ionospheric propagation is far more common on HF than on VHF and up."], ["T3C03", "What is one characteristic of VHF signals received via auroral backscatter?", ["They are often received from 10,000 miles or more", "They are distorted, with a characteristic raspy sound", "They occur only during winter nighttime hours", "They are generally strongest when your antenna is aimed west"], 1, "Auroral backscatter signals sound distorted and raspy."], ["T3C04", "Which of the following types of propagation is most commonly associated with occasional strong signals on the 10-, 6-, and 2-meter bands from beyond the radio horizon?", ["Backscatter", "Sporadic E", "D region absorption", "Gray-line propagation"], 1, "Sporadic E brings occasional strong signals from beyond the horizon on 10, 6 and 2 meters."], ["T3C05", "Which of the following effects may allow radio signals to travel beyond obstructions between the transmitting and receiving stations?", ["Knife-edge diffraction", "Faraday rotation", "Quantum tunneling", "Doppler shift"], 0, "Knife-edge diffraction bends signals around the sharp edge of an obstruction like a ridge."], ["T3C06", "What type of propagation is responsible for allowing over-the-horizon VHF and UHF communications to ranges of approximately 300 miles on a regular basis?", ["Tropospheric ducting", "D region refraction", "F2 region refraction", "Faraday rotation"], 0, "Tropospheric ducting regularly carries VHF and UHF about 300 miles."], ["T3C07", "What band is best suited for communicating via meteor scatter?", ["33 centimeters", "6 meters", "2 meters", "70 centimeters"], 1, "Six meters is the best band for meteor scatter."], ["T3C08", "What causes tropospheric ducting?", ["Discharges of lightning during electrical storms", "Sunspots and solar flares", "Updrafts from hurricanes and tornadoes", "Temperature inversions in the atmosphere"], 3, "Temperature inversions in the atmosphere cause tropospheric ducting."], ["T3C09", "What is generally the best time for long-distance 10-meter band propagation via the F region?", ["From dawn to shortly after sunset during periods of high sunspot activity", "From shortly after sunset to dawn during periods of high sunspot activity", "From dawn to shortly after sunset during periods of low sunspot activity", "From shortly after sunset to dawn during periods of low sunspot activity"], 0, "Ten-meter F region openings are best from dawn to shortly after sunset in high sunspot years."], ["T3C10", "Which of the following bands may provide long-distance communications via the ionosphere’s F region during the peak of the sunspot cycle?", ["6 and 10 meters", "23 centimeters", "70 centimeters and 1.25 meters", "All these choices are correct"], 0, "At the sunspot peak, the F region can carry 6 and 10 meters long distances."], ["T3C11", "Why is the radio horizon for VHF and UHF signals more distant than the visual horizon?", ["Radio signals move somewhat faster than the speed of light", "Radio waves are not blocked by dust particles", "The atmosphere refracts radio waves slightly", "Radio waves are blocked by dust particles"], 2, "The atmosphere bends radio waves slightly, so the radio horizon is farther than the visual one."]], "P": {"wave": ["What a radio wave is", "<p>A radio wave is an electric field and a magnetic field at right angles. Polarization is the orientation of the electric field.</p><p>In free space every radio frequency travels at the speed of light, about 300,000,000 meters per second.</p>"], "wavelength": ["Wavelength and frequency", "<p>Wavelength in meters equals 300 divided by frequency in MHz. Higher frequency means shorter wavelength. Bands are named by wavelength: 2 meters is about 146 MHz, 70 centimeters about 440 MHz.</p>"], "ranges": ["Frequency ranges", "<p>HF is 3 to 30 MHz. VHF is 30 to 300 MHz. UHF is 300 to 3,000 MHz. Each range starts with a 3 and runs up by a factor of ten.</p>"], "multipath": ["Multipath and fading", "<p>Signals arriving by several paths add or cancel. That's why moving a VHF antenna a few feet changes the signal, why mobile signals flutter (picket fencing), why HF signals fade irregularly, and why data error rates rise.</p><p>When obstructions block the direct path, aim a directional antenna for a reflection instead.</p>"], "polar": ["Polarization", "<p>Long-distance CW and SSB on VHF and UHF use horizontal antennas. Mismatched polarization over a line-of-sight path weakens the signal a lot.</p><p>Ionospheric signals return elliptically polarized, so on HF either polarization works.</p>"], "absorb": ["Weather and vegetation", "<p>Trees absorb UHF and microwave signals. Rain and snow shorten microwave range. On 10 and 6 meters, fog and rain have little effect.</p>"], "iono": ["The ionosphere and HF", "<p>The ionosphere reflects HF, which is why long-distance contacts are far more common there. It usually doesn't return UHF, so UHF simplex rarely goes past the radio horizon.</p><p>At the sunspot peak, the F region opens 10 and 6 meters, with 10 meters best from dawn to shortly after sunset.</p>"], "horizon": ["Beyond the horizon on VHF and UHF", "<p>The atmosphere bends radio waves slightly, so the radio horizon lies past the visual one. Knife-edge diffraction bends signals over sharp obstructions.</p><p>Temperature inversions cause tropospheric ducting, good for about 300 miles. Sporadic E brings occasional strong signals on 10, 6 and 2 meters. Six meters is best for meteor scatter. Auroral signals sound raspy.</p>"]}, "CON": {"T3B03": "wave", "T3B01": "wave", "T3B02": "wave", "T3B04": "wave", "T3B11": "wave", "T3B12": "wave", "T3B05": "wavelength", "T3B06": "wavelength", "T3B07": "wavelength", "T3B10": "ranges", "T3B08": "ranges", "T3B09": "ranges", "T3A01": "multipath", "T3A06": "multipath", "T3A08": "multipath", "T3A10": "multipath", "T3A05": "multipath", "T3A03": "polar", "T3A04": "polar", "T3A09": "polar", "T3A02": "absorb", "T3A07": "absorb", "T3A12": "absorb", "T3A11": "iono", "T3C02": "iono", "T3C01": "iono", "T3C10": "iono", "T3C09": "iono", "T3C11": "horizon", "T3C05": "horizon", "T3C08": "horizon", "T3C06": "horizon", "T3C04": "horizon", "T3C07": "horizon", "T3C03": "horizon"}}, {"id": "t4", "code": "T4", "name": "Station setup and operation", "exam": 2, "G": {"T4A": "Station setup, power and digital interfaces", "T4B": "Operating controls, filters and digital voice"}, "Q": [["T4A01", "Which of the following is an appropriate power supply rating for a typical 50-watt output mobile FM transceiver?", ["24.0 volts at 4 amperes", "13.8 volts at 4 amperes", "24.0 volts at 12 amperes", "13.8 volts at 12 amperes"], 3, "Mobile radios run on 13.8 volts, and 50 watts out draws about 12 amps."], ["T4A02", "Which of the following should be considered when selecting an accessory SWR meter?", ["The frequency and power level at which the measurements will be made", "The distance that the meter will be located from the antenna", "The maximum SWR anticipated on the transmission line", "The ability of the meter to compensate for a poor transmission line match to the antenna"], 0, "Pick an SWR meter rated for the frequency and power you'll measure at."], ["T4A03", "Why are short, heavy-gauge wires used for a transceiver’s DC power connection?", ["To minimize voltage drop when transmitting", "To provide a close match to the power supply output impedance", "To avoid RF interference", "To minimize radiative losses in the power cable"], 0, "Short, heavy power leads keep voltage from sagging when the radio draws high current on transmit."], ["T4A04", "How are the audio input and output of a transceiver connected in a station configured to operate using FT8?", ["To a computer running a terminal program and connected to a terminal node controller unit", "To the audio output and input of a computer running FT8 software", "To an FT8 conversion unit, a keyboard, and a computer monitor", "To a computer connected to the FT8converter.com website"], 1, "For FT8, connect the radio's audio out and in to the computer's audio in and out, with FT8 software running."], ["T4A05", "Where should an RF power meter be installed?", ["In the feed line, between the transmitter and antenna", "At the power supply output", "In parallel with the push-to-talk line and the antenna", "In the power supply cable, as close as possible to the radio"], 0, "An RF power meter goes in the feed line, between the transmitter and the antenna."], ["T4A06", "What signals are used in a computer-radio interface for digital mode operation?", ["Receive and transmit mode, status, and location", "Antenna and RF power", "Receive audio, transmit audio, and transmitter keying", "NMEA GPS location and DC power"], 2, "A computer-radio interface carries receive audio, transmit audio and transmitter keying."], ["T4A07", "Which of the following is one of the connections required between a computer and a transceiver to operate digital modes?", ["Computer “line out” to transceiver push-to-talk", "Computer “line in” to transceiver push-to-talk", "Computer “line in” to transceiver speaker connector", "Computer “line out” to transceiver speaker connector"], 2, "The radio's speaker output feeds the computer's line in. Audio goes out to in, in both directions."], ["T4A08", "Which of the following conductors is preferred for bonding at RF?", ["Copper braid removed from coaxial cable", "Copper-clad steel wire", "Twisted-pair cable", "Flat copper strap"], 3, "Flat copper strap is best for RF bonding. Its wide surface keeps impedance low at radio frequencies."], ["T4A09", "How can you determine the length of time that equipment can be powered from a battery?", ["Divide the watt-hour rating of the battery by the peak power consumption of the equipment", "Divide the battery ampere-hour rating by the average current draw of the equipment", "Multiply the watts per hour consumed by the equipment by the battery power rating", "Multiply the square of the current rating of the battery by the input resistance of the equipment"], 1, "Battery runtime is the amp-hour rating divided by the average current draw. A 10 Ah battery at 2 A lasts about five hours."], ["T4A10", "What function does a digital mode hotspot perform for nearby transceivers?", ["Communication with a digital voice or data network", "FT8 digital communications via AFSK using a smartphone connected to the internet", "RTTY encoding and decoding without a computer", "High-speed digital communications for meteor scatter"], 0, "A hotspot is a small device that links nearby digital radios to a digital voice or data network over the internet."], ["T4A11", "Where should the negative power return of a mobile transceiver be connected in a vehicle?", ["At the 12-volt battery chassis ground", "To the shell of the power connector", "To any metal part of the vehicle", "Through the transceiver’s mounting bracket"], 0, "Connect the radio's negative lead at the battery's chassis ground connection."], ["T4A12", "What is an electronic keyer?", ["A device for switching antennas from transmit to receive", "A device for voice activated switching from receive to transmit", "A device that assists in manual sending of Morse code", "An interlock to prevent unauthorized use of a radio"], 2, "An electronic keyer helps you send Morse by hand, generating evenly timed dots and dashes."], ["T4B01", "What is the effect of excessive microphone gain on SSB transmissions?", ["Frequency instability", "Distorted transmitted audio", "Increased SWR", "Sideband inversion"], 1, "Too much mic gain distorts SSB audio."], ["T4B02", "Which of the following can be used to enter a transceiver’s operating frequency?", ["The keypad or VFO knob", "The CTCSS or DTMF encoder", "The Automatic Frequency Control", "All these choices are correct"], 0, "Enter a frequency with the keypad or the VFO knob."], ["T4B03", "How is squelch adjusted so that a weak FM signal can be heard?", ["Set the squelch threshold so that receiver output audio is on all the time", "Turn up the audio level until it overcomes the squelch threshold", "Turn on the anti-squelch function", "Enable squelch enhancement"], 0, "To hear a weak FM signal, open the squelch so the audio is on all the time."], ["T4B04", "What does an FM signal sound like when received slightly off frequency?", ["The audio increases in pitch", "The audio decrease in pitch", "There is no effect except for reduction in amplitude", "The audio becomes distorted"], 3, "An FM signal received slightly off frequency sounds distorted."], ["T4B05", "What does the scanning function of an FM transceiver do?", ["Checks incoming signal deviation", "Prevents interference to nearby repeaters", "Tunes through a range of frequencies to check for activity", "Tunes through a range of frequencies to determine the antenna’s resonant frequency"], 2, "Scanning steps through frequencies looking for activity."], ["T4B06", "Which of the following controls could be used if the voice pitch of a single-sideband signal returning to your CQ call seems too high or low?", ["The AGC or limiter", "The bandwidth selection", "The tone squelch", "The RIT or Clarifier"], 3, "If an SSB voice sounds too high or low, adjust the RIT, also called the clarifier. It shifts receive frequency only."], ["T4B07", "What is a DMR “code plug”?", ["An adapter cable used to connect a DMR radio to a computer for internet access", "Configuration data loaded onto your radio to access repeaters and talkgroups", "An upgrade to DMR programming software provided by the radio manufacturer to accommodate new radio models", "A Coder-Decoder (CODEC) that converts analog voice data to DMR digital data and vice versa"], 1, "A DMR code plug is the configuration file loaded into your radio, with repeaters and talkgroups."], ["T4B08", "What is the advantage of having a choice of receiver filter bandwidths in a multimode transceiver?", ["Permits monitoring several modes simultaneously by selecting a separate filter for each mode", "Permits noise or interference reduction by selecting a bandwidth matching the mode", "Increases the number of frequencies that can be stored in memory", "Increases the amount of offset between receive and transmit frequencies"], 1, "Choosing a filter width that matches the mode cuts noise and interference."], ["T4B09", "How is a specific group of stations selected on a DMR digital voice transceiver?", ["By retrieving the frequencies from transceiver memory", "By enabling the group’s CTCSS tone", "By entering the group’s identification code", "By inserting a five-pin, pre-programmed code plug"], 2, "On DMR, select a group by entering its ID code."], ["T4B10", "Which of the following receiver filter bandwidths provides the best signal-to-noise ratio for SSB reception?", ["500 Hz", "1000 Hz", "2400 Hz", "5000 Hz"], 2, "For SSB voice, 2,400 Hz gives the best signal-to-noise ratio. It's about as wide as the voice signal."], ["T4B11", "Which of the following must be programmed into a D-STAR digital transceiver before transmitting?", ["Your call sign", "Your output power", "The codec type being used", "All these choices are correct"], 0, "A D-STAR radio must have your call sign programmed in before you transmit."]], "P": {"power": ["Powering the station", "<p>Mobile radios run on 13.8 volts; 50 watts out draws about 12 amps. Use short, heavy wires so voltage doesn't sag on transmit. In a car, connect the negative lead at the battery's chassis ground.</p><p>Battery runtime is amp-hours divided by average current draw.</p>"], "meters": ["Meters and bonding", "<p>An RF power meter or SWR meter goes in the feed line between transmitter and antenna. Choose one rated for your frequency and power.</p><p>Bond equipment with flat copper strap; its low impedance works best at RF.</p>"], "digital": ["Computers and digital modes", "<p>For digital modes like FT8, the interface carries receive audio, transmit audio and keying. The radio's audio out goes to the computer's line in, and the computer's audio out to the radio's input.</p><p>A hotspot links nearby digital radios to a network. An electronic keyer helps you send Morse by hand.</p>"], "controls": ["Front-panel controls", "<p>Enter frequency with the keypad or VFO knob. Scanning steps through frequencies looking for activity. To hear a weak FM signal, open the squelch all the way.</p><p>Too much mic gain distorts SSB. An off-frequency FM signal sounds distorted. If an SSB voice sounds too high or low, use RIT, the clarifier.</p>"], "filters": ["Receiver filters", "<p>Picking a filter width that matches the mode reduces noise and interference. For SSB voice, 2,400 Hz is right.</p>"], "dv": ["Digital voice radios", "<p>A DMR code plug is the configuration file with your repeaters and talkgroups. Select a group by entering its ID. A D-STAR radio needs your call sign programmed in before you transmit.</p>"]}, "CON": {"T4A01": "power", "T4A03": "power", "T4A11": "power", "T4A09": "power", "T4A05": "meters", "T4A02": "meters", "T4A08": "meters", "T4A06": "digital", "T4A04": "digital", "T4A07": "digital", "T4A10": "digital", "T4A12": "digital", "T4B02": "controls", "T4B05": "controls", "T4B03": "controls", "T4B01": "controls", "T4B04": "controls", "T4B06": "controls", "T4B08": "filters", "T4B10": "filters", "T4B07": "dv", "T4B09": "dv", "T4B11": "dv"}}, {"id": "t5", "code": "T5", "name": "Electrical principles", "exam": 4, "G": {"T5A": "Current, voltage, power and conductors", "T5B": "Metric prefixes and decibels", "T5C": "Capacitance, inductance, impedance and power math", "T5D": "Ohm's law and series and parallel circuits"}, "Q": [["T5A01", "Electrical current is measured in which of the following units?", ["Volts", "Watts", "Ohms", "Amperes"], 3, "Current is measured in amperes."], ["T5A02", "Electrical power is measured in which of the following units?", ["Volts", "Watts", "Watt-hours", "Amperes"], 1, "Power is measured in watts. Watt-hours measure energy used over time."], ["T5A03", "What is the term for the flow of electrons in an electric circuit?", ["Voltage", "Amperes per second", "Volts per second", "Current"], 3, "Current is the flow of electrons."], ["T5A04", "What term describes the number of times per second that an alternating current makes a complete cycle?", ["Pulse rate", "Wave number", "Frequency", "Wavelength"], 2, "Frequency is the number of complete cycles per second."], ["T5A05", "A difference in which of the following causes electron flow?", ["Voltage", "Ampere-hours", "Capacitance", "Inductance"], 0, "A difference in voltage pushes electrons, the way a difference in pressure pushes water."], ["T5A06", "What is the unit of frequency?", ["Hertz", "Henry", "Farad", "Epicycles per second"], 0, "Frequency is measured in hertz, one cycle per second. Henry (inductance) and farad (capacitance) are look-alike traps."], ["T5A07", "Why are metals generally good conductors of electricity?", ["They have relatively high density", "They have many free electrons", "They have many free protons", "They have relatively high Young’s modulus values"], 1, "Metals conduct well because they have many free electrons."], ["T5A08", "Which of the following is a good electrical insulator?", ["Sea water", "Glass", "Stainless steel", "Graphite"], 1, "Glass is a good insulator. Sea water, steel and graphite all conduct."], ["T5A09", "Which of the following describes alternating current?", ["Current that alternates between a positive direction and zero", "Current that alternates between a negative direction and zero", "Current that alternates between positive and negative directions", "All these answers are correct"], 2, "Alternating current reverses direction, swinging between positive and negative."], ["T5A10", "Which term describes the rate at which electrical energy is used?", ["Resistance", "Current", "Power", "Voltage"], 2, "Power is the rate at which electrical energy is used."], ["T5A11", "What type of current flow is opposed by resistance?", ["Direct current", "Alternating current", "RF current", "All these choices are correct"], 3, "Resistance opposes every kind of current: DC, AC and RF."], ["T5B01", "How many milliamperes is 1.5 amperes?", ["0.0000015 milliamperes", "0.0015 milliamperes", "1500 milliamperes", "1,500,000 milliamperes"], 2, "Milli means thousandths, so there are 1,000 milliamperes in an ampere. Going to the smaller unit, multiply: 1.5 &times; 1,000 = 1,500 mA."], ["T5B02", "Which is equal to 1,500,000 hertz?", ["1500 kHz", "1500 MHz", "15 GHz", "150 kHz"], 0, "Going to the bigger unit, divide. 1,500,000 Hz &divide; 1,000 = 1,500 kHz. (It's also 1.5 MHz, but that isn't offered.)"], ["T5B03", "Which is equal to one kilovolt?", ["One one-thousandth of a volt", "One hundred volts", "One thousand volts", "One million volts"], 2, "Kilo means thousand, as in kilogram or kilometer."], ["T5B04", "Which is equal to one microvolt?", ["One one-millionth of a volt", "One million volts", "One thousand kilovolts", "One one-thousandth of a volt"], 0, "Micro means one-millionth. One one-thousandth is milli, the trap answer."], ["T5B05", "Which is equal to 500 milliwatts?", ["5 watts", "0.5 watts", "500,000 watts", "500,000,000 watts"], 1, "Going to the bigger unit, divide by 1,000: 500 &divide; 1,000 = 0.5 W."], ["T5B06", "Which is equal to 3000 milliamperes?", ["0.003 amperes", "0.3 amperes", "3,000,000 amperes", "3 amperes"], 3, "Divide by 1,000: 3,000 mA &divide; 1,000 = 3 A."], ["T5B07", "Which is equal to 3.525 MHz?", ["0.003525 kHz", "35.25 kHz", "3525 kHz", "3,525,000 kHz"], 2, "Going to the smaller unit, multiply by 1,000: 3.525 &times; 1,000 = 3,525 kHz. Shortcut: slide the decimal three places right."], ["T5B08", "Which is equal to 1,000,000 picofarads?", ["0.001 microfarads", "1 microfarad", "1000 microfarads", "1,000,000,000 microfarads"], 1, "Pico is a millionth of a millionth; micro is a millionth. So a million picofarads make one microfarad."], ["T5B09", "Which decibel value most closely represents a power increase from 5 watts to 10 watts?", ["2 dB", "3 dB", "5 dB", "10 dB"], 1, "5 W to 10 W doubles the power. Doubling is always about +3 dB, whatever the starting level."], ["T5B10", "Which decibel value most closely represents a power decrease from 12 watts to 3 watts?", ["-1 dB", "-3 dB", "-6 dB", "-9 dB"], 2, "12 W to 6 W is one halving (&minus;3 dB); 6 W to 3 W is another (&minus;3 dB). Two halvings make &minus;6 dB."], ["T5B11", "Which decibel value represents a power increase from 20 watts to 200 watts?", ["10 dB", "12 dB", "18 dB", "28 dB"], 0, "20 W to 200 W is ten times the power, and a tenfold increase is exactly 10 dB."], ["T5B12", "Which is equal to 28400 kHz?", ["28.400 kHz", "2.800 MHz", "284.00 MHz", "28.400 MHz"], 3, "Going to the bigger unit, divide by 1,000: 28,400 &divide; 1,000 = 28.400 MHz. That's in the 10-meter band."], ["T5B13", "Which is equal to 2425 MHz?", ["0.002425 GHz", "24.25 GHz", "2.425 GHz", "242.5 GHz"], 2, "Divide by 1,000: 2,425 &divide; 1,000 = 2.425 GHz."], ["T5C01", "What describes the ability to store energy in an electric field?", ["Inductance", "Resistance", "Frequency", "Capacitance"], 3, "Capacitance stores energy in an electric field."], ["T5C02", "What is the unit of capacitance?", ["Farad", "Ohm", "Volt", "Henry"], 0, "Capacitance is measured in farads."], ["T5C03", "What describes the ability to store energy in a magnetic field?", ["Admittance", "Capacitance", "Resistance", "Inductance"], 3, "Inductance stores energy in a magnetic field."], ["T5C04", "What is the unit of inductance?", ["Coulomb", "Farad", "Henry", "Ohm"], 2, "Inductance is measured in henries."], ["T5C05", "What is the unit of impedance?", ["Volt", "Ampere", "Coulomb", "Ohm"], 3, "Impedance is measured in ohms, like resistance."], ["T5C06", "What is the abbreviation for kilohertz?", ["kHz", "khz", "KHz", "KHZ"], 0, "Kilohertz is kHz: lowercase k, capital H for Hertz, lowercase z."], ["T5C07", "What is the abbreviation for megahertz?", ["mHz", "mHZ", "Mhz", "MHz"], 3, "Megahertz is MHz. Capital M is mega; lowercase m would mean milli."], ["T5C08", "What formula is used to calculate electrical power (P) in a DC circuit?", ["P = I x E", "P = E / I", "P = I&sup2; x E", "P = I / E"], 0, "Power equals current times voltage. In ham notation, E is voltage and I is current."], ["T5C09", "How much power is delivered by a voltage of 13.8 volts DC and a current of 10 amperes?", ["138 watts", "0.7 watts", "23.8 watts", "3.8 watts"], 0, "P = I &times; E = 10 &times; 13.8 = 138 W."], ["T5C10", "How much power is delivered by a voltage of 12 volts DC and a current of 2.5 amperes?", ["4.8 watts", "30 watts", "14.5 watts", "0.208 watts"], 1, "P = I &times; E = 2.5 &times; 12 = 30 W."], ["T5C11", "How much current is required to deliver 120 watts at a voltage of 12 volts DC?", ["0.1 amperes", "10 amperes", "12 amperes", "132 amperes"], 1, "Rearrange P = I &times; E to I = P &divide; E. 120 &divide; 12 = 10 A."], ["T5C12", "What is impedance?", ["The opposition to AC current flow", "The inverse of resistance", "The inverse of reactance", "The power handling capability of a component"], 0, "Impedance is the opposition to AC current flow."], ["T5D01", "What formula is used to calculate current in a circuit?", ["I = E x R", "I = E / R", "I = E&sup2; x R", "I = E&sup2; / R"], 1, "Current equals voltage divided by resistance. More voltage pushes more current; more resistance allows less."], ["T5D02", "What formula is used to calculate voltage in a circuit?", ["E = I x R", "E = I / R", "E = I&sup2; x R", "E = I&sup2; / R"], 0, "Voltage equals current times resistance."], ["T5D03", "What formula is used to calculate resistance in a circuit?", ["R = E x I", "R = E / I", "R = E + I", "R = E - I"], 1, "Resistance equals voltage divided by current."], ["T5D04", "What is the resistance of a circuit in which a current of 3 amperes flows when connected to 90 volts?", ["3 ohms", "30 ohms", "1/30 ohm", "270 ohms"], 1, "R = E &divide; I = 90 &divide; 3 = 30 &Omega;."], ["T5D05", "What is the resistance of a circuit for which the applied voltage is 12 volts and the current flow is 1.5 amperes?", ["18 ohms", "0.125 ohms", "8 ohms", "13.5 ohms"], 2, "R = E &divide; I = 12 &divide; 1.5 = 8 &Omega;."], ["T5D06", "What is the resistance of a circuit that draws 4 amperes from a 12-volt source?", ["3 ohms", "16 ohms", "48 ohms", "8 ohms"], 0, "R = E &divide; I = 12 &divide; 4 = 3 &Omega;."], ["T5D07", "What is the current in a circuit with an applied voltage of 120 volts and a resistance of 80 ohms?", ["9600 amperes", "200 amperes", "0.667 amperes", "1.5 amperes"], 3, "I = E &divide; R = 120 &divide; 80 = 1.5 A. Note 0.667 is the trap: it's 80 &divide; 120, upside down."], ["T5D08", "What is the current through a 100-ohm resistor connected across 200 volts?", ["20,000 amperes", "0.5 amperes", "2 amperes", "100 amperes"], 2, "I = E &divide; R = 200 &divide; 100 = 2 A."], ["T5D09", "What is the current through a 24-ohm resistor connected across 240 volts?", ["2400 amperes", "0.1 amperes", "10 amperes", "5760 amperes"], 2, "I = E &divide; R = 240 &divide; 24 = 10 A."], ["T5D10", "What is the voltage across a 2-ohm resistor if a current of 0.5 amperes flows through it?", ["1 volt", "0.25 volts", "2.5 volts", "1.5 volts"], 0, "E = I &times; R = 0.5 &times; 2 = 1 V."], ["T5D11", "What is the voltage across a 10-ohm resistor if a current of 1 ampere flows through it?", ["1 volt", "10 volts", "11 volts", "9 volts"], 1, "E = I &times; R = 1 &times; 10 = 10 V."], ["T5D12", "What is the voltage across a 10-ohm resistor if a current of 2 amperes flows through it?", ["8 volts", "0.2 volts", "12 volts", "20 volts"], 3, "E = I &times; R = 2 &times; 10 = 20 V."], ["T5D13", "In which type of circuit is the current always the same through all components?", ["Series", "Parallel", "Resonant", "Branch"], 0, "In a series circuit there is only one path, so the same current flows through every component."], ["T5D14", "In which type of circuit is the voltage always the same across all components?", ["Series", "Parallel", "Resonant", "Branch"], 1, "In a parallel circuit every component connects across the same two points, so each sees the same voltage."]], "P": {"basics": ["Current, voltage and power", "<p>Current is the flow of electrons, measured in amperes. Voltage is the push that makes it flow. Power is the rate energy is used, measured in watts.</p><p>Metals conduct because they have many free electrons. Glass insulates. Resistance opposes all current, DC, AC and RF alike.</p>"], "ac": ["Alternating current and frequency", "<p>Alternating current reverses between positive and negative. Frequency is the number of complete cycles per second, measured in hertz.</p>"], "prefix": ["Metric prefixes", "<p>Pico is a trillionth, micro a millionth, milli a thousandth. Kilo is a thousand, mega a million, giga a billion.</p><p>Going to a bigger unit, divide by 1,000 per step; to a smaller unit, multiply. Write kHz and MHz exactly that way.</p>"], "db": ["Decibels", "<p>Doubling power is +3 dB, halving is &minus;3 dB, four times (two doublings) is +6 dB, ten times is +10 dB. Decreases are the same numbers, negative: a quarter of the power is &minus;6 dB.</p>"], "lc": ["Capacitance, inductance and impedance", "<p>Capacitance stores energy in an electric field and is measured in farads. Inductance stores it in a magnetic field and is measured in henries. Impedance is the opposition to AC, measured in ohms.</p>"], "power": ["Power formula", "<p>P = I &times; E. P is power in watts, I is current in amperes, E is voltage in volts. To find current, I = P &divide; E.</p>"], "ohm": ["Ohm's law", "<p>E = I &times; R. E is voltage in volts, I is current in amperes, R is resistance in ohms. Rearranged: I = E &divide; R and R = E &divide; I.</p><p>Memory aid: picture a triangle with E on top and I and R side by side below. Cover the one you want: if the other two sit side by side, multiply; if one sits over the other, divide.</p>"], "circuits": ["Series and parallel", "<p>In series there's one path, so the current is the same through every part. In parallel every part sits across the same two points, so the voltage is the same.</p>"]}, "CON": {"T5A03": "basics", "T5A01": "basics", "T5A05": "basics", "T5A10": "basics", "T5A02": "basics", "T5A07": "basics", "T5A08": "basics", "T5A11": "basics", "T5A09": "ac", "T5A04": "ac", "T5A06": "ac", "T5B01": "prefix", "T5B02": "prefix", "T5B03": "prefix", "T5B04": "prefix", "T5B05": "prefix", "T5B06": "prefix", "T5B07": "prefix", "T5B08": "prefix", "T5B12": "prefix", "T5B13": "prefix", "T5C06": "prefix", "T5C07": "prefix", "T5B09": "db", "T5B10": "db", "T5B11": "db", "T5C01": "lc", "T5C02": "lc", "T5C03": "lc", "T5C04": "lc", "T5C12": "lc", "T5C05": "lc", "T5C08": "power", "T5C09": "power", "T5C10": "power", "T5C11": "power", "T5D01": "ohm", "T5D02": "ohm", "T5D03": "ohm", "T5D04": "ohm", "T5D05": "ohm", "T5D06": "ohm", "T5D07": "ohm", "T5D08": "ohm", "T5D09": "ohm", "T5D10": "ohm", "T5D11": "ohm", "T5D12": "ohm", "T5D13": "circuits", "T5D14": "circuits"}}, {"id": "t6", "code": "T6", "name": "Components", "exam": 4, "G": {"T6A": "Components: resistors, capacitors, inductors, switches, batteries", "T6B": "Semiconductors: diodes, transistors, gain", "T6C": "Schematics and symbols", "T6D": "What components do"}, "Q": [["T6A01", "What electrical component opposes the flow of current in a DC circuit?", ["Inductor", "Resistor", "Inverter", "Transformer"], 1, "Resistance is opposition to current; a resistor is the part built to provide it."], ["T6A02", "What type of component is often used as an adjustable volume control?", ["Fixed resistor", "Power resistor", "Potentiometer", "Transformer"], 2, "A potentiometer is a variable resistor with a knob or slider."], ["T6A03", "What electrical parameter is controlled by a potentiometer?", ["Inductance", "Resistance", "Capacitance", "Field strength"], 1, "A potentiometer is a variable resistor, so it controls resistance."], ["T6A04", "What electrical component stores energy in an electric field?", ["Resistor", "Capacitor", "Inductor", "Diode"], 1, "Electric field: capacitor. Magnetic field: inductor."], ["T6A05", "What type of electrical component consists of conductive surfaces separated by an insulator?", ["Resistor", "Potentiometer", "Oscillator", "Capacitor"], 3, "Two plates with an insulator (the dielectric) between them is the definition of a capacitor."], ["T6A06", "What type of electrical component stores energy in a magnetic field?", ["Resistor", "Capacitor", "Inductor", "Diode"], 2, "Current through a coil creates a magnetic field; that's an inductor."], ["T6A07", "What electrical component is typically constructed as a coil of wire?", ["Transistor", "Capacitor", "Diode", "Inductor"], 3, "Coil means inductor. The schematic symbol even looks like loops of wire."], ["T6A08", "What is the function of an SPDT switch?", ["A single circuit is opened or closed", "Two circuits are opened or closed", "A single circuit is switched between one of two other circuits", "Two circuits are each switched between one of two other circuits"], 2, "Single pole: one circuit. Double throw: two places it can connect to."], ["T6A09", "What type of switch is represented by component 3 in figure T-2?", ["Single-pole single-throw", "Single-pole double-throw", "Double-pole single-throw", "Double-pole double-throw"], 0, "Component 3 in T-2 is a simple on/off switch: one pole, one throw.", "T-2"], ["T6A10", "Which of the following battery chemistries is rechargeable?", ["Nickel-metal hydride", "Lithium-ion", "Lead-acid", "All these choices are correct"], 3, "NiMH, lithium-ion and lead-acid all recharge."], ["T6A11", "Which of the following battery chemistries is not rechargeable?", ["Nickel-cadmium", "Carbon-zinc", "Lead-acid", "Lithium-ion"], 1, "Carbon-zinc is the cheap disposable cell. Note that nickel-cadmium is rechargeable."], ["T6B01", "Which is true about forward voltage drop in a diode?", ["It is lower in some diode types than in others", "It is proportional to peak inverse voltage", "It indicates that the diode is defective", "It has no impact on the voltage delivered to the load"], 0, "Every conducting diode drops some voltage; the amount depends on the type, roughly 0.7 V for silicon and less for Schottky."], ["T6B02", "What electronic component allows current to flow in only one direction?", ["Resistor", "Fuse", "Diode", "Driven element"], 2, "A diode is a one-way valve for current."], ["T6B03", "Which of these components can be used as an electronic switch?", ["Varistor", "Potentiometer", "Transistor", "Thermistor"], 2, "A transistor can switch or amplify."], ["T6B04", "Which of the following components can consist of three regions of semiconductor material?", ["Alternator", "Transistor", "Triode", "Pentode"], 1, "A bipolar transistor is three layers, NPN or PNP. Triodes and pentodes are vacuum tubes."], ["T6B05", "What type of transistor has a gate, drain, and source?", ["Varistor", "Field-effect", "Hall-effect", "Bipolar junction"], 1, "FET: gate, drain, source. Bipolar: emitter, base, collector."], ["T6B06", "How is the cathode lead of a semiconductor diode often marked on the package?", ["With the word \"cathode\"", "With a stripe", "With the letter C", "With the letter K"], 1, "The stripe on the body matches the bar in the schematic symbol."], ["T6B07", "What causes a light-emitting diode (LED) to emit light?", ["Forward current", "Reverse current", "Capacitively-coupled RF signal", "Inductively-coupled RF signal"], 0, "An LED lights when current flows the way the diode allows: forward."], ["T6B08", "What does the abbreviation FET stand for?", ["Frequency Emission Transmitter", "Fast Electron Transistor", "Free Electron Transmitter", "Field Effect Transistor"], 3, "Field Effect Transistor."], ["T6B09", "What are the names for the electrodes of a diode?", ["Plus and minus", "Source and drain", "Anode and cathode", "Gate and base"], 2, "Anode and cathode. Source and drain belong to a FET."], ["T6B10", "Which of the following can provide power gain?", ["Transformer", "Transistor", "Reactor", "Resistor"], 1, "Only an active device can add power. A transformer can raise voltage but never power."], ["T6B11", "What does the term gain mean in amplifiers?", ["The output signal voltage relative to the input signal voltage", "The output signal current relative to the input signal current", "The output signal power relative to the input signal power", "All these choices are correct"], 3, "Gain can be expressed in voltage, current or power."], ["T6B12", "What are the names of the electrodes of a bipolar junction transistor?", ["Signal, bias, power", "Emitter, base, collector", "Input, output, supply", "Pole one, pole two, output"], 1, "Emitter, base, collector."], ["T6C01", "What is an electrical diagram using standard component symbols called?", ["Connection chart", "Instrumentation system", "Schematic", "Flow chart"], 2, "A schematic: a circuit diagram drawn with a standard symbol for each part."], ["T6C02", "What is component 1 in figure T-1?", ["Resistor", "Transistor", "Battery", "Connector"], 0, "Figure T-1 key: 1 resistor, 2 transistor, 3 lamp, 4 battery.", "T-1"], ["T6C03", "What is component 2 in figure T-1?", ["Resistor", "Transistor", "Indicator lamp", "Connector"], 1, "Figure T-1 key: 1 resistor, 2 transistor, 3 lamp, 4 battery.", "T-1"], ["T6C04", "What is component 3 in figure T-1?", ["Resistor", "Transistor", "Lamp", "Ground symbol"], 2, "Figure T-1 key: 1 resistor, 2 transistor, 3 lamp, 4 battery.", "T-1"], ["T6C05", "What is component 4 in figure T-1?", ["Resistor", "Transistor", "Ground symbol", "Battery"], 3, "Figure T-1 key: 1 resistor, 2 transistor, 3 lamp, 4 battery.", "T-1"], ["T6C06", "What is component 6 in figure T-2?", ["Resistor", "Capacitor", "Regulator IC", "Transistor"], 1, "Figure T-2 key: 3 SPST switch, 4 transformer, 6 capacitor, 8 LED, 9 variable resistor.", "T-2"], ["T6C07", "What is component 8 in figure T-2?", ["Resistor", "Inductor", "Regulator IC", "Light emitting diode"], 3, "Figure T-2 key: 3 SPST switch, 4 transformer, 6 capacitor, 8 LED, 9 variable resistor.", "T-2"], ["T6C08", "What is component 9 in figure T-2?", ["Variable capacitor", "Variable inductor", "Variable resistor", "Variable transformer"], 2, "Figure T-2 key: 3 SPST switch, 4 transformer, 6 capacitor, 8 LED, 9 variable resistor.", "T-2"], ["T6C09", "What is component 4 in figure T-2?", ["Variable inductor", "Double-pole switch", "Potentiometer", "Transformer"], 3, "Figure T-2 key: 3 SPST switch, 4 transformer, 6 capacitor, 8 LED, 9 variable resistor.", "T-2"], ["T6C10", "What is component 3 in figure T-3?", ["Connector", "Meter", "Variable capacitor", "Variable inductor"], 3, "Figure T-3 key: 3 variable inductor, 4 antenna.", "T-3"], ["T6C11", "What is component 4 in figure T-3?", ["Antenna", "Transmitter", "Dummy load", "Ground"], 0, "Figure T-3 key: 3 variable inductor, 4 antenna.", "T-3"], ["T6C12", "Which of the following is accurately represented in electrical schematics?", ["Wire lengths", "Physical appearance of components", "Component connections", "All these choices are correct"], 2, "A schematic shows only how parts connect, not layout or wire length."], ["T6D01", "Which of the following devices or circuits changes an alternating current into a varying direct current signal?", ["Transformer", "Rectifier", "Amplifier", "Reflector"], 1, "A rectifier turns AC into DC."], ["T6D02", "What is a relay?", ["An electrically-controlled switch", "A current-controlled amplifier", "An inverting amplifier", "A pass transistor"], 0, "A relay is a switch flipped by electricity: current through a coil makes an electromagnet that moves the switch contacts."], ["T6D03", "Which of the following is a reason to use shielded wire?", ["To decrease the resistance of DC power connections", "To increase the current carrying capability of the wire", "To prevent coupling of unwanted signals to or from the wire", "To reduce receiver overload"], 2, "Shielding keeps signals from leaking in or out."], ["T6D04", "Which of the following displays an electrical quantity as a numeric value?", ["Potentiometer", "Transistor", "Meter", "Relay"], 2, "A meter displays a measurement."], ["T6D05", "What type of circuit controls the amount of voltage from a power supply?", ["Regulator", "Oscillator", "Filter", "Phase inverter"], 0, "A voltage regulator holds the output steady."], ["T6D06", "What component changes 120 V AC power to a lower AC voltage for other uses?", ["Variable capacitor", "Transformer", "Transistor", "Diode"], 1, "Transformers step AC voltage up or down. The output is still AC."], ["T6D07", "Which of the following is commonly used as a visual indicator?", ["LED", "FET", "Zener diode", "All these choices are correct"], 0, "An LED. A Zener diode doesn't light up."], ["T6D08", "Which of the following is combined with an inductor to make a resonant circuit?", ["Resistor", "Zener diode", "Potentiometer", "Capacitor"], 3, "Inductor plus capacitor makes a tuned circuit."], ["T6D09", "What is the name of a device that combines several semiconductors and other components into one package?", ["Transducer", "Multi-pole relay", "Integrated circuit", "Transformer"], 2, "An integrated circuit, or IC."], ["T6D10", "What is the function of component 2 in figure T-1?", ["Give off light when current flows through it", "Supply electrical energy", "Control the flow of current", "Convert electrical energy into radio waves"], 2, "Component 2 in T-1 is the transistor, and a transistor controls current. Giving off light is the lamp, component 3.", "T-1"], ["T6D11", "Which of the following is a resonant or tuned circuit?", ["An inductor and a capacitor in series or parallel", "A linear voltage regulator", "A resistor circuit used for reducing standing wave ratio", "A circuit designed to provide high-fidelity audio"], 0, "Inductor plus capacitor, either arrangement."]], "P": {"passive": ["Resistors, capacitors and inductors", "<p>A resistor opposes the flow of current. A potentiometer is a resistor you can adjust, like a volume knob, so it controls resistance.</p><p>A capacitor stores energy in an electric field, using conductive surfaces separated by an insulator. An inductor stores energy in a magnetic field and is usually built as a coil of wire.</p><p>Memory hook: electric field, capacitor; magnetic field, inductor.</p>"], "switch": ["Switches", "<p>Switches are named by poles and throws. Poles are how many separate circuits the switch controls; throws are how many positions each pole can connect to.</p><p>A single-pole single-throw (SPST) switch simply opens or closes one circuit. A single-pole double-throw (SPDT) switch connects one circuit to either of two others.</p>"], "battery": ["Batteries", "<p>Common rechargeable chemistries are nickel-metal hydride (NiMH), nickel-cadmium (NiCd), lithium-ion and lead-acid.</p><p>Carbon-zinc cells, the cheap disposable kind, are not rechargeable.</p>"], "diode": ["Diodes and LEDs", "<p>A diode lets current flow in only one direction. Its two electrodes are the anode and the cathode, and the cathode end is usually marked with a stripe.</p><p>Current flowing the allowed way is forward current. While conducting, a diode drops a small voltage, called the forward voltage drop, and the amount differs between diode types.</p><p>An LED (light-emitting diode) is a diode that gives off light when forward current flows through it.</p>"], "transistor": ["Transistors and gain", "<p>A transistor is a semiconductor, often built from three layers of material, that can act as an electronic switch or as an amplifier.</p><p>A bipolar junction transistor's electrodes are the emitter, base and collector. A field-effect transistor (FET) has a gate, drain and source.</p><p>Gain is how much an amplifier increases a signal: output relative to input, in voltage, current or power. A transistor can provide power gain; passive parts like transformers and resistors cannot.</p>"], "schem": ["Schematics", "<p>A schematic is a circuit diagram drawn with standard component symbols.</p><p>It shows how components are connected. It does not show what they look like or how long the wires are.</p>"], "fig1": ["Figure T-1", "<p>Figure T-1 is a simple circuit in which a transistor turns a lamp on and off.</p><p>Its numbered parts: 1 is a resistor (zigzag), 2 is a transistor (circle with an arrow on one lead), 3 is a lamp (dome shape) and 4 is a battery (long and short lines). The transistor's job is to control the flow of current to the lamp.</p>"], "fig2": ["Figure T-2", "<p>Figure T-2 is a power supply. AC comes in through a fuse (2) and an on/off switch (3, a single-pole single-throw switch). A transformer (4) lowers the voltage, a diode (5) changes it to DC, and a capacitor (6) smooths it.</p><p>An LED (8) with its resistor (7) lights to show power is on. Component 9 is a variable resistor, drawn as a zigzag with an arrow pointing into it.</p>"], "fig3": ["Figure T-3", "<p>Figure T-3 is an antenna tuner, which matches a radio to its antenna.</p><p>It uses variable capacitors (2, plates with a diagonal arrow through them), a variable inductor (3, a coil with an arrow contact that slides along it) and an antenna (4, an upside-down triangle on a stem).</p>"], "psu": ["Power supply parts", "<p>A transformer changes AC voltage up or down, such as 120 V AC to a lower AC voltage.</p><p>A rectifier, built from diodes, changes alternating current into direct current. A regulator controls the voltage coming out of a power supply so it stays steady.</p>"], "control": ["Control and display parts", "<p>A relay is an electrically controlled switch. A meter displays an electrical quantity as a value. An LED is commonly used as a visual indicator.</p><p>An integrated circuit (IC) combines several semiconductors and other components into one package.</p>"], "tuned": ["Tuned circuits and shielding", "<p>An inductor and a capacitor connected together, in series or parallel, make a resonant or tuned circuit, which favors one frequency. That's how a radio picks out one station.</p><p>Shielded wire has a metal layer around it that keeps unwanted signals from coupling into or out of the wire.</p>"]}, "CON": {"T6A01": "passive", "T6A02": "passive", "T6A03": "passive", "T6A04": "passive", "T6A05": "passive", "T6A06": "passive", "T6A07": "passive", "T6A08": "switch", "T6A09": "fig2", "T6A10": "battery", "T6A11": "battery", "T6B01": "diode", "T6B02": "diode", "T6B06": "diode", "T6B07": "diode", "T6B09": "diode", "T6B03": "transistor", "T6B04": "transistor", "T6B05": "transistor", "T6B08": "transistor", "T6B10": "transistor", "T6B11": "transistor", "T6B12": "transistor", "T6C01": "schem", "T6C12": "schem", "T6C02": "fig1", "T6C03": "fig1", "T6C04": "fig1", "T6C05": "fig1", "T6D10": "fig1", "T6C06": "fig2", "T6C07": "fig2", "T6C08": "fig2", "T6C09": "fig2", "T6C10": "fig3", "T6C11": "fig3", "T6D01": "psu", "T6D05": "psu", "T6D06": "psu", "T6D02": "control", "T6D04": "control", "T6D07": "control", "T6D09": "control", "T6D03": "tuned", "T6D08": "tuned", "T6D11": "tuned"}}, {"id": "t7", "code": "T7", "name": "Practical circuits", "exam": 4, "G": {"T7A": "Station equipment and radio circuits", "T7B": "Interference and transmitter problems", "T7C": "SWR, dummy loads and coax", "T7D": "Test meters and soldering"}, "Q": [["T7A01", "Which term describes the ability of a receiver to detect the presence of a signal?", ["RF gain", "Sensitivity", "Selectivity", "Total Harmonic Distortion"], 1, "Sensitivity is how weak a signal the receiver can detect."], ["T7A02", "What is a transceiver?", ["A device that combines a receiver and transmitter", "A device for matching feed line impedance to 50 ohms", "A device for automatically sending and decoding Morse code", "A device for converting receiver and transmitter frequencies to another band"], 0, "Transceiver: transmitter plus receiver in one box, like your FT-65R."], ["T7A03", "Which of the following is used to convert a signal from one frequency to another?", ["Phase splitter", "Mixer", "Inverter", "Amplifier"], 1, "A mixer combines two frequencies to produce new ones, shifting a signal from one frequency to another."], ["T7A04", "Which term describes the ability of a receiver to discriminate between multiple signals?", ["Discrimination ratio", "Sensitivity", "Selectivity", "Harmonic distortion"], 2, "Selectivity is the ability to separate a wanted signal from nearby ones. Sensitivity is the detection one."], ["T7A05", "What is the name of a circuit that generates a signal at a specific frequency?", ["Reactance modulator", "Phase modulator", "Low-pass filter", "Oscillator"], 3, "An oscillator generates a signal at a set frequency."], ["T7A06", "What device converts the RF input and output of a transceiver to another band?", ["High-pass filter", "Low-pass filter", "Transverter", "Phase converter"], 2, "A transverter shifts a transceiver's RF to another band, such as running a 10-meter rig on 2 meters."], ["T7A07", "What is the function of a transceiver’s PTT input?", ["Input for a key used to send CW", "Switches transceiver from receive to transmit when grounded", "Provides a transmit tuning tone when grounded", "Input for a preamplifier tuning tone"], 1, "PTT, push to talk. Grounding the PTT line switches the rig from receive to transmit."], ["T7A08", "Which of the following describes combining speech with an RF carrier signal?", ["Impedance matching", "Oscillation", "Modulation", "Low-pass filtering"], 2, "Modulation is putting information, like your voice, onto a carrier."], ["T7A09", "What is the function of the switch which selects either SSB or CW-FM on some VHF power amplifiers?", ["Change the mode of the transmitted signal", "Set the amplifier for proper operation in the selected mode", "Change the frequency range of the amplifier to operate in the proper segment of the band", "Reduce the received signal noise"], 1, "The SSB/CW-FM switch sets the amplifier to operate properly in that mode. It doesn't change the mode itself."], ["T7A10", "What can be added to the output of a transceiver to increase the transmitted output power?", ["A potentiometer", "An RF power amplifier", "An impedance multiplier", "All these choices are correct"], 1, "An RF power amplifier boosts transmitter output."], ["T7A11", "What is the function of the Variable Frequency Oscillator (VFO) circuit in a transceiver?", ["Set the receive and transmit frequency", "Provide automatic frequency control", "Inject a variable frequency to allow CW reception", "Generate and demodulate single sideband signals"], 0, "The VFO sets the frequency you receive and transmit on: the tuning knob."], ["T7B01", "What can you do if you are told your FM handheld or mobile transceiver is over-deviating?", ["Talk louder into the microphone", "Let the transceiver cool off", "Change to a higher power level", "Talk farther away from the microphone"], 3, "Over-deviating means your voice is driving the FM signal too wide. Back away from the mic."], ["T7B02", "What would cause a broadcast AM or FM radio to receive an amateur radio transmission unintentionally?", ["The receiver is unable to reject strong signals outside the AM or FM band", "The microphone gain of the transmitter is turned up too high", "The audio amplifier of the transmitter is overloaded", "The deviation of an FM transmitter is set too low"], 0, "A consumer radio that hears you usually can't reject strong signals outside its band. That's the receiver's weakness, not your fault."], ["T7B03", "Which of the following can cause radio frequency interference?", ["Fundamental overload", "Harmonics", "Spurious emissions", "All these choices are correct"], 3, "Fundamental overload, harmonics and spurious emissions all cause RFI."], ["T7B04", "Which of the following might be the cause of low RF power output from a solid-state transceiver?", ["Poor amplifier noise figure", "Poor amplifier linearity", "Low SWR", "High SWR"], 3, "High SWR makes solid-state rigs fold back power to protect themselves, so output drops."], ["T7B05", "Which of the following might reduce interference by an amateur station to a non-amateur over-the-air radio receiver?", ["Block the amateur signal with a filter at the antenna input of the affected receiver", "Block the interfering signal with a filter on the amateur transmitter", "Switch the transmitter from FM to SSB", "Switch the transmitter to a narrow-band mode"], 0, "Fix it at the victim: a filter at the affected receiver's antenna input blocks your signal."], ["T7B06", "Which of the following actions should you take if a neighbor tells you that your station’s transmissions are interfering with their radio or TV reception?", ["Make sure that your station is functioning properly and that it does not cause interference to your own radio or television when it is tuned to the same channel", "Immediately turn off your transmitter and contact the nearest FCC office for assistance", "Install a harmonic doubler on the output of your transmitter and tune it until the interference is eliminated", "All these choices are correct"], 0, "First check your own station: does it interfere with your own TV or radio on the same channel?"], ["T7B07", "Which of the following can reduce interference to a 2-meter band transceiver from a nearby commercial FM station?", ["Installing an RF preamplifier", "Using double-shielded coaxial cable", "Installing bypass capacitors on the microphone cable", "Installing a band-reject filter"], 3, "A band-reject filter knocks out the strong commercial FM signal. A preamp would make it worse."], ["T7B08", "What should you do if something in a neighbor’s home is causing harmful interference to your amateur station?", ["Work with your neighbor to identify the offending device", "Politely inform your neighbor that FCC rules prohibit the use of devices that cause interference", "Make sure your station meets the standards of good amateur practice", "All these choices are correct"], 3, "Work with the neighbor, explain the rules politely and make sure your own station is clean. All of these."], ["T7B09", "What should be the first step to resolve non-fiber optic cable TV interference caused by your amateur radio transmission?", ["Add a low-pass filter to the TV antenna input", "Add a high-pass filter to the TV antenna input", "Add a preamplifier to the TV antenna input", "Be sure all TV feed line coaxial connectors are installed properly"], 3, "With cable TV, check that all the coax connectors are installed properly first."], ["T7B10", "What might be a problem if you receive a report that your audio signal through an FM repeater is distorted or unintelligible?", ["Your transmitter is slightly off frequency", "You are speaking too loudly or too close to the microphone", "You are in a bad location", "All these choices are correct"], 3, "Off frequency, too loud or too close to the mic, or a bad location can each garble you through a repeater."], ["T7B11", "Which of the following can eliminate distorted voice transmissions?", ["Adding extra feedline to the antenna to lower SWR", "Turning the radio on and off to reset the computer-controlled circuitry", "Adding a clip-on ferrite “choke” to the microphone cable to prevent the transmitted signal from feeding back into the transmitter", "Turning the squelch control fully clockwise to prevent the transmitted signal from triggering the squelch circuit"], 2, "RF getting back into the mic cable distorts audio. A clip-on ferrite choke stops it."], ["T7C01", "What is the primary purpose of a dummy load?", ["To prevent transmitting signals over the air when making tests", "To prevent over-modulation of a transmitter", "To improve the efficiency of an antenna", "To improve the signal-to-noise ratio of a receiver"], 0, "A dummy load lets you test a transmitter without putting a signal on the air."], ["T7C02", "Which of the following is used to determine if an antenna is resonant at the desired operating frequency?", ["A VTVM", "An antenna analyzer", "A Q meter", "A frequency counter"], 1, "An antenna analyzer shows whether an antenna is resonant at your frequency."], ["T7C03", "What does a typical RF dummy load consist of?", ["A low-voltage power supply and an AC relay", "A 50-ohm non-inductive resistor mounted on a heat sink", "A low-voltage power supply and a DC relay", "A 50-ohm inductive reactance mounted in a shielded enclosure"], 1, "A dummy load is a 50-ohm non-inductive resistor on a heat sink. It turns RF into heat."], ["T7C04", "What reading on an SWR meter indicates a perfect impedance match between the antenna and the feed line?", ["50:50", "Zero", "1:1", "Full Scale"], 2, "1:1 SWR is a perfect match."], ["T7C05", "Why do most solid-state transmitters reduce output power as SWR increases beyond a certain level?", ["To protect the RF output amplifier transistors", "To comply with FCC rules on spectral purity", "Because power supplies cannot supply enough current at high SWR", "To lower the SWR on the transmission line"], 0, "Solid-state transmitters cut power at high SWR to protect the output transistors."], ["T7C06", "What does an SWR reading of 4:1 indicate?", ["Loss of -4 dB", "Good impedance match", "Gain of +4 dB", "Impedance mismatch"], 3, "4:1 is a significant impedance mismatch. SWR is a ratio, not decibels."], ["T7C07", "What happens to power lost in a feed line?", ["It increases the SWR", "It is radiated as harmonics", "It is converted into heat", "It distorts the signal"], 2, "Power lost in feed line becomes heat."], ["T7C08", "Which instrument can be used to determine SWR?", ["Voltmeter", "Ohmmeter", "Iambic pentameter", "Directional wattmeter"], 3, "A directional wattmeter reads forward and reflected power, which gives you SWR. \"Iambic pentameter\" is the joke answer."], ["T7C09", "Which of the following causes failure of coaxial cables?", ["Moisture contamination", "Solder flux contamination", "Rapid fluctuation in transmitter output power", "Operation at 100% duty cycle for an extended period"], 0, "Water getting into coax is the main way it fails."], ["T7C10", "Why should the outer jacket of coaxial cable be resistant to ultraviolet light?", ["Ultraviolet light can increase the resistance of the conductors", "Ultraviolet light can increase losses in the cable’s jacket", "Ultraviolet and RF signals can mix, causing interference", "Ultraviolet light can damage the jacket and allow water to enter the cable"], 3, "Sunlight's UV cracks the jacket, and then water gets in."], ["T7C11", "What is an advantage of foam-dielectric versus solid-dielectric coaxial cable?", ["It is more resistant to moisture contamination", "It has higher voltage breakdown", "It has less loss per foot", "It has a better impedance match to 50 ohms"], 2, "Foam dielectric has lower loss per foot than solid."], ["T7D01", "Which instrument would you use to measure electric potential?", ["An ammeter", "A voltmeter", "A potentiometer", "An ohmmeter"], 1, "A voltmeter measures electric potential, which is voltage."], ["T7D02", "How is a voltmeter connected to a component to measure applied voltage?", ["In series", "In parallel", "In quadrature", "In phase"], 1, "Voltmeters go in parallel, across the component."], ["T7D03", "When configured to measure current, how is a multimeter connected to a component?", ["In series", "In parallel", "In quadrature", "In phase"], 0, "To measure current, the meter goes in series, so the current flows through it."], ["T7D04", "Which instrument is used to measure electric current?", ["An ohmmeter", "An electrometer", "A voltmeter", "An ammeter"], 3, "An ammeter measures current."], ["T7D05", "How does an ohmmeter measure the resistance of a circuit or component?", ["By applying a small current and measuring the resulting voltage", "By placing a variable resistor in parallel with the circuit", "By placing a variable resistor in series with the circuit", "By applying a variable voltage and measuring the resulting current change"], 0, "An ohmmeter pushes a small current through and measures the resulting voltage."], ["T7D06", "Which of the following can damage a multimeter?", ["Attempting to measure resistance using the voltage setting", "Failing to connect one of the probes to ground", "Attempting to measure voltage when using the resistance setting", "Not allowing it to warm up properly"], 2, "Measuring voltage on the resistance setting can damage the meter, because the meter supplies its own current on that setting."], ["T7D07", "Which of the following measurements are made using a multimeter?", ["Signal strength and noise", "Impedance and reactance", "Voltage and resistance", "All these choices are correct"], 2, "A multimeter measures voltage and resistance (and current)."], ["T7D08", "Which of the following types of solder should not be used for radio and electronic applications?", ["Acid-core solder", "Lead-tin solder", "Rosin-core solder", "Tin-copper solder"], 0, "Never use acid-core solder on electronics. The acid corrodes connections."], ["T7D09", "What is the characteristic appearance of a cold tin-lead solder joint?", ["Dark black spots", "A bright or shiny surface", "A rough or lumpy surface", "A greenish tinge"], 2, "A cold joint looks rough or lumpy. A good one is smooth and shiny."], ["T7D10", "What reading indicates that an ohmmeter is connected across a large, discharged capacitor?", ["Increasing resistance with time", "Decreasing resistance with time", "Steady full-scale reading", "Alternating between open and short circuit"], 0, "The ohmmeter charges the capacitor, so current falls and the resistance reading climbs over time."], ["T7D11", "Which of the following precautions should be taken when measuring in-circuit resistance with an ohmmeter?", ["Ensure that the applied voltages are correct", "Ensure that the circuit is not powered", "Ensure that the circuit is grounded", "Ensure that the circuit is operating at the correct frequency"], 1, "Measure resistance only with the circuit unpowered."]], "P": {"t7a": ["Inside a radio", "<p>A transceiver is a transmitter and receiver in one. The VFO sets your frequency. An oscillator generates a signal; a mixer shifts a signal to a new frequency; modulation puts your voice onto the carrier.</p><p>Sensitivity is how weak a signal a receiver can detect. Selectivity is how well it separates nearby signals.</p>"], "t7aadd": ["Add-ons", "<p>An RF power amplifier boosts output; its SSB or CW-FM switch sets it up for that mode. A transverter moves a rig to another band. Grounding the PTT line puts the rig in transmit.</p>"], "t7b": ["Interference and distortion", "<p>RFI comes from fundamental overload, harmonics and spurious emissions. When a neighbor complains, check first that your station doesn't bother your own TV or radio. Cures usually go at the affected receiver: a filter at its antenna input, a band-reject filter against a strong FM station, good coax connectors on cable TV.</p><p>If something in a neighbor's house bothers you, work with them politely and keep your own station clean.</p>"], "t7baudio": ["Bad audio and low power", "<p>Over-deviating FM: back away from the mic. Garbled through a repeater: off frequency, too close or loud, or a bad spot. RF in the mic cable: add a ferrite choke. Low output from a solid-state rig: suspect high SWR.</p>"], "t7c": ["SWR and dummy loads", "<p>SWR measures the match between feed line and antenna. 1:1 is perfect; 4:1 is a mismatch. Read it with a directional wattmeter; check antenna resonance with an antenna analyzer. Solid-state rigs cut power at high SWR to protect their transistors.</p><p>A dummy load, a 50-ohm non-inductive resistor on a heat sink, lets you test without transmitting over the air.</p>"], "t7ccoax": ["Coax", "<p>Lost power in feed line turns into heat. Moisture is what kills coax, often after UV cracks the jacket. Foam dielectric has less loss per foot than solid.</p>"], "t7d": ["Meters and soldering", "<p>Voltmeter: voltage, connected in parallel. Ammeter: current, connected in series. Ohmmeter: applies a small current and reads the voltage; use it only on unpowered circuits, and never measure voltage on the resistance setting.</p><p>On a large discharged capacitor, an ohmmeter's reading climbs as the capacitor charges. Never use acid-core solder; a cold joint looks rough or lumpy.</p>"]}, "CON": {"T7A02": "t7a", "T7A11": "t7a", "T7A05": "t7a", "T7A03": "t7a", "T7A08": "t7a", "T7A01": "t7a", "T7A04": "t7a", "T7A10": "t7aadd", "T7A09": "t7aadd", "T7A06": "t7aadd", "T7A07": "t7aadd", "T7B03": "t7b", "T7B06": "t7b", "T7B02": "t7b", "T7B05": "t7b", "T7B07": "t7b", "T7B09": "t7b", "T7B08": "t7b", "T7B01": "t7baudio", "T7B10": "t7baudio", "T7B11": "t7baudio", "T7B04": "t7baudio", "T7C04": "t7c", "T7C06": "t7c", "T7C08": "t7c", "T7C02": "t7c", "T7C05": "t7c", "T7C01": "t7c", "T7C03": "t7c", "T7C07": "t7ccoax", "T7C09": "t7ccoax", "T7C10": "t7ccoax", "T7C11": "t7ccoax", "T7D01": "t7d", "T7D02": "t7d", "T7D03": "t7d", "T7D04": "t7d", "T7D07": "t7d", "T7D05": "t7d", "T7D11": "t7d", "T7D06": "t7d", "T7D10": "t7d", "T7D08": "t7d", "T7D09": "t7d"}}, {"id": "t8", "code": "T8", "name": "Signals and emissions", "exam": 4, "G": {"T8A": "FM, SSB and bandwidth", "T8B": "Satellites", "T8C": "Direction finding, contests, internet linking", "T8D": "Digital modes"}, "Q": [["T8A01", "Which of the following is a form of amplitude modulation?", ["Spread spectrum", "Packet radio", "Single sideband", "Phase shift keying (PSK)"], 2, "SSB is AM with the carrier and one sideband removed, so it's still amplitude modulation."], ["T8A02", "What type of modulation is commonly used for VHF packet radio transmissions?", ["FM or PM", "SSB", "AM", "PSK"], 0, "VHF packet radio usually rides on FM or PM."], ["T8A03", "Which type of voice mode is often used for long-distance (weak signal) contacts on the VHF and UHF bands?", ["FM", "DRM", "SSB", "PM"], 2, "SSB is the voice mode for weak-signal, long-distance work on VHF and UHF."], ["T8A04", "Which type of modulation is commonly used for VHF and UHF voice repeaters?", ["AM", "SSB", "PSK", "FM or PM"], 3, "Voice repeaters use FM or PM."], ["T8A05", "Which of the following signal types has the narrowest bandwidth?", ["FM voice", "SSB voice", "CW", "Slow-scan TV"], 2, "CW is the narrowest signal of the four."], ["T8A06", "Which sideband is normally used for 10-meter HF, VHF, and UHF single-sideband communications?", ["Upper sideband", "Lower sideband", "Suppressed sideband", "Inverted sideband"], 0, "Upper sideband for 10 meters, VHF and UHF."], ["T8A07", "What is one characteristic of single sideband (SSB) compared to FM?", ["SSB signals are easier to tune in correctly", "SSB signals are less susceptible to interference", "SSB signals have narrower bandwidth", "SSB signals are less susceptible to high SWR"], 2, "SSB takes much less bandwidth than FM."], ["T8A08", "What is the approximate bandwidth of a typical single sideband (SSB) voice signal?", ["1 kHz", "3 kHz", "6 kHz", "15 kHz"], 1, "SSB voice is about 3 kHz wide."], ["T8A09", "What is the approximate bandwidth of an FM voice signal on VHF repeaters?", ["Less than 500 Hz", "About 150 kHz", "Between 10 and 15 kHz", "Between 50 and 125 kHz"], 2, "FM voice on VHF repeaters runs 10 to 15 kHz wide."], ["T8A10", "What is the approximate bandwidth of AM fast-scan TV transmissions?", ["More than 10 MHz", "About 6 MHz", "About 3 MHz", "About 1 MHz"], 1, "Fast-scan TV is about 6 MHz wide, like broadcast TV."], ["T8A11", "What is the approximate bandwidth required to transmit a CW signal?", ["2.4 kHz", "150 Hz", "1000 Hz", "15 kHz"], 1, "CW needs about 150 Hz."], ["T8A12", "Which of the following is a disadvantage of FM compared with single sideband?", ["Voice quality is poorer", "Only one signal can be received at a time", "FM signals are harder to tune", "FM signals are more susceptible to high SWR"], 1, "FM's capture effect means you hear only the strongest signal. With SSB you can hear several at once."], ["T8B01", "What telemetry information is typically transmitted by satellite beacons?", ["The signal strength of received signals", "Time of day accurate to plus or minus 1/10 second", "Health and status of the satellite", "All these choices are correct"], 2, "Satellite beacons send telemetry on the health and status of the satellite."], ["T8B02", "What is the impact of using excessive effective radiated power on a satellite uplink?", ["Possibility of commanding the satellite to an improper mode", "Blocking access by other users", "Overloading the satellite batteries", "Possibility of rebooting the satellite control computer"], 1, "Too much uplink power hogs the satellite's transponder and blocks other users."], ["T8B03", "Which of the following are provided by satellite tracking programs?", ["Maps showing the real-time position of the satellite track over Earth", "The time, azimuth, and elevation of the start, maximum altitude, and end of a pass", "The apparent frequency of the satellite transmission, including effects of Doppler shift", "All these choices are correct"], 3, "Tracking programs give maps, pass times with azimuth and elevation, and Doppler-corrected frequencies. All of these."], ["T8B04", "What mode of transmission is commonly used by amateur radio satellites?", ["SSB", "FM", "CW/data", "All these choices are correct"], 3, "Satellites use SSB, FM and CW/data. All of these."], ["T8B05", "What is a satellite beacon?", ["The primary transmit antenna on the satellite", "An indicator light that shows where to point your antenna", "A reflective surface on the satellite", "A transmission from a satellite that contains status information"], 3, "A satellite beacon is a transmission carrying status information."], ["T8B06", "Which of the following are inputs to a satellite tracking program?", ["The satellite transmitted power", "The Keplerian elements", "The last observed time of zero Doppler shift", "All these choices are correct"], 1, "Tracking programs need the Keplerian elements, which describe the orbit."], ["T8B07", "What is Doppler shift in reference to satellite communications?", ["A change in the satellite orbit", "A mode where the satellite receives signals on one band and transmits on another", "An observed change in signal frequency caused by relative motion between the satellite and Earth station", "A special digital communications mode for some satellites"], 2, "Doppler shift is the frequency change from relative motion between the satellite and you."], ["T8B08", "What does it mean if a satellite is operating in U/V mode?", ["The satellite uplink is in the 15-meter band and the downlink is in the 10-meter band", "The satellite uplink is in the 70-centimeter band and the downlink is in the 2-meter band", "The satellite operates using ultraviolet frequencies", "The satellite frequencies are usually variable"], 1, "U/V: uplink on UHF (70 cm), downlink on VHF (2 m). First letter is the uplink."], ["T8B09", "What causes spin fading of satellite signals?", ["Circular polarized noise interference radiated from the sun", "Rotation of the satellite and its antennas", "Doppler shift of the received signal", "Interfering signals within the satellite uplink band"], 1, "Spin fading comes from the satellite and its antennas rotating."], ["T8B10", "What does the term LEO mean in reference to communication satellites?", ["Low Energy Orbit, which conserves battery power", "Low Elevation Orbit, which appears close to the horizon from the earth station", "Low Equilibrium Orbit, which has a slightly unstable period", "Low Earth Orbit, which has a period of around 100 minutes"], 3, "LEO is Low Earth Orbit, about 100 minutes per orbit."], ["T8B11", "Who is permitted to receive telemetry from an amateur radio satellite?", ["Anyone", "Only the satellite control operator", "Only the control operator or a licensed radio amateur who has received the encryption key from the control operator", "Only a licensed radio amateur who has received the encryption key from AMSAT"], 0, "Anyone may receive satellite telemetry."], ["T8B12", "Which of the following is a way to determine whether your satellite uplink power into a linear transponder satellite is neither too low nor too high?", ["Check your signal strength report in the telemetry data", "Listen for distortion on your downlink signal", "Your signal strength on the downlink should be about the same as the beacon", "Compare your signal to others on the downlink using an internet SDR receiver"], 2, "Set your uplink power so your downlink signal is about as strong as the satellite's beacon."], ["T8C01", "Which of the following methods is used to locate sources of noise interference or jamming?", ["Echolocation", "Doppler radar", "Radio direction finding", "Phase locking"], 2, "Radio direction finding locates noise sources and jammers."], ["T8C02", "Which of these items would be useful for a hidden transmitter hunt?", ["Calibrated SWR meter", "A directional antenna", "A directional wattmeter", "All these choices are correct"], 1, "A fox hunt needs a directional antenna."], ["T8C03", "What operating activity involves contacting as many stations as possible during a specified period?", ["Simulated emergency exercises", "Net operations", "Hidden transmitter hunts", "Contesting"], 3, "Contesting means working as many stations as possible in a set period."], ["T8C04", "Which of the following is good practice when contacting another station in a contest?", ["Signing only the last two letters of your call if there are many other stations calling", "Contacting the station twice to be sure that you are in his log", "Sending only the minimum information needed for proper identification and the contest exchange", "Adding “Please copy” before your exchange"], 2, "In a contest, send only your call and the exchange. Keep it short."], ["T8C05", "What is a grid locator?", ["A letter-number designator assigned to a geographic location", "A letter-number designator assigned to an azimuth and elevation", "An instrument for locating faults in power amplifiers", "An instrument for radio direction finding"], 0, "A grid locator is a letter-number code for a location, like CM87 for much of the Bay Area."], ["T8C06", "How is over the air access to Internet Radio Linking Project (IRLP) nodes accomplished?", ["By obtaining a password that is sent via voice to the node", "By using Dual-Tone Multi-Frequency (DTMF) signals", "By entering the proper internet password", "By using Continuous Tone-Coded Squelch System (CTCSS) tone codes"], 1, "You reach an IRLP node over the air with DTMF tones from your keypad."], ["T8C07", "What is Voice Over Internet Protocol (VoIP)?", ["A set of rules specifying how to identify your station when linked over the internet to another station", "A technique employed to “spot” DX stations via the internet", "A technique for measuring the modulation quality of a transmitter using remote sites monitored via the internet", "A method of delivering voice communications over the internet using digital techniques"], 3, "VoIP delivers voice over the internet digitally."], ["T8C08", "What is the Internet Radio Linking Project (IRLP)?", ["A technique to connect amateur radio systems, such as repeaters, via the internet", "A system for providing access to websites via amateur radio", "A system for informing amateurs in real time of the frequency of active DX stations", "A technique for measuring signal strength of an amateur transmitter via the internet"], 0, "IRLP links repeaters and other radio systems over the internet."], ["T8C09", "Which of the following protocols enables an amateur station to transmit through a repeater without using a radio to initiate the transmission?", ["IRLP", "D-STAR", "DMR", "EchoLink"], 3, "EchoLink lets you reach a repeater from a computer or phone, no radio needed."], ["T8C10", "What is required before using the EchoLink system?", ["Complete the required EchoLink training", "Purchase a license to use the EchoLink software", "Register your call sign and provide proof of license", "At least a General Class license"], 2, "EchoLink requires registering your call sign and proving you're licensed."], ["T8C11", "What is an amateur radio station that connects other amateur stations to the internet?", ["A gateway", "A repeater", "A digipeater", "A beacon"], 0, "A gateway connects amateur stations to the internet."], ["T8D01", "Which of the following is a digital communications mode?", ["Packet radio", "IEEE 802.11", "FT8", "All these choices are correct"], 3, "Packet, 802.11 Wi-Fi and FT8 are all digital modes."], ["T8D02", "What is FT8?", ["A wideband FM voice mode", "A digital mode capable of low signal-to-noise operation", "An eight-channel multiplex mode for FM repeaters", "A digital slow-scan TV mode with forward error correction and automatic color compensation"], 1, "FT8 is a digital mode that works at very low signal-to-noise ratios."], ["T8D03", "What kind of data can be transmitted by APRS?", ["GPS position data", "Text messages", "Weather data", "All these choices are correct"], 3, "APRS carries GPS positions, text messages and weather data."], ["T8D04", "What is meant by the term \"NTSC?\"", ["A digital transmission standard for encrypting data", "A special mode for satellite uplink", "An analog fast-scan color TV signal", "A frame compression scheme for TV signals"], 2, "NTSC is the analog fast-scan color TV standard."], ["T8D05", "Which of the following is an application of APRS?", ["Providing real-time tactical digital communications in conjunction with a map showing the locations of stations", "Automatically showing the number of packets transmitted via PACTOR during a specific time interval", "Providing voice over internet connection between repeaters", "Providing information on the number of stations signed into a repeater"], 0, "APRS gives real-time tactical messaging tied to a map of station positions."], ["T8D06", "What does the abbreviation \"PSK\" mean?", ["Pulse Shift Keying", "Phase Shift Keying", "Packet Sampled Keying", "Power Sampled Keying"], 1, "PSK stands for Phase Shift Keying."], ["T8D07", "Which of the following describes DMR?", ["A technique for time-multiplexing two digital voice signals on a single 12.5 kHz repeater channel", "An automatic position tracking mode for FM mobiles communicating through repeaters", "An automatic computer logging technique for hands-off logging when communicating while operating a vehicle", "A digital technique for transmitting on two repeater inputs simultaneously for automatic error correction"], 0, "DMR fits two digital voice signals in one 12.5 kHz repeater channel by taking turns in time slots."], ["T8D08", "Which of the following is included in packet radio transmissions?", ["A checksum that permits error detection", "A header that contains the call sign of the station to which the information is being sent", "Automatic repeat request in case of error", "All these choices are correct"], 3, "Packet includes a checksum, a header with the destination call sign and automatic repeat requests. All of these."], ["T8D09", "What is CW?", ["A type of electromagnetic propagation", "A digital mode used primarily on 2-meter FM", "Error correction for digital transmission using code words", "Another name for a Morse code transmission"], 3, "CW is Morse code."], ["T8D10", "Which of the following operating activities is supported by digital mode software in the WSJT-X software suite?", ["Earth-Moon-Earth", "Weak signal propagation beacons", "Meteor scatter", "All these choices are correct"], 3, "WSJT-X supports moonbounce (EME), propagation beacons and meteor scatter. All of these."], ["T8D11", "What is the role of ARQ in a transmission system?", ["A special transmission format limited to video signals", "A system used to encrypt command signals to an amateur radio satellite", "An error correction method in which the receiving station detects errors and sends a request for retransmission", "A method of compressing data using autonomous reiterative Q codes prior to final encoding"], 2, "ARQ: the receiver spots an error and asks for a resend."], ["T8D12", "Which of the following best describes an amateur radio mesh network?", ["An amateur-radio data network using commercial Wi-Fi equipment with modified firmware", "A wide-bandwidth digital voice mode employing DMR protocols", "An amateur-radio satellite communications network using modified commercial satellite TV hardware", "An internet linking protocol allowing communication through repeaters around the world"], 0, "A ham mesh network runs on commercial Wi-Fi gear with modified firmware."]], "P": {"t8modes": ["FM versus SSB", "<p>SSB is a form of AM. It's the weak-signal voice mode on VHF and UHF, using upper sideband on 10 meters and up. FM or PM is the repeater mode and carries VHF packet.</p><p>SSB is narrower than FM. FM's drawback: the strongest signal captures the receiver, so you hear only one signal at a time.</p>"], "t8bw": ["Bandwidths", "<p>CW about 150 Hz, the narrowest of these. SSB voice about 3 kHz. FM voice 10 to 15 kHz. Fast-scan TV about 6 MHz. Slow-scan TV (still pictures) fits in a voice channel, about 3 kHz.</p>"], "t8sat": ["Satellites", "<p>A beacon transmits telemetry on the satellite's health and status, and anyone may receive it. Set uplink power so your downlink matches the beacon; too much blocks other users. Satellites use SSB, FM and CW/data.</p><p>Uplink is your signal up to the satellite; downlink is the satellite's signal down to you. U/V mode: uplink 70 cm, downlink 2 m. LEO means low Earth orbit, about 100 minutes around. Doppler shift is frequency change from relative motion. Spin fading is from the satellite rotating.</p><p>Tracking programs take Keplerian elements and give maps, pass times and Doppler-corrected frequencies.</p>"], "t8act": ["Activities", "<p>Radio direction finding locates noise and jammers; a fox hunt needs a directional antenna. Contesting means working as many stations as possible in a set time, sending only the call and exchange. A grid locator is a letter-number code for a location.</p>"], "t8net": ["Internet linking", "<p>VoIP carries voice over the internet. IRLP links repeaters over the internet, and you reach a node over the air with DTMF tones. EchoLink lets you reach a repeater without a radio, after registering your call and proving your license. A gateway connects amateur stations to the internet.</p>"], "t8dig": ["Digital modes", "<p>Packet, Wi-Fi and FT8 are digital. FT8 works at very low signal-to-noise. PSK is phase shift keying. CW is Morse code. NTSC is analog color TV.</p><p>APRS sends GPS position, messages and weather, shown on a map. DMR puts two voice signals in one 12.5 kHz channel by time slot. Packet carries a checksum, a header with the destination call, and ARQ, which asks for a resend on error. WSJT-X supports EME, beacons and meteor scatter. Mesh networks run on modified commercial Wi-Fi gear.</p>"]}, "CON": {"T8A01": "t8modes", "T8A03": "t8modes", "T8A06": "t8modes", "T8A04": "t8modes", "T8A02": "t8modes", "T8A07": "t8modes", "T8A12": "t8modes", "T8A05": "t8bw", "T8A11": "t8bw", "T8A08": "t8bw", "T8A09": "t8bw", "T8A10": "t8bw", "T8B05": "t8sat", "T8B01": "t8sat", "T8B11": "t8sat", "T8B12": "t8sat", "T8B02": "t8sat", "T8B04": "t8sat", "T8B08": "t8sat", "T8B10": "t8sat", "T8B07": "t8sat", "T8B09": "t8sat", "T8B06": "t8sat", "T8B03": "t8sat", "T8C01": "t8act", "T8C02": "t8act", "T8C03": "t8act", "T8C04": "t8act", "T8C05": "t8act", "T8C07": "t8net", "T8C08": "t8net", "T8C06": "t8net", "T8C09": "t8net", "T8C10": "t8net", "T8C11": "t8net", "T8D01": "t8dig", "T8D02": "t8dig", "T8D06": "t8dig", "T8D09": "t8dig", "T8D04": "t8dig", "T8D03": "t8dig", "T8D05": "t8dig", "T8D07": "t8dig", "T8D08": "t8dig", "T8D11": "t8dig", "T8D10": "t8dig", "T8D12": "t8dig"}}, {"id": "t9", "code": "T9", "name": "Antennas and feed lines", "exam": 2, "G": {"T9A": "Antennas", "T9B": "Feed lines, connectors and SWR"}, "Q": [["T9A01", "What is a beam antenna?", ["An antenna built from square aluminum beams", "An omnidirectional antenna invented by Clarence Beam", "An antenna that concentrates signals in one direction", "An antenna that focuses the signal into two intense rays"], 2, "A beam antenna concentrates signal in one direction."], ["T9A02", "Which of the following describes a type of antenna loading?", ["Electrically lengthening by inserting inductors in radiating elements", "Inserting a resistor in the radiating portion of the antenna to make it resonant", "Installing a spring in the base of a mobile vertical antenna to make it more flexible", "Strengthening the radiating elements of a beam antenna to better resist wind damage"], 0, "Loading means electrically lengthening an antenna, for example by inserting coils in its elements."], ["T9A03", "How is the polarization of an antenna described?", ["By the shape of the driven element", "By the orientation of the electric field", "By the orientation of the magnetic field", "By the direction of radiation"], 1, "Antenna polarization is the orientation of the electric field."], ["T9A04", "What is a disadvantage of a handheld radio transceiver’s short flexible antenna compared to a full-sized quarter-wave antenna?", ["It has low efficiency", "It transmits only circularly polarized signals", "It is more susceptible to receiver desensitization", "It only works on analog signals, not digital ones"], 0, "A short flexible handheld antenna is inefficient compared with a full quarter-wave."], ["T9A05", "Which of the following increases the resonant frequency of a dipole antenna?", ["Lengthening it", "Inserting coils in series with radiating wires", "Shortening it", "Adding capacitive loading to the ends of the radiating wires"], 2, "Shortening a dipole raises its resonant frequency."], ["T9A06", "Which of the following types of antennas offers the greatest gain?", ["5/8 wave vertical", "Isotropic", "J pole", "Yagi"], 3, "Of these, the Yagi has the most gain."], ["T9A07", "What is a potential drawback of using a handheld VHF transceiver inside a vehicle that lacks an externally mounted antenna?", ["Signal strength is reduced due to the shielding effect of the vehicle", "The bandwidth of the antenna will decrease, increasing SWR", "The SWR might decrease, decreasing the signal strength", "The handheld will overheat due to reflected power in the vehicle"], 0, "A car's metal body shields the signal, so a handheld used inside is weaker without an outside antenna."], ["T9A08", "Why is a 19-inch-long vertical antenna often used on 2 meters?", ["It has high gain", "It is a resonant half-wave", "It is a resonant quarter-wave", "It has low RF radiation exposure"], 2, "A quarter wave on 2 meters is about 19 inches."], ["T9A09", "What is an advantage of a 5/8-wavelength whip antenna for VHF or UHF mobile service compared to a 1/4-wave antenna?", ["It has more gain", "It radiates at a higher angle", "It has lower SWR", "It has lower impedance"], 0, "A 5/8-wave whip has more gain than a 1/4-wave."], ["T9A10", "In which direction does a half-wave dipole antenna radiate the strongest signal?", ["Equally in all directions", "Off the ends of the antenna", "In the direction of the feed line", "Broadside to the antenna"], 3, "A dipole radiates strongest broadside, at right angles to the wire."], ["T9A11", "What is antenna gain?", ["The additional power that is added to the transmitter power", "The additional power that is required in the antenna when transmitting on a higher frequency", "The increase in signal strength in a specified direction compared to a reference antenna", "The increase in impedance on receive or transmit compared to a reference antenna"], 2, "Gain is the increase in signal strength in a given direction compared with a reference antenna. It isn't extra power."], ["T9B01", "Which of the following connectors should be carefully taped for weather protection when used outdoors?", ["PL259", "BNC", "Type N", "All these choices are correct"], 3, "Tape every outdoor connector for weather protection, whatever the type."], ["T9B02", "What is the most common impedance of coaxial cables used in amateur radio?", ["8 ohms", "50 ohms", "600 ohms", "12 ohms"], 1, "Most amateur coax is 50 ohms."], ["T9B03", "Why is coaxial cable the most common feed line for amateur radio antenna systems?", ["It is easy to use and requires few special installation considerations", "It has less loss than any other type of feed line", "It can handle more power than any other type of feed line", "It is less expensive than any other type of feed line"], 0, "Coax is popular because it's easy to use and needs few special installation steps."], ["T9B04", "What is the major function of an antenna tuner (antenna coupler)?", ["It matches the antenna system impedance to the transceiver's output impedance", "It helps a receiver automatically tune in weak stations", "It allows an antenna to be used on both transmit and receive", "It automatically selects the proper antenna for the frequency band being used"], 0, "An antenna tuner matches the antenna system's impedance to the transceiver's output."], ["T9B05", "What happens as the frequency of a signal in coaxial cable is increased?", ["The characteristic impedance decreases", "The loss decreases", "The characteristic impedance increases", "The loss increases"], 3, "Coax loss rises with frequency."], ["T9B06", "Which of the following connector types is most suitable as an RF connector for frequencies above 400 MHz?", ["PL-259", "Type N", "RS-213", "DB-25"], 1, "Type N connectors suit frequencies above 400 MHz."], ["T9B07", "Which of the following is true of PL-259 type coax connectors?", ["They are preferred for microwave operation", "They are watertight", "They are commonly used at HF and VHF frequencies", "They are a bayonet-type connector"], 2, "PL-259 connectors are common on HF and VHF."], ["T9B08", "Which of the following is a source of loss in coaxial feed line?", ["Water intrusion into coaxial connectors", "High SWR", "Multiple connectors in the line", "All these choices are correct"], 3, "Water in connectors, high SWR and extra connectors all add loss."], ["T9B09", "What can cause erratic changes in SWR?", ["Local thunderstorm", "Loose connection in the antenna or feed line", "Over-modulation", "Overload from a strong local station"], 1, "A loose connection in the antenna or feed line makes SWR jump around."], ["T9B10", "What is the electrical difference between RG-58 and RG-213 coaxial cable?", ["There is no significant difference between the two types", "RG-58 cable has two shields", "RG-213 cable has less loss at a given frequency", "RG-58 cable can handle higher power levels"], 2, "RG-213 is thicker than RG-58 and has less loss at a given frequency."], ["T9B11", "Which of the following types of feed line has the lowest loss?", ["50-ohm flexible coax", "Multi-conductor unbalanced cable", "Air-insulated hardline", "75-ohm flexible coax"], 2, "Air-insulated hardline has the lowest loss."], ["T9B12", "What is standing wave ratio (SWR)?", ["A measure of how well a load is matched to a transmission line", "The ratio of amplifier power output to input", "The transmitter efficiency ratio", "An indication of the quality of your station’s ground connection"], 0, "SWR measures how well the load is matched to the feed line. 1:1 is a perfect match."]], "P": {"types": ["Antenna types and gain", "<p>A beam antenna concentrates signal in one direction; a Yagi is a beam and has more gain than a vertical, J-pole or isotropic antenna. Gain is stronger signal in one direction compared with a reference antenna, not added power.</p><p>A dipole radiates strongest broadside to the wire. A 5/8-wave whip has more gain than a 1/4-wave.</p>"], "length": ["Length and resonance", "<p>Shorter antennas resonate higher in frequency. Wavelength in meters is 300 divided by frequency in MHz. A quarter wave on 2 meters is about 19 inches. Loading coils make an antenna act electrically longer than it is.</p>"], "handheld": ["Polarization and handhelds", "<p>Polarization is the orientation of the electric field. A handheld's short flexible antenna is inefficient, and inside a car the metal body shields its signal.</p>"], "coax": ["Coax and loss", "<p>Most amateur coax is 50 ohms. Coax is the usual feed line because it's easy to install. Loss rises with frequency. RG-213 has less loss than RG-58, and air-insulated hardline has the least of all.</p><p>Water in connectors, high SWR and extra connectors all add loss.</p>"], "conn": ["Connectors", "<p>PL-259s are common on HF and VHF. Type N suits frequencies above 400 MHz. Tape any outdoor connector against weather.</p>"], "swr": ["SWR and tuners", "<p>SWR measures how well the antenna load is matched to the feed line; 1:1 is a perfect match. Erratic SWR points to a loose connection. An antenna tuner matches the antenna system's impedance to the transceiver's output.</p>"]}, "CON": {"T9A01": "types", "T9A06": "types", "T9A11": "types", "T9A10": "types", "T9A09": "types", "T9A05": "length", "T9A08": "length", "T9A02": "length", "T9A03": "handheld", "T9A04": "handheld", "T9A07": "handheld", "T9B02": "coax", "T9B03": "coax", "T9B05": "coax", "T9B10": "coax", "T9B11": "coax", "T9B08": "coax", "T9B07": "conn", "T9B06": "conn", "T9B01": "conn", "T9B12": "swr", "T9B09": "swr", "T9B04": "swr"}}, {"id": "t0", "code": "T0", "name": "Safety", "exam": 3, "G": {"T0A": "Electrical safety, fuses and batteries", "T0B": "Towers, antennas and lightning grounding", "T0C": "RF exposure"}, "Q": [["T0A01", "Which of the following is a safety hazard of a 12-volt storage battery that lacks internal protection circuitry?", ["Touching both terminals with your hands can cause electrical shock", "Shorting the terminals can cause burns, fire, or an explosion", "RF emissions from a nearby transmitter can cause the electrolyte to emit poison gas", "All these choices are correct"], 1, "Shorting an unprotected 12-volt battery's terminals can cause burns, fire or an explosion. Twelve volts won't shock you through dry hands."], ["T0A02", "What health hazard is posed by electrical current flowing through the body?", ["It may cause injury by heating body tissue", "It may disrupt the electrical functions of cells", "It may cause involuntary muscle contractions", "All these choices are correct"], 3, "Current through the body can heat tissue, disrupt cell function and cause involuntary muscle contractions."], ["T0A03", "In the United States, what circuit does black wire insulation indicate in a three-wire 120 V AC cable?", ["Neutral", "Hot", "Equipment ground", "Negative"], 1, "In US AC wiring, black is hot. White is neutral, green or bare is ground."], ["T0A04", "What is the purpose of a fuse in an electrical circuit?", ["To prevent power supply ripple from damaging a component", "To remove power in case of an overload", "To limit current and prevent shocks", "All these choices are correct"], 1, "A fuse removes power when there's an overload."], ["T0A05", "Why should a 5-ampere fuse never be replaced with a 20-ampere fuse?", ["The larger fuse would be likely to blow because it is rated for higher current", "The power supply ripple would greatly increase", "Excessive current could cause a fire", "Voltage drop in the higher current fuse could result in excessively low voltage to the device"], 2, "A 20-amp fuse in a 5-amp circuit lets enough current flow to start a fire before it blows."], ["T0A06", "What is a good way to guard against electrical shock at your station?", ["Use three-wire cords and plugs for all AC powered equipment", "Connect all AC powered station equipment to a common safety ground", "Ensure all capacitors used for high-voltage DC are fully discharged before working inside equipment", "All these choices are correct"], 3, "Three-wire cords, a common safety ground and discharged capacitors all guard against shock."], ["T0A07", "Where should a lightning arrester be installed in a coaxial feed line?", ["At the output connector of a transceiver", "At the antenna feed point", "At the AC power service panel", "On a grounded panel near where feed lines enter the building"], 3, "Put the lightning arrester on a grounded panel near where the feed lines enter the building."], ["T0A08", "Where should a fuse or circuit breaker be installed in a 120V AC power circuit?", ["In series with the hot conductor only", "In series with the hot and neutral conductors", "In parallel with the hot conductor only", "In parallel with the hot and neutral conductors"], 0, "A fuse or breaker goes in series with the hot conductor only."], ["T0A09", "What should be done to all external ground rods or earth connections?", ["Waterproof them with silicone caulk or electrical tape", "Keep them as far apart as possible", "Bond them together with heavy wire or conductive strap", "Tune them for resonance on the lowest frequency of operation"], 2, "Bond all ground rods together with heavy wire or strap."], ["T0A10", "What hazard exists when rapidly charging or discharging an unprotected battery?", ["Overheating or out-gassing", "Excess output ripple", "Electric shock", "Overvoltage"], 0, "Charging or discharging an unprotected battery too fast can overheat it or make it vent gas."], ["T0A11", "What hazard exists in a power supply immediately after turning it off?", ["Circulating currents in the dc filter", "Leakage flux in the power transformer", "Voltage transients from kickback diodes", "Charge stored in filter capacitors"], 3, "Filter capacitors can hold a dangerous charge after the supply is switched off."], ["T0A12", "Which of the following precautions should be taken when measuring high voltages with a voltmeter?", ["Ensure that the voltmeter has very low impedance", "Ensure that the voltmeter and its leads are rated for use at the voltages being measured", "Ensure that the circuit is grounded through the voltmeter", "Ensure that the voltmeter is set to the correct frequency"], 1, "Use a voltmeter and leads rated for the voltage you're measuring."], ["T0B01", "Which of the following is good practice when installing ground wires on a tower for lightning protection?", ["Put a drip loop in the ground connection to prevent water damage to the ground system", "Make sure all ground wire bends are right angles", "Ensure that connections are short and direct", "All these choices are correct"], 2, "Lightning ground wires should be short and direct."], ["T0B02", "What is required when climbing an antenna tower?", ["Have sufficient training on safe tower climbing techniques", "Use appropriate tie-off to the tower at all times", "Always wear an approved climbing harness", "All these choices are correct"], 3, "Tower climbing requires training, an approved harness and constant tie-off."], ["T0B03", "Under what circumstances is it safe to climb a tower without a helper or observer?", ["When no electrical work is being performed", "When no mechanical work is being performed", "When the work being done is not more than 20 feet above the ground", "Never"], 3, "Never climb a tower without a helper or observer."], ["T0B04", "Which of the following is an important safety precaution to observe when putting up an antenna tower?", ["Wear a ground strap connected to your wrist at all times", "Insulate the base of the tower to avoid lightning strikes", "Look for and stay clear of any overhead electrical wires", "All these choices are correct"], 2, "Watch for overhead power lines and stay clear of them."], ["T0B05", "What is the purpose of a safety wire through a turnbuckle used to tension guy lines?", ["Secure the guy line if the turnbuckle breaks", "Prevent loosening of the turnbuckle from vibration", "Provide a ground path for lightning strikes", "Provide an ability to measure for proper tensioning"], 1, "A safety wire through a turnbuckle keeps vibration from loosening it."], ["T0B06", "What is the minimum safe distance from a power line to allow when installing an antenna?", ["Add the height of the antenna to the height of the power line and multiply by a factor of 1.5", "The height of the power line above ground", "1/2 wavelength at the operating frequency", "Enough so that if the antenna falls, no part of it can come within 10 feet of the power wires"], 3, "Keep an antenna far enough away that if it falls, no part comes within 10 feet of power lines."], ["T0B07", "Which of the following is an important safety rule to remember when using a crank-up tower?", ["This type of tower must never be painted", "This type of tower must never be grounded", "This type of tower must not be climbed unless it is retracted, or mechanical safety locking devices have been installed", "All these choices are correct"], 2, "Never climb a crank-up tower unless it's retracted or mechanical safety locks are installed."], ["T0B08", "Which is a proper grounding method for a tower?", ["A single four-foot ground rod, driven into the ground no more than 12 inches from the base", "A ferrite-core RF choke connected between the tower and ground", "A connection between the tower base and a cold-water pipe", "Separate eight-foot ground rods for each tower leg, bonded to the tower and each other"], 3, "Ground a tower with a separate 8-foot rod at each leg, bonded to the tower and to each other."], ["T0B09", "Why should you avoid attaching an antenna to a utility pole?", ["The antenna will not work properly because of induced voltages", "The antenna may unbalance the power transformer, causing power fluctuations", "The antenna could contact high-voltage power lines", "All these choices are correct"], 2, "An antenna on a utility pole could touch high-voltage lines."], ["T0B10", "Which of the following is true when installing grounding conductors used for lightning protection?", ["Use only non-insulated wire", "Wires must be carefully routed with precise right-angle bends", "Sharp bends must be avoided", "Common grounds must be avoided"], 2, "Avoid sharp bends in lightning ground wires. Lightning resists sudden turns."], ["T0B11", "Which of the following establishes grounding requirements for an amateur radio tower or antenna?", ["FCC Part 97 rules", "Local electrical codes", "FAA tower lighting regulations", "UL recommended practices"], 1, "Local electrical codes set grounding requirements, not the FCC."], ["T0C01", "What type of radiation are radio signals?", ["Gamma radiation", "Ionizing radiation", "Alpha radiation", "Non-ionizing radiation"], 3, "Radio signals are non-ionizing radiation."], ["T0C02", "Which of the following bands has the lowest maximum permissible exposure for RF safety?", ["3.5 MHz", "50 MHz", "440 MHz", "1296 MHz"], 1, "Of these, 50 MHz has the lowest MPE limit. The strictest limits fall between 30 and 300 MHz, where the body absorbs RF most."], ["T0C03", "How does the allowable power density for RF safety change if duty cycle changes from 100 percent to 50 percent?", ["It increases by a factor of 3", "It decreases by 50 percent", "It increases by a factor of 2", "There is no adjustment allowed for lower duty cycle"], 2, "Halve the duty cycle and the allowed power density doubles."], ["T0C04", "What factors affect the RF exposure of people near an amateur station antenna?", ["Frequency and power level of the RF field", "Distance from the antenna to a person", "Radiation pattern of the antenna", "All these choices are correct"], 3, "Exposure depends on frequency and power, distance from the antenna and the antenna's radiation pattern."], ["T0C05", "Why do exposure limits vary with frequency?", ["Lower frequency RF fields have more energy than higher frequency fields", "Lower frequency RF fields do not penetrate the human body", "Higher frequency RF fields are transient in nature", "The human body absorbs more RF energy at some frequencies than at others"], 3, "The body absorbs more RF at some frequencies than at others, so the limits vary with frequency."], ["T0C06", "Which of the following is an acceptable method to determine whether your station complies with FCC RF exposure regulations?", ["By calculation based on FCC OET Bulletin 65", "By calculation based on computer modeling", "By measurement of field strength using calibrated equipment", "All these choices are correct"], 3, "OET Bulletin 65 calculations, computer modeling and calibrated field measurements are all acceptable."], ["T0C07", "What hazard is created by touching an antenna during a transmission?", ["Electrocution", "RF burn to skin", "Exposure to ionizing radiation", "All these choices are correct"], 1, "Touching an antenna while it transmits can cause an RF burn."], ["T0C08", "Which of the following actions can reduce exposure to RF radiation?", ["Relocate antennas", "Relocate the transmitter", "Increase the duty cycle", "All these choices are correct"], 0, "Moving the antenna away from people reduces exposure. Moving the transmitter doesn't."], ["T0C09", "How can you make sure your station stays in compliance with RF safety regulations?", ["By informing the FCC of any changes made in your station", "By re-evaluating the station whenever an item in the transmitter or antenna system is changed", "By making sure your antennas have low SWR", "By using only Underwriter Laboratories approved transmitting equipment"], 1, "Re-evaluate the station whenever you change anything in the transmitter or antenna system."], ["T0C10", "Why is duty cycle one of the factors used to determine safe RF radiation exposure levels?", ["It affects the average exposure to radiation", "It affects the peak exposure to radiation", "It takes into account the antenna feed line loss", "It takes into account the thermal effects of the final amplifier"], 0, "Duty cycle sets the average exposure."], ["T0C11", "What is the definition of duty cycle during the averaging time for RF exposure?", ["The difference between the lowest and highest power output of a transmitter", "The difference between the PEP and the average power output of a transmitter", "The percentage of time that a transmitter is transmitting", "The percentage of time that a transmitter is not transmitting"], 2, "Duty cycle is the percentage of time the transmitter is transmitting."], ["T0C12", "How does RF radiation differ from ionizing radiation (radioactivity)?", ["RF radiation does not have sufficient energy to cause chemical changes in cells and damage DNA", "RF radiation can only be detected with an RF dosimeter", "RF radiation is limited in range to a few feet", "RF radiation is perfectly safe"], 0, "RF lacks the energy to cause chemical changes in cells or damage DNA. Ionizing radiation has it. RF can still heat tissue, so it isn't perfectly safe."], ["T0C13", "Who is responsible for ensuring that no person is exposed to RF energy above the FCC exposure limits?", ["The FCC", "The station licensee", "Anyone who is near an antenna", "The local zoning board"], 1, "The station licensee is responsible for keeping people below the exposure limits."]], "P": {"shock": ["Shock and AC wiring", "<p>Current through the body can heat tissue, disrupt cells and cause involuntary muscle contractions. Guard against shock with three-wire cords, a common safety ground, and by discharging capacitors before working inside equipment.</p><p>In US wiring, black is hot. Power supplies can hold a charge in filter capacitors after they're switched off. Use a voltmeter rated for the voltage.</p>"], "fuses": ["Fuses", "<p>A fuse removes power on overload. It goes in series with the hot conductor only. Never put in a larger fuse than specified: too much current could start a fire.</p>"], "battery": ["Batteries", "<p>Shorting an unprotected battery can cause burns, fire or explosion. Charging or discharging it too fast can overheat it or make it vent gas.</p>"], "lightning": ["Lightning and grounding", "<p>Mount lightning arresters on a grounded panel where feed lines enter the building. Bond all ground rods together. Ground wires should be short and direct, with no sharp bends.</p><p>A tower gets an 8-foot rod at each leg, bonded together. Local electrical codes set the requirements.</p>"], "tower": ["Towers and power lines", "<p>Climbing takes training, a harness and constant tie-off, and never without a helper. Climb a crank-up tower only when retracted or locked. Safety wire keeps turnbuckles from vibrating loose.</p><p>Stay clear of overhead power lines. If an antenna falls, no part should come within 10 feet of them. Never attach an antenna to a utility pole.</p>"], "rf": ["RF exposure basics", "<p>Radio signals are non-ionizing: they lack the energy to damage DNA. The body absorbs some frequencies more than others, so limits vary, and they're strictest from 30 to 300 MHz. Touching a transmitting antenna can cause an RF burn.</p>"], "duty": ["Duty cycle", "<p>Duty cycle is the percentage of time you're transmitting. It sets average exposure, so halving it doubles the allowed power density.</p>"], "comply": ["Staying compliant", "<p>Exposure depends on frequency, power, distance and antenna pattern. Check compliance by OET Bulletin 65 calculation, modeling or measurement. Re-evaluate after any change, and move antennas away from people to cut exposure. The station licensee is responsible.</p>"]}, "CON": {"T0A02": "shock", "T0A06": "shock", "T0A03": "shock", "T0A11": "shock", "T0A12": "shock", "T0A04": "fuses", "T0A08": "fuses", "T0A05": "fuses", "T0A01": "battery", "T0A10": "battery", "T0A07": "lightning", "T0A09": "lightning", "T0B01": "lightning", "T0B10": "lightning", "T0B08": "lightning", "T0B11": "lightning", "T0B02": "tower", "T0B03": "tower", "T0B07": "tower", "T0B05": "tower", "T0B04": "tower", "T0B06": "tower", "T0B09": "tower", "T0C01": "rf", "T0C12": "rf", "T0C05": "rf", "T0C02": "rf", "T0C07": "rf", "T0C11": "duty", "T0C10": "duty", "T0C03": "duty", "T0C04": "comply", "T0C06": "comply", "T0C09": "comply", "T0C08": "comply", "T0C13": "comply"}}, {"id": "math", "code": "Math", "name": "Electronics math", "exam": 0, "G": {"T5B": "Unit conversions and decibels", "T5C": "Calculating power", "T5D": "Ohm's law and circuits", "X": "Math questions elsewhere in the pool"}, "Q": [["T5B01", "How many milliamperes is 1.5 amperes?", ["0.0000015 milliamperes", "0.0015 milliamperes", "1500 milliamperes", "1,500,000 milliamperes"], 2, "Milli means thousandths, so there are 1,000 milliamperes in an ampere. Going to the smaller unit, multiply: 1.5 &times; 1,000 = 1,500 mA."], ["T5B02", "Which is equal to 1,500,000 hertz?", ["1500 kHz", "1500 MHz", "15 GHz", "150 kHz"], 0, "Going to the bigger unit, divide. 1,500,000 Hz &divide; 1,000 = 1,500 kHz. (It's also 1.5 MHz, but that isn't offered.)"], ["T5B03", "Which is equal to one kilovolt?", ["One one-thousandth of a volt", "One hundred volts", "One thousand volts", "One million volts"], 2, "Kilo means thousand, as in kilogram or kilometer."], ["T5B04", "Which is equal to one microvolt?", ["One one-millionth of a volt", "One million volts", "One thousand kilovolts", "One one-thousandth of a volt"], 0, "Micro means one-millionth. One one-thousandth is milli, the trap answer."], ["T5B05", "Which is equal to 500 milliwatts?", ["5 watts", "0.5 watts", "500,000 watts", "500,000,000 watts"], 1, "Going to the bigger unit, divide by 1,000: 500 &divide; 1,000 = 0.5 W."], ["T5B06", "Which is equal to 3000 milliamperes?", ["0.003 amperes", "0.3 amperes", "3,000,000 amperes", "3 amperes"], 3, "Divide by 1,000: 3,000 mA &divide; 1,000 = 3 A."], ["T5B07", "Which is equal to 3.525 MHz?", ["0.003525 kHz", "35.25 kHz", "3525 kHz", "3,525,000 kHz"], 2, "Going to the smaller unit, multiply by 1,000: 3.525 &times; 1,000 = 3,525 kHz. Shortcut: slide the decimal three places right."], ["T5B08", "Which is equal to 1,000,000 picofarads?", ["0.001 microfarads", "1 microfarad", "1000 microfarads", "1,000,000,000 microfarads"], 1, "Pico is a millionth of a millionth; micro is a millionth. So a million picofarads make one microfarad."], ["T5B09", "Which decibel value most closely represents a power increase from 5 watts to 10 watts?", ["2 dB", "3 dB", "5 dB", "10 dB"], 1, "5 W to 10 W doubles the power. Doubling is always about +3 dB, whatever the starting level."], ["T5B10", "Which decibel value most closely represents a power decrease from 12 watts to 3 watts?", ["-1 dB", "-3 dB", "-6 dB", "-9 dB"], 2, "12 W to 6 W is one halving (&minus;3 dB); 6 W to 3 W is another (&minus;3 dB). Two halvings make &minus;6 dB."], ["T5B11", "Which decibel value represents a power increase from 20 watts to 200 watts?", ["10 dB", "12 dB", "18 dB", "28 dB"], 0, "20 W to 200 W is ten times the power, and a tenfold increase is exactly 10 dB."], ["T5B12", "Which is equal to 28400 kHz?", ["28.400 kHz", "2.800 MHz", "284.00 MHz", "28.400 MHz"], 3, "Going to the bigger unit, divide by 1,000: 28,400 &divide; 1,000 = 28.400 MHz. That's in the 10-meter band."], ["T5B13", "Which is equal to 2425 MHz?", ["0.002425 GHz", "24.25 GHz", "2.425 GHz", "242.5 GHz"], 2, "Divide by 1,000: 2,425 &divide; 1,000 = 2.425 GHz."], ["T5C08", "What formula is used to calculate electrical power (P) in a DC circuit?", ["P = I x E", "P = E / I", "P = I&sup2; x E", "P = I / E"], 0, "Power equals current times voltage. In ham notation, E is voltage and I is current."], ["T5C09", "How much power is delivered by a voltage of 13.8 volts DC and a current of 10 amperes?", ["138 watts", "0.7 watts", "23.8 watts", "3.8 watts"], 0, "P = I &times; E = 10 &times; 13.8 = 138 W."], ["T5C10", "How much power is delivered by a voltage of 12 volts DC and a current of 2.5 amperes?", ["4.8 watts", "30 watts", "14.5 watts", "0.208 watts"], 1, "P = I &times; E = 2.5 &times; 12 = 30 W."], ["T5C11", "How much current is required to deliver 120 watts at a voltage of 12 volts DC?", ["0.1 amperes", "10 amperes", "12 amperes", "132 amperes"], 1, "Rearrange P = I &times; E to I = P &divide; E. 120 &divide; 12 = 10 A."], ["T5D01", "What formula is used to calculate current in a circuit?", ["I = E x R", "I = E / R", "I = E&sup2; x R", "I = E&sup2; / R"], 1, "Current equals voltage divided by resistance. More voltage pushes more current; more resistance allows less."], ["T5D02", "What formula is used to calculate voltage in a circuit?", ["E = I x R", "E = I / R", "E = I&sup2; x R", "E = I&sup2; / R"], 0, "Voltage equals current times resistance."], ["T5D03", "What formula is used to calculate resistance in a circuit?", ["R = E x I", "R = E / I", "R = E + I", "R = E - I"], 1, "Resistance equals voltage divided by current."], ["T5D04", "What is the resistance of a circuit in which a current of 3 amperes flows when connected to 90 volts?", ["3 ohms", "30 ohms", "1/30 ohm", "270 ohms"], 1, "R = E &divide; I = 90 &divide; 3 = 30 &Omega;."], ["T5D05", "What is the resistance of a circuit for which the applied voltage is 12 volts and the current flow is 1.5 amperes?", ["18 ohms", "0.125 ohms", "8 ohms", "13.5 ohms"], 2, "R = E &divide; I = 12 &divide; 1.5 = 8 &Omega;."], ["T5D06", "What is the resistance of a circuit that draws 4 amperes from a 12-volt source?", ["3 ohms", "16 ohms", "48 ohms", "8 ohms"], 0, "R = E &divide; I = 12 &divide; 4 = 3 &Omega;."], ["T5D07", "What is the current in a circuit with an applied voltage of 120 volts and a resistance of 80 ohms?", ["9600 amperes", "200 amperes", "0.667 amperes", "1.5 amperes"], 3, "I = E &divide; R = 120 &divide; 80 = 1.5 A. Note 0.667 is the trap: it's 80 &divide; 120, upside down."], ["T5D08", "What is the current through a 100-ohm resistor connected across 200 volts?", ["20,000 amperes", "0.5 amperes", "2 amperes", "100 amperes"], 2, "I = E &divide; R = 200 &divide; 100 = 2 A."], ["T5D09", "What is the current through a 24-ohm resistor connected across 240 volts?", ["2400 amperes", "0.1 amperes", "10 amperes", "5760 amperes"], 2, "I = E &divide; R = 240 &divide; 24 = 10 A."], ["T5D10", "What is the voltage across a 2-ohm resistor if a current of 0.5 amperes flows through it?", ["1 volt", "0.25 volts", "2.5 volts", "1.5 volts"], 0, "E = I &times; R = 0.5 &times; 2 = 1 V."], ["T5D11", "What is the voltage across a 10-ohm resistor if a current of 1 ampere flows through it?", ["1 volt", "10 volts", "11 volts", "9 volts"], 1, "E = I &times; R = 1 &times; 10 = 10 V."], ["T5D12", "What is the voltage across a 10-ohm resistor if a current of 2 amperes flows through it?", ["8 volts", "0.2 volts", "12 volts", "20 volts"], 3, "E = I &times; R = 2 &times; 10 = 20 V."], ["T5D13", "In which type of circuit is the current always the same through all components?", ["Series", "Parallel", "Resonant", "Branch"], 0, "In a series circuit there is only one path, so the same current flows through every component."], ["T5D14", "In which type of circuit is the voltage always the same across all components?", ["Series", "Parallel", "Resonant", "Branch"], 1, "In a parallel circuit every component connects across the same two points, so each sees the same voltage."], ["T3B06", "What is the formula for converting frequency to approximate wavelength in meters?", ["Wavelength in meters equals frequency in hertz multiplied by 300", "Wavelength in meters equals frequency in hertz divided by 300", "Wavelength in meters equals frequency in megahertz divided by 300", "Wavelength in meters equals 300 divided by frequency in megahertz"], 3, "Wavelength = 300 &divide; MHz. Check it: 300 &divide; 146 MHz is about 2 meters, which is why that band is called 2 meters."], ["T9A08", "Why is a 19-inch-long vertical antenna often used on 2 meters?", ["It has high gain", "It is a resonant half-wave", "It is a resonant quarter-wave", "It has low RF radiation exposure"], 2, "A 2-meter wavelength is about 79 inches; a quarter of that is about 20 inches, trimmed slightly in practice to 19. Divide 300 by the frequency in MHz for a full wavelength, then by 4 for a quarter."], ["T4A09", "How can you determine the length of time that equipment can be powered from a battery?", ["Divide the watt-hour rating of the battery by the peak power consumption of the equipment", "Divide the battery ampere-hour rating by the average current draw of the equipment", "Multiply the watts per hour consumed by the equipment by the battery power rating", "Multiply the square of the current rating of the battery by the input resistance of the equipment"], 1, "Hours = ampere-hours &divide; average amps. A 10 Ah battery feeding a radio that averages 2 A lasts about 5 hours. Average, not peak, because you aren't transmitting the whole time."], ["T4A01", "Which of the following is an appropriate power supply rating for a typical 50-watt output mobile FM transceiver?", ["24.0 volts at 4 amperes", "13.8 volts at 4 amperes", "24.0 volts at 12 amperes", "13.8 volts at 12 amperes"], 3, "Mobile radios run on 13.8 V, a car's charging voltage. Then P = I &times; E: 13.8 &times; 4 is only 55 W, too little for 50 W out once inefficiency is counted. 13.8 &times; 12 gives about 165 W, plenty of headroom."], ["T0C03", "How does the allowable power density for RF safety change if duty cycle changes from 100 percent to 50 percent?", ["It increases by a factor of 3", "It decreases by 50 percent", "It increases by a factor of 2", "There is no adjustment allowed for lower duty cycle"], 2, "Transmitting half the time halves average exposure, so the allowable power density doubles."]], "P": {"prefix": ["Metric prefixes", "<p>A prefix in front of a unit tells you how many of the base unit you have. The ones on the exam: pico (p) is a trillionth, micro (&micro;) a millionth, milli (m) a thousandth, kilo (k) a thousand, mega (M) a million and giga (G) a billion.</p><p>Most conversions are one step of 1,000: milli to base unit, base to kilo, kilo to mega, mega to giga. Going to a smaller unit, multiply by 1,000, so the number gets bigger. Going to a bigger unit, divide, so the number gets smaller.</p><p>Case matters: lowercase m is milli, capital M is mega.</p>"], "db": ["Decibels", "<p>A decibel (dB) describes a change in power as a ratio rather than in watts. Positive numbers are increases; negative numbers are decreases.</p><p>Three anchors cover every exam question: double the power is +3 dB, four times is +6 dB, ten times is +10 dB. Halving is &minus;3 dB, a quarter is &minus;6 dB and a tenth is &minus;10 dB.</p><p>The same ratio always gives the same dB, whether you go from 5 to 10 watts or from 50 to 100 watts.</p>"], "power": ["Electrical power", "<p>Power is the rate at which electrical energy is used, measured in watts (W).</p><p>In a DC circuit, P = I &times; E: power equals current (I, in amperes) times voltage (E, in volts). Know any two and you can find the third: I = P &divide; E and E = P &divide; I.</p><p>Ham notation uses E for voltage and I for current. Neither letter stands for anything else on this exam.</p>"], "ohm": ["Ohm's law", "<p>Voltage (E, in volts) pushes current (I, in amperes) through a circuit, and resistance (R, in ohms) opposes it.</p><p>Ohm's law ties them together: E = I &times; R. Rearranged, I = E &divide; R and R = E &divide; I.</p><p>Memory aid: picture a triangle with E on top and I and R side by side below it. Cover the one you want. If the other two sit side by side, multiply; if one sits over the other, divide.</p>"], "circuit": ["Series and parallel", "<p>In a series circuit, components are strung end to end in a single path, so the same current flows through every one of them.</p><p>In a parallel circuit, each component connects across the same two points, so each one sees the same voltage.</p><p>Memory hook: series shares current, parallel shares voltage.</p>"], "wave": ["Wavelength", "<p>A radio wave's wavelength is the distance it travels during one cycle. The higher the frequency, the shorter the wavelength.</p><p>Wavelength in meters = 300 &divide; frequency in megahertz, because radio waves travel at the speed of light, about 300 million meters per second.</p><p>Bands are named by approximate wavelength: 146 MHz works out to about 2 meters. A quarter-wave antenna is a quarter of a wavelength long, about 19 inches on 2 meters.</p>"], "battery": ["Battery run time", "<p>Battery capacity is rated in ampere-hours (Ah): how many amperes it can deliver for how many hours.</p><p>Run time in hours = ampere-hours &divide; average current draw in amperes.</p><p>Use the average current, not the peak. A radio draws far less while receiving than while transmitting, and you spend most of your time listening.</p>"], "supply": ["Powering a mobile radio", "<p>Mobile radios are built for a car's electrical system, about 13.8 volts while the engine runs, so their power supplies are rated at 13.8 volts.</p><p>To size the supply, use P = I &times; E and allow headroom: a radio's input power must be well above its RF output power, because no transmitter is 100 percent efficient.</p>"], "duty": ["Duty cycle and RF exposure", "<p>Duty cycle is the percentage of time a transmitter is actually transmitting.</p><p>FCC RF exposure limits are based on average exposure over a period of time, so a lower duty cycle means lower average exposure.</p><p>Halving the duty cycle halves your average exposure, so twice the power density is allowed for the same level of safety.</p>"]}, "CON": {"T5B01": "prefix", "T5B02": "prefix", "T5B03": "prefix", "T5B04": "prefix", "T5B05": "prefix", "T5B06": "prefix", "T5B07": "prefix", "T5B08": "prefix", "T5B12": "prefix", "T5B13": "prefix", "T5B09": "db", "T5B10": "db", "T5B11": "db", "T5C08": "power", "T5C09": "power", "T5C10": "power", "T5C11": "power", "T5D01": "ohm", "T5D02": "ohm", "T5D03": "ohm", "T5D04": "ohm", "T5D05": "ohm", "T5D06": "ohm", "T5D07": "ohm", "T5D08": "ohm", "T5D09": "ohm", "T5D10": "ohm", "T5D11": "ohm", "T5D12": "ohm", "T5D13": "circuit", "T5D14": "circuit", "T3B06": "wave", "T9A08": "wave", "T4A09": "battery", "T4A01": "supply", "T0C03": "duty"}, "F": {"T5B01": ["mA = A &times; 1,000", ["<b>mA</b> = milliamperes", "<b>A</b> = amperes", "<b>1,000</b> = milliamperes in one ampere"]], "T5B02": ["kHz = Hz &divide; 1,000", ["<b>kHz</b> = kilohertz", "<b>Hz</b> = hertz, cycles per second", "<b>1,000</b> = hertz in one kilohertz"]], "T5B03": ["1 kV = 1,000 V", ["<b>kV</b> = kilovolts", "<b>V</b> = volts", "<b>k</b> (kilo) = thousand"]], "T5B04": ["1 &micro;V = 1 V &divide; 1,000,000", ["<b>&micro;V</b> = microvolts", "<b>V</b> = volts", "<b>&micro;</b> (micro) = one-millionth"]], "T5B05": ["W = mW &divide; 1,000", ["<b>W</b> = watts", "<b>mW</b> = milliwatts", "<b>1,000</b> = milliwatts in one watt"]], "T5B06": ["A = mA &divide; 1,000", ["<b>A</b> = amperes", "<b>mA</b> = milliamperes", "<b>1,000</b> = milliamperes in one ampere"]], "T5B07": ["kHz = MHz &times; 1,000", ["<b>kHz</b> = kilohertz", "<b>MHz</b> = megahertz", "<b>1,000</b> = kilohertz in one megahertz"]], "T5B08": ["&micro;F = pF &divide; 1,000,000", ["<b>&micro;F</b> = microfarads", "<b>pF</b> = picofarads", "<b>1,000,000</b> = picofarads in one microfarad"]], "T5B09": ["dB = 10 &times; log<sub>10</sub>(P<sub>2</sub> &divide; P<sub>1</sub>)", ["<b>dB</b> = change in decibels", "<b>P<sub>1</sub></b> = starting power, in watts", "<b>P<sub>2</sub></b> = ending power, in watts", "<b>log<sub>10</sub></b> = base-10 logarithm; log(2) &asymp; 0.3, so doubling gives 10 &times; 0.3 = 3 dB"]], "T5B10": ["dB = 10 &times; log<sub>10</sub>(P<sub>2</sub> &divide; P<sub>1</sub>)", ["<b>dB</b> = change in decibels", "<b>P<sub>1</sub></b> = starting power, in watts", "<b>P<sub>2</sub></b> = ending power, in watts", "<b>log<sub>10</sub></b> = base-10 logarithm; log(2) &asymp; 0.3, so doubling gives 10 &times; 0.3 = 3 dB"]], "T5B11": ["dB = 10 &times; log<sub>10</sub>(P<sub>2</sub> &divide; P<sub>1</sub>)", ["<b>dB</b> = change in decibels", "<b>P<sub>1</sub></b> = starting power, in watts", "<b>P<sub>2</sub></b> = ending power, in watts", "<b>log<sub>10</sub></b> = base-10 logarithm; log(2) &asymp; 0.3, so doubling gives 10 &times; 0.3 = 3 dB"]], "T5B12": ["MHz = kHz &divide; 1,000", ["<b>MHz</b> = megahertz", "<b>kHz</b> = kilohertz", "<b>1,000</b> = kilohertz in one megahertz"]], "T5B13": ["GHz = MHz &divide; 1,000", ["<b>GHz</b> = gigahertz", "<b>MHz</b> = megahertz", "<b>1,000</b> = megahertz in one gigahertz"]], "T5C08": ["P = I &times; E", ["<b>P</b> = power, in watts", "<b>I</b> = current, in amperes", "<b>E</b> = voltage, in volts (from &ldquo;electromotive force&rdquo;)"]], "T5C09": ["P = I &times; E", ["<b>P</b> = power, in watts", "<b>I</b> = current, in amperes", "<b>E</b> = voltage, in volts (from &ldquo;electromotive force&rdquo;)"]], "T5C10": ["P = I &times; E", ["<b>P</b> = power, in watts", "<b>I</b> = current, in amperes", "<b>E</b> = voltage, in volts (from &ldquo;electromotive force&rdquo;)"]], "T5C11": ["I = P &divide; E", ["<b>I</b> = current, in amperes", "<b>P</b> = power, in watts", "<b>E</b> = voltage, in volts (from &ldquo;electromotive force&rdquo;)"]], "T5D01": ["I = E &divide; R", ["<b>I</b> = current, in amperes", "<b>E</b> = voltage, in volts (from &ldquo;electromotive force&rdquo;)", "<b>R</b> = resistance, in ohms (&Omega;)"]], "T5D02": ["E = I &times; R", ["<b>E</b> = voltage, in volts (from &ldquo;electromotive force&rdquo;)", "<b>I</b> = current, in amperes", "<b>R</b> = resistance, in ohms (&Omega;)"]], "T5D03": ["R = E &divide; I", ["<b>R</b> = resistance, in ohms (&Omega;)", "<b>E</b> = voltage, in volts (from &ldquo;electromotive force&rdquo;)", "<b>I</b> = current, in amperes"]], "T5D04": ["R = E &divide; I", ["<b>R</b> = resistance, in ohms (&Omega;)", "<b>E</b> = voltage, in volts (from &ldquo;electromotive force&rdquo;)", "<b>I</b> = current, in amperes"]], "T5D05": ["R = E &divide; I", ["<b>R</b> = resistance, in ohms (&Omega;)", "<b>E</b> = voltage, in volts (from &ldquo;electromotive force&rdquo;)", "<b>I</b> = current, in amperes"]], "T5D06": ["R = E &divide; I", ["<b>R</b> = resistance, in ohms (&Omega;)", "<b>E</b> = voltage, in volts (from &ldquo;electromotive force&rdquo;)", "<b>I</b> = current, in amperes"]], "T5D07": ["I = E &divide; R", ["<b>I</b> = current, in amperes", "<b>E</b> = voltage, in volts (from &ldquo;electromotive force&rdquo;)", "<b>R</b> = resistance, in ohms (&Omega;)"]], "T5D08": ["I = E &divide; R", ["<b>I</b> = current, in amperes", "<b>E</b> = voltage, in volts (from &ldquo;electromotive force&rdquo;)", "<b>R</b> = resistance, in ohms (&Omega;)"]], "T5D09": ["I = E &divide; R", ["<b>I</b> = current, in amperes", "<b>E</b> = voltage, in volts (from &ldquo;electromotive force&rdquo;)", "<b>R</b> = resistance, in ohms (&Omega;)"]], "T5D10": ["E = I &times; R", ["<b>E</b> = voltage, in volts (from &ldquo;electromotive force&rdquo;)", "<b>I</b> = current, in amperes", "<b>R</b> = resistance, in ohms (&Omega;)"]], "T5D11": ["E = I &times; R", ["<b>E</b> = voltage, in volts (from &ldquo;electromotive force&rdquo;)", "<b>I</b> = current, in amperes", "<b>R</b> = resistance, in ohms (&Omega;)"]], "T5D12": ["E = I &times; R", ["<b>E</b> = voltage, in volts (from &ldquo;electromotive force&rdquo;)", "<b>I</b> = current, in amperes", "<b>R</b> = resistance, in ohms (&Omega;)"]], "T5D13": ["I<sub>total</sub> = I<sub>1</sub> = I<sub>2</sub> = I<sub>3</sub>", ["<b>I<sub>total</sub></b> = current through the whole circuit, in amperes", "<b>I<sub>1</sub>, I<sub>2</sub>, I<sub>3</sub></b> = current through each component in series"]], "T5D14": ["E<sub>total</sub> = E<sub>1</sub> = E<sub>2</sub> = E<sub>3</sub>", ["<b>E<sub>total</sub></b> = voltage across the whole circuit, in volts", "<b>E<sub>1</sub>, E<sub>2</sub>, E<sub>3</sub></b> = voltage across each component in parallel"]], "T3B06": ["&lambda; = 300 &divide; f", ["<b>&lambda;</b> (lambda) = wavelength, in meters", "<b>f</b> = frequency, in megahertz", "<b>300</b> = speed of light, in millions of meters per second"]], "T9A08": ["L = (300 &divide; f) &divide; 4", ["<b>L</b> = quarter-wave length, in meters (1 meter &asymp; 39.4 inches)", "<b>f</b> = frequency, in megahertz", "<b>300 &divide; f</b> = one full wavelength; dividing by 4 gives a quarter"]], "T4A09": ["t = Ah &divide; I<sub>avg</sub>", ["<b>t</b> = run time, in hours", "<b>Ah</b> = battery capacity, in ampere-hours", "<b>I<sub>avg</sub></b> = average current draw, in amperes"]], "T4A01": ["P = I &times; E", ["<b>P</b> = power, in watts", "<b>I</b> = current, in amperes", "<b>E</b> = voltage, in volts (from &ldquo;electromotive force&rdquo;)"]], "T0C03": ["S<sub>allowed</sub> = S<sub>MPE</sub> &divide; DC", ["<b>S<sub>allowed</sub></b> = allowable power density at your duty cycle", "<b>S<sub>MPE</sub></b> = maximum permissible exposure limit at 100% duty cycle", "<b>DC</b> = duty cycle as a decimal; 50% = 0.5, so the limit doubles"]]}, "cheat": "<p><b>Ohm's law:</b> E = I &times; R, I = E &divide; R, R = E &divide; I. E is volts, I is amps, R is ohms.</p>\n<p><b>Power:</b> P = I &times; E, in watts. Rearranged, I = P &divide; E.</p>\n<p><b>Prefixes:</b> pico (trillionth), micro (millionth), milli (thousandth), kilo (thousand), mega (million), giga (billion). Each step between milli, base unit, kilo, mega and giga is a factor of 1,000.</p>\n<p><b>Converting:</b> to a smaller unit, multiply; to a bigger unit, divide.</p>\n<p><b>Decibels:</b> double the power = +3 dB, four times = +6 dB, ten times = +10 dB. Decreases are the same numbers, negative.</p>\n<p><b>Wavelength:</b> meters = 300 &divide; MHz. A quarter-wave antenna is that &divide; 4.</p>\n<p><b>Battery run time:</b> hours = ampere-hours &divide; average current in amps.</p>"}, {"id": "symbols", "code": "Sym", "name": "Schematic symbols", "exam": 0, "S": [{"n": "Resistor", "def": "Opposes the flow of current in a circuit. Resistance is measured in ohms.", "f": "res", "d": "<polyline points=\"10,30 35,30 40,20 48,40 56,20 64,40 72,20 80,40 85,30 110,30\"/>", "t": "The zigzag. Opposes current."}, {"n": "Variable resistor", "def": "A resistor whose resistance can be adjusted. A potentiometer, such as a volume control, is one kind.", "f": "res", "d": "<polyline points=\"10,30 35,30 40,20 48,40 56,20 64,40 72,20 80,40 85,30 110,30\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"52\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"35\"/><polygon points=\"60.0,35.0 63.5,42.2 56.5,42.2\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"52\" x2=\"94\" y2=\"52\"/><line x1=\"94\" y1=\"52\" x2=\"94\" y2=\"30\"/><circle cx=\"94\" cy=\"30\" r=\"2.8\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/>", "t": "Zigzag with an arrow pushed up into it and looped back to one end. Component 9 in figure T-2."}, {"n": "Capacitor", "def": "Stores energy in an electric field, using conductive surfaces separated by an insulator. Capacitance is measured in farads.", "f": "cap", "d": "<line x1=\"10\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"55\" y2=\"30\"/><line x1=\"55\" y1=\"14\" x2=\"55\" y2=\"46\"/><path d=\"M68 14 Q61 30 68 46\"/><line x1=\"64.5\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"30\"/>", "t": "A straight plate facing a curved plate with a gap between: conductive surfaces separated by an insulator. Component 6 in figure T-2."}, {"n": "Variable capacitor", "def": "A capacitor whose capacitance can be adjusted, often used for tuning.", "f": "cap", "d": "<line x1=\"10\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"55\" y2=\"30\"/><line x1=\"55\" y1=\"14\" x2=\"55\" y2=\"46\"/><line x1=\"65\" y1=\"14\" x2=\"65\" y2=\"46\"/><line x1=\"65\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"30\"/><line x1=\"36\" y1=\"52\" x2=\"86\" y2=\"8\"/><polygon points=\"86.0,8.0 82.9,15.4 78.3,10.1\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/>", "t": "Two plates with a diagonal arrow through them. Component 2 in figure T-3."}, {"n": "Inductor", "def": "Stores energy in a magnetic field and is typically built as a coil of wire. Inductance is measured in henrys. Paired with a capacitor, it forms a resonant (tuned) circuit.", "f": "ind", "d": "<path d=\"M10 30 L30 30 a7.5 7.5 0 0 1 15 0 a7.5 7.5 0 0 1 15 0 a7.5 7.5 0 0 1 15 0 a7.5 7.5 0 0 1 15 0 L110 30\"/>", "t": "The loops are a coil of wire."}, {"n": "Variable inductor", "def": "An inductor whose inductance can be adjusted, often used for tuning, as in the antenna tuner of figure T-3.", "f": "ind", "d": "<path d=\"M10 30 L30 30 a7.5 7.5 0 0 1 15 0 a7.5 7.5 0 0 1 15 0 a7.5 7.5 0 0 1 15 0 a7.5 7.5 0 0 1 15 0 L110 30\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"52\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"33\"/><polygon points=\"60.0,33.0 63.5,40.2 56.5,40.2\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"52\" x2=\"96\" y2=\"52\"/><line x1=\"96\" y1=\"52\" x2=\"96\" y2=\"30\"/><circle cx=\"96\" cy=\"30\" r=\"2.8\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/>", "t": "A coil with an arrow tapping into it, looped back to one end. Component 3 in figure T-3."}, {"n": "Transformer", "def": "Changes AC voltage up or down, such as 120 V AC to a lower AC voltage. The output is still AC, and a transformer cannot provide power gain.", "f": "ind", "d": "<path d=\"M28 8 L40 8 a5 5 0 0 1 0 10 a5 5 0 0 1 0 10 a5 5 0 0 1 0 10 a5 5 0 0 1 0 10 L28 48\"/><path d=\"M92 8 L80 8 a5 5 0 0 0 0 10 a5 5 0 0 0 0 10 a5 5 0 0 0 0 10 a5 5 0 0 0 0 10 L92 48\"/><line x1=\"57\" y1=\"6\" x2=\"57\" y2=\"50\"/><line x1=\"63\" y1=\"6\" x2=\"63\" y2=\"50\"/>", "t": "Two coils facing each other, with lines between them for the iron core. Component 4 in figure T-2."}, {"n": "Battery", "def": "Supplies electrical energy as DC. Nickel-metal hydride, lithium-ion, lead-acid and nickel-cadmium are rechargeable; carbon-zinc is not.", "f": "pwr", "d": "<line x1=\"10\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"52\" y2=\"30\"/><line x1=\"52\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"52\" y2=\"48\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"21\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"39\"/><line x1=\"68\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"68\" y2=\"48\"/><line x1=\"76\" y1=\"21\" x2=\"76\" y2=\"39\"/><line x1=\"76\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"30\"/>", "t": "Alternating long and short lines. The long line is the positive side. Component 4 in figure T-1."}, {"n": "Fuse", "def": "Removes power by opening the circuit when current gets too high. Never replace it with a higher-rated fuse, since excess current could cause a fire.", "f": "pwr", "d": "<line x1=\"10\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"35\" y2=\"30\"/><rect x=\"35\" y=\"22\" width=\"50\" height=\"16\" rx=\"2\"/><line x1=\"35\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"85\" y2=\"30\"/><line x1=\"85\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"30\"/>", "t": "A small box with the wire running straight through it. Component 2 in figure T-2."}, {"n": "Diode", "def": "Allows current to flow in only one direction. Its electrodes are the anode and cathode. Diodes form rectifiers, which change AC into DC.", "f": "dio", "d": "<line x1=\"10\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"45\" y2=\"30\"/><polygon points=\"45,17 45,43 70,30\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><line x1=\"70\" y1=\"17\" x2=\"70\" y2=\"43\"/><line x1=\"70\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"30\"/>", "t": "The triangle points the way current may flow. The bar is the cathode, matching the stripe on the part. Component 5 in figure T-2."}, {"n": "Light-emitting diode", "def": "A diode that gives off light when forward current flows through it. Commonly used as a visual indicator.", "f": "dio", "d": "<line x1=\"10\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"45\" y2=\"30\"/><polygon points=\"45,17 45,43 70,30\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><line x1=\"70\" y1=\"17\" x2=\"70\" y2=\"43\"/><line x1=\"70\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"30\"/><line x1=\"66\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"76\" y2=\"50\"/><polygon points=\"76.0,50.0 68.4,47.4 73.4,42.4\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><line x1=\"58\" y1=\"44\" x2=\"68\" y2=\"54\"/><polygon points=\"68.0,54.0 60.4,51.4 65.4,46.4\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/>", "t": "A diode with two arrows pointing away: light going out. Component 8 in figure T-2."}, {"n": "Zener diode", "def": "A diode designed to conduct backward once a set voltage is reached, so it holds that voltage steady. It's used as a simple voltage regulator.", "f": "dio", "d": "<line x1=\"10\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"45\" y2=\"30\"/><polygon points=\"45,17 45,43 70,30\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><line x1=\"70\" y1=\"17\" x2=\"70\" y2=\"43\"/><line x1=\"70\" y1=\"17\" x2=\"64\" y2=\"13\"/><line x1=\"70\" y1=\"43\" x2=\"76\" y2=\"47\"/><line x1=\"70\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"30\"/>", "t": "A diode whose bar has both ends bent like a Z. Used to regulate voltage. Component 10 in figure T-2."}, {"n": "NPN transistor", "def": "A bipolar junction transistor, with electrodes named emitter, base and collector. A transistor can amplify a signal, providing gain, or act as an electronic switch that controls the flow of current.", "f": "tr", "d": "<circle cx=\"64\" cy=\"30\" r=\"23\"/><line x1=\"10\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"52\" y2=\"30\"/><line x1=\"52\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"52\" y2=\"44\"/><line x1=\"52\" y1=\"24\" x2=\"72\" y2=\"13\"/><line x1=\"72\" y1=\"13\" x2=\"72\" y2=\"2\"/><line x1=\"72\" y1=\"47\" x2=\"72\" y2=\"58\"/><line x1=\"52\" y1=\"36\" x2=\"72\" y2=\"47\"/><polygon points=\"72.0,47.0 64.0,46.6 67.4,40.5\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/>", "t": "Circle with three leads. The emitter arrow points out: Not Pointing iN. Component 2 in figure T-1."}, {"n": "PNP transistor", "def": "A bipolar junction transistor of the opposite polarity to NPN, with the same three electrodes and the same jobs: amplifying and switching.", "f": "tr", "d": "<circle cx=\"64\" cy=\"30\" r=\"23\"/><line x1=\"10\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"52\" y2=\"30\"/><line x1=\"52\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"52\" y2=\"44\"/><line x1=\"52\" y1=\"24\" x2=\"72\" y2=\"13\"/><line x1=\"72\" y1=\"13\" x2=\"72\" y2=\"2\"/><line x1=\"72\" y1=\"47\" x2=\"72\" y2=\"58\"/><line x1=\"72\" y1=\"47\" x2=\"52\" y2=\"36\"/><polygon points=\"52.0,36.0 60.0,36.4 56.6,42.5\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/>", "t": "Same shape as NPN, but the emitter arrow points in: Pointing iN Proudly."}, {"n": "Lamp", "def": "Gives off light when current flows through it.", "f": "ind2", "d": "<line x1=\"10\" y1=\"44\" x2=\"46\" y2=\"44\"/><path d=\"M46 44 L46 30 A14 14 0 0 1 74 30 L74 44\"/><path d=\"M54 44 L54 32 A6 6 0 0 1 66 32 L66 44\"/><line x1=\"74\" y1=\"44\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"44\"/>", "t": "A bulb-shaped dome. Component 3 in figure T-1."}, {"n": "Meter", "def": "Displays an electrical quantity, such as voltage or current, as a value. A voltmeter connects in parallel with a component; an ammeter connects in series.", "f": "ind2", "d": "<line x1=\"10\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"44\" y2=\"30\"/><circle cx=\"60\" cy=\"30\" r=\"16\"/><text x=\"60\" y=\"36.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"18\" font-family=\"Atkinson Hyperlegible, sans-serif\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">M</text><line x1=\"76\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"30\"/>", "t": "A circle with a letter inside: M for meter, or V and A for voltmeter and ammeter."}, {"n": "Ground", "def": "The common reference point of a circuit, connected to the equipment chassis or the earth.", "f": "gnd", "d": "<line x1=\"60\" y1=\"6\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"34\"/><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"34\" x2=\"80\" y2=\"34\"/><line x1=\"48\" y1=\"34\" x2=\"42\" y2=\"47\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"34\" x2=\"54\" y2=\"47\"/><line x1=\"72\" y1=\"34\" x2=\"66\" y2=\"47\"/>", "t": "A short line with diagonal hatching beneath it. It appears several times in figures T-1 and T-2 and once in T-3; component 5 in figure T-1 is one."}, {"n": "Antenna", "def": "Converts electrical energy into radio waves when transmitting, and radio waves back into electrical energy when receiving.", "f": "gnd", "d": "<line x1=\"60\" y1=\"58\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"6\"/><polygon points=\"40,6 80,6 60,32\"/>", "t": "An upside-down triangle on a stem. Component 4 in figure T-3."}, {"n": "Connector", "def": "Joins a cable or device to a circuit in a way that can be disconnected, such as a plug and jack.", "f": "gnd", "d": "<line x1=\"14\" y1=\"22\" x2=\"28\" y2=\"30\"/><line x1=\"14\" y1=\"38\" x2=\"28\" y2=\"30\"/><line x1=\"28\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"45\" y2=\"30\"/><circle cx=\"51\" cy=\"30\" r=\"6\"/><line x1=\"57\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"30\"/>", "t": "A sideways V (the contact) on a short line ending in a small open circle (the terminal). Figure T-1 has a pair at its left-hand input; they are not numbered."}, {"n": "Switch, single-pole single-throw", "def": "Opens or closes a single circuit.", "f": "sw", "d": "<line x1=\"10\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"42.5\" y2=\"30\"/><circle cx=\"46\" cy=\"30\" r=\"3.5\"/><line x1=\"49\" y1=\"28\" x2=\"75\" y2=\"15\"/><circle cx=\"78\" cy=\"30\" r=\"3.5\"/><line x1=\"81.5\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"30\"/>", "t": "One lever, one contact: simply on or off. Component 3 in figure T-2."}, {"n": "Switch, single-pole double-throw", "def": "Switches a single circuit between one of two other circuits.", "f": "sw", "d": "<line x1=\"10\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"36.5\" y2=\"30\"/><circle cx=\"40\" cy=\"30\" r=\"3.5\"/><line x1=\"43\" y1=\"28\" x2=\"72\" y2=\"20\"/><circle cx=\"78\" cy=\"16\" r=\"3.5\"/><circle cx=\"78\" cy=\"44\" r=\"3.5\"/><line x1=\"81.5\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"16\"/><line x1=\"81.5\" y1=\"44\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"44\"/>", "t": "One lever that can land on either of two contacts."}]}];
window.EXPLAIN={"T0A01": "A 12-volt battery won't shock you through dry hands, but it can deliver huge current. Shorting its terminals, such as with a wrench, can cause burns, fire or even an explosion.\n\nRemove rings and watches, and keep tools away from the terminals.", "T0A02": "Current flowing through the body can heat tissue, disrupt the electrical function of cells (including the heart), and cause involuntary muscle contractions, so you can't let go.\n\nAll of the choices are correct.", "T0A03": "In US AC wiring, black is hot, carrying the voltage. White is neutral, and green or bare copper is ground.\n\nNever assume; turn off the breaker and test before working.", "T0A04": "A fuse removes power when there's an overload. When too much current flows, it melts and opens the circuit.\n\nThat protects equipment and wiring and prevents fires. It won't protect you from shock: a fuse needs amps to blow, and a few hundredths of an amp through your chest can kill.", "T0A05": "A fuse protects the wiring. A 5-amp circuit's wiring isn't designed for 20 amps.\n\nWith a 20-amp fuse, enough current could flow to overheat wiring and start a fire before the fuse blows. Always replace with the same rating.", "T0A06": "Use three-wire cords and plugs, connect all station equipment to a common safety ground, and discharge capacitors before working on equipment. The third, ground wire gives a fault a safe path, so the breaker trips instead of a metal case becoming live.\n\nAll of the choices are correct.", "T0A07": "A lightning arrester belongs on a grounded panel near where feed lines enter the building.\n\nThat sends lightning energy to ground before it gets inside. At the radio would be too late: the surge would already be in the house.", "T0A08": "A fuse or circuit breaker is installed in series with the hot conductor only. In series means all the current must pass through it, so when it opens, the power stops.\n\nNever fuse the neutral or ground, which could leave equipment energized with the fuse blown.", "T0A09": "All external ground rods and earth connections should be bonded together with heavy wire or conductive strap.\n\nThat keeps them at the same potential during a lightning strike, preventing dangerous voltage differences.", "T0A10": "Charging or discharging an unprotected battery too quickly can cause overheating or out-gassing, venting flammable gases.\n\nLead-acid batteries release hydrogen. Lithium batteries can catch fire.", "T0A11": "Power supply filter capacitors store a charge and can hold a dangerous voltage after the supply is turned off.\n\nThat charge can stay even with the cord unplugged. Always discharge them safely before working inside.", "T0A12": "When measuring high voltage, use a voltmeter and test leads rated for that voltage.\n\nUnderrated equipment can arc, fail or shock you.", "T0B01": "Lightning ground wires should be short and direct. Long wires and sharp bends raise impedance and can cause lightning to jump elsewhere.\n\nA drip loop or right-angle bends would do the opposite, so keep the path straight and short.", "T0B02": "Climbing a tower requires training, an approved climbing harness and staying tied off at all times.\n\nAll of the choices are correct.", "T0B03": "Never climb a tower without a helper or observer on the ground.\n\nIf something goes wrong, someone needs to be there to help.", "T0B04": "When putting up a tower or antenna, look for and stay clear of overhead power lines.\n\nContact with a power line can be fatal. The other choices are wrong: towers are grounded, not insulated, and a wrist strap is for static-sensitive electronics, not towers.", "T0B05": "A safety wire through a turnbuckle keeps vibration from slowly loosening it.\n\nLoose guy lines could let the tower fall.", "T0B06": "Place antennas so that if they fall, no part can come within 10 feet of power lines.\n\nPlan for the worst case.", "T0B07": "Never climb a crank-up tower unless it's fully retracted or mechanical safety locks are installed.\n\nThe cables or mechanism could fail and drop the sections.", "T0B08": "For a tower, each leg should have its own 8-foot ground rod, bonded to the tower and to each other.\n\nThat spreads the lightning energy into the earth.", "T0B09": "Utility poles carry high-voltage power lines. Attaching an antenna to one risks contact with those lines.\n\nIt's also usually prohibited by the utility.", "T0B10": "Lightning ground conductors should avoid sharp bends. Lightning's fast current resists sudden turns and can jump off the wire.\n\nUse gentle curves.", "T0B11": "Local electrical codes set grounding requirements for towers and antennas.\n\nThe FCC doesn't set these rules. Check your local codes.", "T0C01": "Radio signals are non-ionizing radiation. They don't have enough energy to knock electrons out of atoms or break chemical bonds.\n\nX-rays and gamma rays are ionizing.", "T0C02": "The strictest RF exposure limits fall between 30 and 300 MHz, where the human body absorbs RF most efficiently. There an adult is roughly half a wavelength tall, so the body soaks up energy like an antenna.\n\nOf the choices, 50 MHz has the lowest maximum permissible exposure (MPE).", "T0C03": "Duty cycle is the percentage of time a transmitter is on. If it drops from 100 to 50 percent, the average exposure halves.\n\nSo the allowable power density doubles, increasing by a factor of 2.", "T0C04": "RF exposure depends on frequency and power, distance from the antenna and the antenna's radiation pattern.\n\nAll of the choices are correct.", "T0C05": "The human body absorbs more RF energy at some frequencies than at others, especially around 30 to 300 MHz.\n\nSo exposure limits vary with frequency. It isn't that lower frequencies carry more energy; if anything, the reverse is true.", "T0C06": "To check compliance with RF exposure rules, you can use calculations from FCC OET Bulletin 65, computer modeling, or measurements with calibrated equipment.\n\nAll of the choices are acceptable.", "T0C07": "Touching an antenna while it transmits can cause an RF burn to the skin.\n\nRF current flows through you at the contact point, heating tissue.", "T0C08": "Moving antennas away from people reduces RF exposure.\n\nThe antenna is the source; moving the transmitter doesn't change exposure. Raising the duty cycle would increase it.", "T0C09": "Re-evaluate your station whenever you change anything in the transmitter or antenna system.\n\nNew power levels, antennas or locations can change exposure.", "T0C10": "Duty cycle affects average RF exposure. A transmitter on less of the time causes less average exposure.\n\nThat's why it's a factor in exposure calculations.", "T0C11": "Duty cycle is the percentage of time a transmitter is transmitting during the averaging period.\n\nCW and SSB voice have lower duty cycles than FM and many digital modes, which keep the transmitter at full power the whole time you're keyed.", "T0C12": "RF radiation doesn't have enough energy to cause chemical changes in cells or damage DNA.\n\nIonizing radiation, like X-rays and gamma rays, does.\n\nThat doesn't make RF perfectly safe: strong RF fields heat body tissue, which is why exposure limits exist.", "T0C13": "The station licensee is responsible for ensuring no one is exposed to RF above FCC limits.\n\nThat includes neighbors and visitors.", "T1A01": "Part 97.1 gives five reasons the amateur service exists: emergency communication, advancing the radio art, improving technical and communication skills, a reserve of trained operators and technicians, and international goodwill.\n\nThe wrong answers describe other things. Radio for anyone who wants it is the Citizens Band idea, and contests are popular but not a stated purpose. If an answer sounds like skill-building or public service, it is probably the right one.", "T1A02": "The Federal Communications Commission writes the Part 97 rules and enforces them, including fines and license actions. Its rules come from US law and international treaties.\n\nThe ARRL is the national association of hams. It lobbies, publishes and runs volunteer examiner programs, but it has no legal power over anyone. Remember: FCC makes the rules, the ARRL helps hams live with them.", "T1A03": "A phonetic alphabet replaces letters with words (Alpha, Bravo, Charlie) so a call sign survives noise and accents. On phone, the FCC encourages it but does not require it.\n\nThe rule only requires that a voice ID be in English. Phonetics are a recommended aid, not required for emergency messages or foreign contacts either. Nobody gets cited for saying \"K F one\" instead of \"Kilo Foxtrot One.\"", "T1A04": "The FCC stopped mailing paper licenses years ago. Once your exam results are processed, it emails you a link to download your official license grant as a PDF.\n\nThat makes keeping a working email address on file with the FCC important, which shows up again in question T1C04. Check your spam folder after an exam session.", "T1A05": "The FCC's Universal Licensing System (ULS) is the official record. Your license exists, legally, once it appears there with your call sign.\n\nThe Certificate of Successful Completion of Examination (CSCE) your examiners hand you only proves you passed. It matters for upgrades, not for going on the air as a new ham. Think: the database is the license.", "T1A06": "Propagation beacons are automatic transmitters that send a call sign and short message all day. Hearing one tells you the band is open to the beacon's location.\n\nOn HF, the rules allow automatically controlled beacons only between 28.200 and 28.300 MHz on 10 meters. That slice sits just below the Technician phone segment that starts at 28.300 MHz, which makes it easy to picture.", "T1A07": "Part 97 defines a space station purely by altitude: any amateur station more than 50 km above the Earth's surface. That includes satellites, the ISS amateur station and even a high-altitude rocket while it is up there.\n\nThe number to remember is 50 km. Balloons usually top out around 30 to 40 km, so they don't count.", "T1A08": "Repeaters need coordinated channels so two machines in the same area don't step on each other. A Volunteer Frequency Coordinator keeps the records and recommends input and output pairs.\n\nThe FCC doesn't assign repeater frequencies, and coordination isn't strictly required, but coordinated repeaters get preference if interference disputes arise. The key word is volunteer.", "T1A09": "Frequency coordinators are chosen from the bottom up. The amateurs in a local or regional area whose stations are eligible to be repeater or auxiliary stations pick the coordinator for their region.\n\nNeither the FCC nor the ARRL appoints them. It's self-governance, matching the volunteer spirit of the service.", "T1A10": "RACES, the Radio Amateur Civil Emergency Service, lets hams support government emergency management. Because RACES works under civil defense authorities, a license alone isn't enough.\n\nThe control operator must also be certified as currently enrolled by a civil defense organization. Think of it as a ham license plus a badge from the emergency agency.", "T1A11": "Willful or malicious interference means deliberately disrupting other stations, and Part 97 bans it outright. It's one of the most serious violations and can cost you your license and bring heavy fines.\n\nThe other choices are permitted activities. VHF contacts with foreign hams and third-party traffic over digital modes are both normal amateur operating.", "T1B01": "On 10 meters, Technicians may use phone (voice) between 28.300 and 28.500 MHz, with up to 200 watts PEP. They also have CW and data from 28.000 to 28.300 MHz.\n\nThis 200 kHz voice window is the Technician's main taste of HF voice, and when the band is open it can reach around the world. Picture 28.3 to 28.5 as \"the Tech phone slice.\"", "T1B02": "The ISS carries amateur gear, and any US ham with a Technician license or higher may contact it on VHF and UHF. No special NASA permission is needed.\n\nThe usual frequencies are around 145.800 MHz for downlink voice. Contacts last only a few minutes during each pass, since the station moves fast across the sky.", "T1B03": "The 6-meter band runs from 50 to 54 MHz. Of the choices, only 52.525 MHz falls inside, and it happens to be the national 6-meter FM simplex calling frequency.\n\nA handy check: the wavelength in meters is about 300 divided by the frequency in MHz. 300 / 52.5 is about 5.7 meters, close to 6.", "T1B04": "Two meters covers 144 to 148 MHz, and 146.52 MHz sits right in the middle. It's the national FM simplex calling frequency, where hams call each other without a repeater.\n\nWavelength check: 300 / 146 is about 2 meters. Many new hams program 146.52 into their handhelds first, so it's worth memorizing.", "T1B05": "FT8 is a popular weak-signal digital mode. Technicians have data privileges on part of 10 meters (28.000 to 28.300 MHz), and on 6 and 2 meters everywhere except the CW-only bottom 100 kHz, so they can run FT8 on all three.\n\nWhen \"All these choices are correct\" appears with three bands Technicians clearly have, it's usually the answer.", "T1B06": "Technicians have HF privileges on 80, 40, 15 and 10 meters, but on the first three they're limited to CW. Voice on HF is allowed only on 10 meters, from 28.300 to 28.500 MHz.\n\nThe General license is what opens voice on the other HF bands. For the exam, link \"Technician HF phone\" to \"10 meters only.\"", "T1B07": "The bottom 100 kHz of both 6 and 2 meters is reserved for CW (Morse code). That's 50.0 to 50.1 MHz and 144.0 to 144.1 MHz.\n\nWeak-signal operators use these segments for long-distance CW contacts and beacons. Only 6 and 2 meters have a CW-only slice at the bottom. The other choices are traps: 219 to 220 MHz is for data, and the 420 and 902 MHz bands allow many modes right from their lower edge.", "T1B08": "Some amateur bands are shared with other radio services. Where amateurs are secondary, the primary users come first.\n\nYou may hear non-amateur stations there, and you must not cause them harmful interference. If they interfere with you, you have to put up with it. Secondary means second in line.", "T1B09": "Your radio's frequency display may not be perfectly accurate, oscillators can drift as they warm up, and every signal has sidebands that extend above and below the dial frequency.\n\nAny of those can push part of your signal outside the band, which is a violation. Keep your transmissions comfortably inside the edges, especially on SSB, which is about 3 kHz wide.", "T1B10": "Above 50 MHz, every amateur band includes at least one segment where SSB voice is allowed. On 6 and 2 meters that is everything above the CW-only bottom 100 kHz, and on the higher bands phone is allowed nearly everywhere (the 219 to 220 MHz data segment is the exception). By band-plan custom, SSB activity on 6 and 2 meters gathers near the low end, around 50.125 and 144.200 MHz.\n\nSSB on VHF and UHF is used for weak-signal and long-distance contacts, while FM is used for local repeater chat.", "T1B11": "In their HF segments (80, 40, 15 and 10 meters), Technicians may run up to 200 watts PEP. PEP, peak envelope power, is the highest power at the crest of the signal.\n\nAbove 30 MHz the limit jumps to the full 1,500 watts. Remember: HF 200, VHF and up 1,500.", "T1B12": "Above 30 MHz, Technicians have the same 1,500 watts PEP legal limit as every other license class, except in a few specific restricted segments.\n\nThat high limit is rarely needed. Good practice is always to use the minimum power necessary to make the contact.", "T1C01": "Today the FCC issues new licenses in only three classes: Technician, General and Amateur Extra.\n\nNovice and Advanced licenses still exist for people who already hold them and keep renewing, but no one can earn them new. Technician Plus is gone as a class: those licenses are renewed as Technician. The three-step ladder is Technician, General, Extra.", "T1C02": "Any licensed amateur may apply for a vanity call sign, a call sign you choose rather than one assigned in sequence.\n\nYour license class decides which formats you may pick. Technicians can choose from Group C and D formats, while Extras can get the short, prized 1x2 and 2x1 calls.", "T1C03": "International ham contacts must stay within the spirit of the service: talk about amateur radio itself, or personal remarks like weather, family and hobbies.\n\nBusiness, advertising and anything for profit are out. Think of it as a friendly chat between hobbyists across borders.", "T1C04": "The FCC communicates with licensees by email, so you must keep a current address on file in ULS.\n\nIf the FCC can't reach you, it may revoke your station license or suspend your operator license. Update your email whenever it changes, just as you would a mailing address.", "T1C05": "New Technicians get a Group D call sign. The format is two letters, one digit, then three letters, like KF1XXX.\n\nThe digit is a call area number. Shorter formats depend on license class. Technicians may also hold Group C calls (one letter, a digit, three letters, like N1XYZ), but the sequential supply ran out long ago. Group B calls, like KB1AB, need an Advanced or Extra license, and Group A calls, like K1AB or KA1X, are for Extras only.", "T1C06": "In international waters, you may operate your amateur station aboard a vessel documented (registered) in the United States, as long as the ship's master gives permission.\n\nYour FCC license works there because a vessel documented in the US is under US rules, even far from shore. On a foreign-flagged vessel, different rules apply.", "T1C07": "You can apply to renew your license up to 90 days before it expires. That gives you time to handle any problems with the application.\n\nRenewal is done online through the FCC's ULS. Numbers for licenses: 10 years long, renew in the last 90 days, 2 years of grace afterward.", "T1C08": "An amateur license lasts ten years. Renewal resets the clock for another ten.\n\nMost hams renew several times over their lives without ever retaking an exam. Pair the facts: ten-year term, 90-day renewal window, two-year grace period.", "T1C09": "If your license expires, you have two years to renew it without retaking the exam.\n\nDuring that grace period your license is expired, so you can't transmit (see T1C11). After two years it's gone, and you'd have to test again to get a new one.", "T1C10": "You may transmit as soon as your license grant shows up in the FCC's ULS database. Passing the exam alone doesn't let you transmit.\n\nThis usually takes a few days to a couple of weeks after your exam session. Watch ULS for your call sign to appear.", "T1C11": "During the two-year grace period, the license is expired. You may renew it, but you can't transmit until the renewal is granted and shows in the database.\n\nThe grace period protects your call sign and spares you the exam, not your operating privileges.", "T1D01": "The International Telecommunication Union (ITU) coordinates radio worldwide. If a country's government tells the ITU it objects to amateur contacts with its stations, FCC-licensed hams must not contact them.\n\nThe ARRL and IARU are amateur clubs and can't ban anything. Look for the answer that names the international treaty body.", "T1D02": "Amateur radio is two-way communication between hams, so broadcasting to the general public is prohibited.\n\nSome one-way transmissions are allowed: code practice, telemetry, beacons, and brief announcements of interest to other hams. The test: is it meant for the public at large? Then it's broadcasting.", "T1D03": "Amateur radio is meant to be open. Messages encoded to hide their meaning are generally forbidden.\n\nThe narrow exception is control commands to space stations or to radio-controlled model craft, where secrecy keeps outsiders from taking over the device. Standard digital modes aren't encryption, since their formats are published.", "T1D04": "Music is normally prohibited on the amateur bands. The one exception is when it's incidental to an authorized retransmission of communications from a manned spacecraft.\n\nFor example, if NASA audio from an astronaut includes background music, a ham station relaying that feed with NASA's prior approval is allowed to carry it.", "T1D05": "Hams may mention amateur equipment they're selling or trading, but not on a regular basis. Occasional swap announcements are fine.\n\nRunning a business on the air, or selling non-ham items, isn't allowed. The rule protects the noncommercial nature of the service.", "T1D06": "Indecent and obscene language is prohibited on the amateur bands.\n\nThe FCC doesn't publish a list of banned words. Use the standard you'd follow speaking in public, since anyone, including kids, may be listening.", "T1D07": "An auxiliary station sends one-way transmissions that support another station, such as linking a remote receiver site back to the main repeater transmitter, or carrying control signals.\n\nAuxiliary stations are the plumbing behind repeater systems. Remember auxiliary as one-way support links.", "T1D08": "Amateur radio is noncommercial, so operators generally can't be paid for operating. One exception: a teacher may be paid when operating is part of classroom instruction at an educational institution.\n\nThat lets schools use ham radio in lessons without breaking the rules.", "T1D09": "Amateurs may help broadcasters or news crews only when the information directly relates to the immediate safety of human life or protection of property, and only when no other means of communication is available.\n\nIt's an emergency exception, not a way to supply routine news.", "T1D10": "The FCC defines broadcasting in the amateur service as transmissions intended for reception by the general public.\n\nThat's why broadcasting is prohibited: hams talk to other hams. Announcements aimed at hams, like a club net notice, aren't broadcasting.", "T1D11": "Signals sent only to control a model craft, like an RC plane or boat, don't need on-air identification. Instead, the transmitter must carry a label showing the call sign and the licensee's name and address.\n\nThe other choices are traps: brief adjustments, unmodulated carriers and very low power all still need your call sign. Space stations and telecommand stations are also exempt, but they aren't offered here.", "T1D12": "Test transmissions still must be identified with your call sign, at least every 10 minutes and at the end.\n\nIf you're tuning an antenna or checking a radio on the air, give your call sign. Better still, use a dummy load when you can.", "T1E01": "Every amateur station that transmits must have a control operator, a licensed ham responsible for it. The answer is never.\n\nEven automatic stations, like repeaters, have a control operator who sets up the system and answers for its operation.", "T1E02": "When using an amateur satellite, the control operator can be any ham whose license allows transmitting on the satellite's uplink frequency.\n\nMost satellite uplinks are on 2 meters or 70 cm, where Technicians have full privileges. So yes, Technicians can work satellites.", "T1E03": "The station licensee, the person whose call sign the station uses, decides who the control operator is.\n\nYou can operate a friend's station with their permission. They designate you as control operator, and you follow your own license limits.", "T1E04": "Transmitting privileges follow the control operator's license class, not the station owner's.\n\nA Technician running an Extra's station still has only Technician privileges. An Extra using a Technician's station may use full Extra privileges, but must then identify with the station's call followed by their own, such as KF1XYZ/K1AB.", "T1E05": "The control point is where the control operator actually controls the station: the radio's front panel, a remote computer or a phone app.\n\nWith remote operation, the control point can be far from the transmitter itself. Control point means where the controlling happens.", "T1E06": "Outside an emergency, a Technician may never be the control operator in a band segment reserved for Amateur Extra licensees.\n\nPrivileges follow the control operator's license, so a Technician can't borrow an Extra's privileges even at the Extra's station.", "T1E07": "When the control operator isn't the station licensee, both are responsible for the station operating properly.\n\nThe owner can't hand off all accountability, and the guest operator can't claim it was someone else's radio. Shared responsibility.", "T1E08": "Automatic control means the station operates without a control operator at a control point, with safeguards built in. A repeater is the classic example.\n\nIt listens on one frequency and retransmits on another by itself, around the clock, while its control operator is responsible but not watching every transmission.", "T1E09": "Any amateur station may be operated by remote control, as long as the control operator follows the rules from the remote control point.\n\nRemote operation is common today, letting hams run home stations from work or while traveling.", "T1E10": "Remote control means the control operator is at a control point away from the station, for example operating your home radio over the internet.\n\nDon't confuse it with telecommand, which is controlling a model aircraft or a space station by radio.", "T1E11": "A control operator is an amateur designated by the station licensee to be responsible for the station's transmissions and FCC rule compliance.\n\nOften the licensee is the control operator of their own station, but they can name someone else. The definition stresses responsibility and designation.", "T1F01": "An FCC representative may inspect your station and its records at any time upon request. No appointment, warrant or advance notice is needed.\n\nAgreeing to that is a condition of holding the license. In practice it's rare, but the rule is clear.", "T1F02": "Tactical call signs like \"Race Headquarters\" help clarity during events, but they don't replace your FCC call.\n\nYou must still give your assigned call sign at least every 10 minutes during the conversation and at the end. The same 10-minute rule applies everywhere.", "T1F03": "Give your call sign at least every 10 minutes during a contact and when you finish. Identifying at the start is polite but not required.\n\nA timer helps on long contacts. Remember the pattern: every 10 minutes and at the end.", "T1F04": "When identifying by voice, you must use English. The FCC needs to recognize call signs anywhere.\n\nYou can carry on the rest of the conversation in any language, but the call sign itself goes out in English.", "T1F05": "On phone, you may identify either by voice or by sending your call sign in CW (Morse code).\n\nMany repeaters identify themselves in CW automatically. Voice-only isn't required, which is why answer D is wrong: a CW ID counts too.", "T1F06": "Indicators like /W3 or /M (mobile) can be added to a call sign. When speaking, you may say stroke, slant or slash, as in \"KL7CC stroke W3.\"\n\nAll are acceptable, so the answer is all of the choices.", "T1F07": "A person without a license may talk to a foreign station through your station only if the foreign country has a third-party agreement with the United States.\n\nWithout that agreement, international messages for an unlicensed person aren't allowed. Your station's control operator must be present.", "T1F08": "Third-party communication is a message from one control operator to another on behalf of someone else, a third party, who usually isn't a ham.\n\nPassing a message for a non-ham friend, or letting them speak through your station, is third-party traffic.", "T1F09": "A repeater receives a signal on one frequency and simultaneously retransmits it on another, usually from a high site with more power, extending the range of handhelds and mobiles.\n\nSimultaneous retransmission on a different channel is the definition of a repeater.", "T1F10": "If a repeater accidentally retransmits a rule-breaking message, the control operator of the originating station is responsible, not the repeater owner or its control operator.\n\nThe person who started the transmission is accountable for its content.", "T1F11": "A club station license requires at least four members.\n\nThe trustee can hold any license class, and the club doesn't need to register with the ARRL. Four people make a club.", "T2A01": "A repeater listens on one frequency (its input) and transmits on another (its output). On 2 meters, the two are usually 600 kHz apart.\n\nWhether you add or subtract the 600 kHz depends on the repeater, which is why it's \"plus or minus.\" Most radios set the offset automatically from the band plan. Pair it: 2 meters, 600 kHz; 70 cm, 5 MHz.", "T2A02": "146.520 MHz is the national FM simplex calling frequency on 2 meters. Simplex means direct radio-to-radio contact, with no repeater.\n\nCall there, make contact, then move to another simplex frequency to chat so the calling channel stays open. It's also a good place to listen when traveling.", "T2A03": "On the 70-centimeter band (420 to 450 MHz), repeaters usually use a 5 MHz offset between input and output.\n\nHigher bands use bigger offsets because they're wider and the transmitter and receiver need more separation to coexist. Remember: 2 meters, 600 kHz; 70 cm, 5 MHz.", "T2A04": "On a repeater, if you're looking for a specific station, say their call sign, then give yours, like \"W1ABC, this is KF1XYZ.\"\n\nCalling CQ on a repeater isn't customary, and \"break break\" is reserved for urgent situations. Their call first, then yours.", "T2A05": "To answer a CQ, say the calling station's call sign followed by your own. That tells them you're calling them, and who you are.\n\nThe same order applies everywhere: the station you're calling first, then your identification.", "T2A06": "Off the repeaters, on simplex or HF, a general call is made with \"CQ.\" Say CQ a few times, then \"this is\" and your call sign, then listen.\n\nIf no one answers, repeat. Keep calls short so stations tuning by can catch you.", "T2A07": "A repeater's offset is the difference between the frequency it listens on and the frequency it transmits on.\n\nYour radio listens on the output and transmits on the input, so it must be set to the right offset to reach the repeater.", "T2A08": "CQ is a general call meaning \"calling any station.\" It invites anyone who hears it to answer.\n\nIt dates back to telegraph days and is used in voice, CW and digital modes alike.", "T2A09": "On a repeater, the custom is to say your call sign followed by \"listening\" or \"monitoring.\" That tells others you're available for a chat.\n\nCQ is common on HF and simplex, but on repeaters \"listening\" is the norm.", "T2A10": "A band plan is a voluntary guideline that says which modes or activities go where within a band, such as CW at the bottom, then SSB, then FM and repeaters.\n\nIt adds detail on top of the FCC's rules but isn't itself law. Following it keeps operators from interfering with each other. Voluntary but respected.", "T2A11": "Simplex means transmitting and receiving on the same frequency, talking directly radio to radio.\n\nDuplex, used by repeaters, means separate transmit and receive frequencies. Simplex: same frequency, no repeater.", "T2B01": "The reverse function swaps your radio's transmit and receive frequencies so you hear the repeater's input, where the other station is transmitting.\n\nIf you can hear them directly, you can move to a simplex frequency and leave the repeater free for others.", "T2B02": "CTCSS, Continuous Tone-Coded Squelch System, sends a low tone below the range of hearing along with your voice. A repeater set to that tone opens only when it hears it.\n\nThis keeps the repeater from responding to interference or distant users. Many radios call it \"tone\" or \"PL.\"", "T2B03": "A linked repeater network connects several repeaters so that whatever one receives, all of them transmit. You can talk through one repeater and be heard across a wide region.\n\nLinks may use radio or the internet.", "T2B04": "If you can hear a repeater but can't get into it, several settings could be wrong: the offset, a missing CTCSS tone or a wrong DCS code.\n\nAny of these keeps the repeater from accepting your signal, so the answer is all of the choices. Check the repeater's listing for its exact settings.", "T2B05": "FM signals vary their frequency with your voice. If you talk too loudly or too close to the mic, you overdeviate: your signal swings wider than the receiving radio's filter can pass.\n\nThe result is distorted audio that cuts out on voice peaks. Speak normally, with the mic a few inches away.", "T2B06": "DTMF, Dual-Tone Multi-Frequency, sends two audio tones at once for each key. It's the same system as a phone keypad.\n\nHams use DTMF to send commands to repeaters, such as turning on links or reading out status.", "T2B07": "On DMR (Digital Mobile Radio) repeaters, a talkgroup is a channel within the system. You join one by programming its ID number into your radio's channel settings.\n\nThere's no sign-up for a talkgroup: set the right ID and you're in. Using DMR at all does require your own DMR ID number, a free one-time online registration, but that's a separate step.", "T2B08": "No one owns a frequency. When two stations interfere with each other, they should talk it out and decide who stays and who moves.\n\nCourtesy and cooperation are expected. Nets and emergencies get extra consideration.", "T2B09": "Band plans set aside simplex channels so nearby stations can talk directly without tying up a repeater.\n\nRepeaters serve many users, so if you're close enough to reach each other directly, simplex is the courteous choice.", "T2B10": "Q signals are three-letter codes starting with Q. QRM means man-made interference from other stations. QRN means natural noise, like static from lightning.\n\nMemory hook: QRM, M for man-made; QRN, N for natural.", "T2B11": "QSY means \"change frequency\" or \"I'm changing frequency.\" Hams say \"let's QSY to 146.55\" when moving a conversation off a calling channel.\n\nOther common ones: QTH is location, QSL is acknowledgment.", "T2B12": "On DMR repeaters, the color code is an access code your radio must transmit to use a particular repeater. It works like a CTCSS tone does on an FM repeater.\n\nWithout the right color code, the repeater ignores your signal.", "T2B13": "Squelch mutes the receiver's audio when no signal is present, cutting the constant hiss you'd otherwise hear.\n\nWhen a signal arrives, squelch opens and you hear the station. Set it just past where the noise stops.", "T2B14": "On DMR, a talkgroup is an identifier that sorts radio traffic. Your radio plays only the groups you've chosen, so you aren't bothered by everything else on the system.\n\nTalkgroups can be local, regional or worldwide.", "T2C01": "FCC rules always apply to amateur stations. There's no exemption for emergencies, public service events, RACES or ARES.\n\nDuring a real emergency, some flexibility exists, like operating outside your privileges to protect life, but that comes from the rules themselves.", "T2C02": "The Net Control Station runs a net. It calls the net to order, takes check-ins and directs which station transmits when.\n\nThis keeps busy nets orderly, especially during emergencies when many stations need to pass information.", "T2C03": "When a word is unusual or easily misheard, spell it with a standard phonetic alphabet: Alpha, Bravo, Charlie and so on.\n\nThat makes names, addresses and technical terms come through correctly, even with noise.", "T2C04": "RACES, Radio Amateur Civil Emergency Service, is an FCC-authorized service under Part 97 for civil defense communications. It's activated during emergencies by government agencies.\n\nUnlike ARES, members must be certified by a civil defense organization.", "T2C05": "In net operation, traffic means formal messages exchanged between stations, often radiograms with a standard format.\n\nTraffic nets pass messages across the country, and during emergencies they relay health and welfare information.", "T2C06": "ARES, the Amateur Radio Emergency Service, is a group of licensed hams who voluntarily register their skills and equipment for public service communications.\n\nIt's organized by the ARRL and works with local agencies. No government certification is required, unlike RACES.", "T2C07": "In a directed net, you transmit only when net control tells you to, unless you're reporting an emergency.\n\nThis keeps the net efficient and avoids people talking over each other.", "T2C08": "Winlink is a system that sends email over amateur radio. Addresses are built from call signs, like KF1XYZ@winlink.org.\n\nIt's a key emergency tool when internet service is down.", "T2C09": "Operators may go outside their license privileges only in emergencies involving immediate safety of human life or protection of property.\n\nIf someone's life is at stake and no other way exists, any frequency or mode may be used.", "T2C10": "A formal message, or radiogram, has a preamble at the top. It contains tracking information: message number, precedence, the station of origin, the check, and the time and date.\n\nIt helps relay stations handle and trace the message.", "T2C11": "In a radiogram's preamble, the \"check\" is the number of words in the message text.\n\nThe receiving station counts the words and compares. If they don't match, something was lost or added.", "T2C12": "RACES requires certification by a civil defense agency because it operates under government emergency management.\n\nARES, MARS (the military affiliate system) and SKYWARN (storm spotters) don't require civil defense certification.", "T3A01": "At VHF, signals reach you by several routes at once: directly, and bounced off buildings, hills and vehicles. Those copies arrive slightly out of step.\n\nWhere they line up, they reinforce; where they're opposite, they cancel. Moving the antenna a few feet changes the mix, so strength can jump or vanish. That's multipath.", "T3A02": "Leaves and branches are full of water, which absorbs energy at UHF and microwave frequencies. The shorter the wavelength, the worse it gets.\n\nStrong signals may push through, but weak ones get lost. Microwave links are often planned around tree lines for this reason.", "T3A03": "For long-distance weak-signal work on VHF and UHF, using CW or SSB, operators use horizontally polarized antennas, such as horizontal Yagis.\n\nFM and repeater operation use vertical polarization, since vertical antennas are easy on cars and handhelds. Mixing the two costs signal strength.", "T3A04": "Cross-polarization means one antenna is vertical and the other horizontal. Over a direct line-of-sight path, the receiving antenna picks up only a small fraction of the signal.\n\nThe loss can be 20 dB or more, meaning a hundredth of the power or less gets through. Match polarization: vertical to vertical, horizontal to horizontal.", "T3A05": "Radio waves reflect off buildings, mountains and other large surfaces. If the direct path to a repeater is blocked, aim your directional antenna to bounce the signal off something toward it.\n\nTry different directions and listen for the best signal. It's like using a mirror to see around a corner.", "T3A06": "Picket fencing is rapid flutter heard on mobile VHF and UHF signals. As the car moves, multipath reflections change quickly, so the signal strength pulses.\n\nThe name comes from the sound of a stick dragged along a picket fence.", "T3A07": "At microwave frequencies, rain, snow and fog absorb and scatter radio energy. The drops are a significant size compared with the very short wavelengths.\n\nHeavy rain can shorten a microwave link's range noticeably. Lower bands are barely affected.", "T3A08": "Signals bouncing off the ionosphere can take several paths that shift as the ionosphere changes. The copies combine at random, sometimes adding, sometimes canceling.\n\nThe result is irregular fading, where a station swells and fades over seconds or minutes.", "T3A09": "When a signal travels through the ionosphere, its polarization twists, coming back elliptically polarized, a mix of vertical and horizontal.\n\nSo on HF skywave contacts, polarization barely matters. A vertical antenna can talk to a horizontal one without the big loss you'd see at VHF.", "T3A10": "Multipath delivers several copies of a signal at slightly different times. For data, that blurs the timing of each bit or symbol.\n\nThe receiver struggles to tell one bit from the next, so error rates go up. Many digital modes include error correction to cope.", "T3A11": "The ionosphere is a layer of the upper atmosphere ionized by solar radiation, roughly 50 to 600 km up. It can bend HF radio waves back to Earth.\n\nThat's what makes long-distance HF contacts possible. The troposphere (where weather happens) and the stratosphere are lower and not ionized enough to do this, and \"electrosphere\" isn't a standard atmospheric layer.", "T3A12": "Fog and rain have little effect on 10 and 6 meters. The waves are long compared with water droplets, so they pass right through.\n\nWeather absorption becomes a real factor only at microwave frequencies, several gigahertz and up.", "T3B01": "A radio wave is made of an electric field and a magnetic field. They're at right angles to each other, and both are at right angles to the direction the wave travels.\n\nPicture three perpendicular arrows: electric up, magnetic sideways, travel forward.", "T3B02": "Polarization is defined by the orientation of the electric field. A vertical antenna produces a vertical electric field, so its wave is vertically polarized.\n\nMatch the antenna to the polarization for the strongest signal.", "T3B03": "A radio wave has two parts: an electric field and a magnetic field. They travel together and constantly regenerate each other.\n\nThat's why it's called an electromagnetic wave.", "T3B04": "Radio waves are electromagnetic waves, just like light. In free space they travel at the speed of light.\n\nThat's about 300,000,000 meters per second, the source of the 300 in the wavelength formula.", "T3B05": "Radio waves all travel at the same speed. Higher frequency means more cycles per second, so each cycle must be shorter.\n\nWavelength and frequency are inversely related: double the frequency, halve the wavelength.", "T3B06": "Wavelength in meters equals 300 divided by frequency in MHz. The 300 comes from the speed of light, 300 million meters per second.\n\nExample: 146 MHz gives 300 / 146, about 2 meters. That's why it's called the 2-meter band.", "T3B07": "Amateur bands are often named by their approximate wavelength in meters: 2 meters, 6 meters, 70 centimeters and so on.\n\nUse 300 / MHz to connect the names to frequencies.", "T3B08": "VHF, Very High Frequency, covers 30 to 300 MHz. It includes 6 meters, 2 meters and the 1.25-meter band.\n\nThe ranges go in steps of ten: HF 3 to 30, VHF 30 to 300, UHF 300 to 3,000 MHz.", "T3B09": "UHF, Ultra High Frequency, covers 300 to 3,000 MHz. It includes the 70-centimeter band (420 to 450 MHz) and 33 and 23 centimeters.\n\nPattern: each range is ten times the one below.", "T3B10": "HF, High Frequency, runs 3 to 30 MHz. It holds many ham bands; Technicians have limited privileges on four of them: 80, 40, 15 and 10 meters.\n\nHF is where the ionosphere enables worldwide contacts.", "T3B11": "Radio waves travel at the speed of light, about 300,000,000 meters per second.\n\nThat number gives the 300 in the wavelength formula: wavelength equals 300 divided by frequency in MHz.", "T3B12": "All radio frequencies travel at the same speed in free space: the speed of light.\n\nFrequency changes the wavelength, not the speed.", "T3C01": "UHF signals usually pass straight through the ionosphere instead of bending back to Earth. Without a reflector, they travel in roughly straight lines.\n\nOnce past the radio horizon, the curve of the Earth blocks them, so UHF simplex is mostly local.", "T3C02": "On HF, the ionosphere commonly bends signals back to Earth, enabling contacts across continents.\n\nOn VHF and above, that happens far less often, so those bands are mostly line of sight, with occasional special propagation.\n\nThe other choices are backward: HF antennas are larger (longer wavelengths), HF signals are narrower, and HF has more static, not less.", "T3C03": "Aurora creates a reflecting curtain in the northern sky. VHF signals scattered off it come back distorted, with a raspy, buzzing sound.\n\nVoice can be hard to understand, so CW is often preferred during auroral openings.", "T3C04": "Sporadic E forms patches of dense ionization in the E region of the ionosphere, usually in early summer. It can reflect 10, 6 and sometimes 2 meters.\n\nOpenings come suddenly and bring strong signals from hundreds to over a thousand miles away.", "T3C05": "Knife-edge diffraction occurs when radio waves bend around a sharp edge, such as a ridge or building top.\n\nSome of the signal spills over into the area behind the obstruction, allowing contact where line of sight is blocked.", "T3C06": "The troposphere is the lowest layer of the atmosphere, where weather happens. Tropospheric ducting traps VHF and UHF signals in a layer of it, usually under a temperature inversion, guiding them far beyond the normal horizon.\n\nRanges of around 300 miles are common, and sometimes much more: ducts over the Pacific have carried signals from California to Hawaii.", "T3C07": "Meteors leave short-lived trails of ionized gas that can reflect radio signals. Lower frequencies reflect off these thin trails best, and 6 meters is the lowest-frequency band among the choices, so it works best.\n\nContacts last seconds, so operators use special digital modes. Meteor showers bring the best activity.", "T3C08": "Normally air gets colder with height. In a temperature inversion, a layer of warm air sits above cooler air.\n\nThe boundary bends VHF and UHF signals and can trap them in a duct, carrying them far beyond the horizon.", "T3C09": "Ten-meter F region propagation needs strong solar ionization, so it's best during daylight in high sunspot years.\n\nOpenings run from dawn to shortly after sunset. At night the ionosphere weakens, and 10 meters usually goes quiet.", "T3C10": "At the peak of the sunspot cycle, the F region is ionized enough to return 10 and even 6 meters, making worldwide contacts possible.\n\nThe cycle lasts about 11 years.", "T3C11": "The atmosphere bends VHF and UHF waves slightly back toward the Earth, so they follow its curve a little.\n\nAs a result, the radio horizon is roughly 15 percent farther than the visual horizon.", "T4A01": "Mobile radios are designed for a car's electrical system, which runs about 13.8 volts with the engine on. A 50-watt transmitter isn't perfectly efficient, so it draws roughly 10 to 12 amps.\n\nP = I × E: 13.8 V × 12 A is about 165 watts of input, enough to cover 50 watts of RF plus losses. Choose a supply rated for at least 12 amps; 4 amps is far too little. The 24-volt choices are wrong because mobile radios are built for a car's 12-volt system.", "T4A02": "An SWR meter measures how well your antenna system is matched. Meters are designed for particular frequency ranges and power levels.\n\nAn HF meter may be inaccurate at 440 MHz, and a low-power meter can be damaged by a big amplifier. Match the meter to the frequency and power you'll use.", "T4A03": "When transmitting, a radio may draw 10 amps or more. Long or thin wires have more resistance, so the voltage drops before reaching the radio.\n\nE = I × R: even 0.1 ohm at 12 amps loses 1.2 volts. Short, heavy-gauge wires keep the radio at full voltage.", "T4A04": "For FT8, the computer does the work. The radio's audio output goes to the computer's audio input so the software can decode signals. The computer's audio output goes to the radio's audio input to transmit.\n\nIt's a loop: radio out to computer in, computer out to radio in.", "T4A05": "An RF power meter measures the energy going to the antenna, so it goes in the feed line between the transmitter and the antenna.\n\nMany meters also measure reflected power, which gives SWR.", "T4A06": "A computer-radio interface needs three things: receive audio from radio to computer, transmit audio from computer to radio, and a keying signal to switch the radio into transmit.\n\nMany modern radios handle all three over one USB cable.", "T4A07": "To decode digital signals, the computer needs the radio's received audio. So the radio's speaker output connects to the computer's line in.\n\nThe opposite path, computer out to radio mic or data input, carries transmit audio.", "T4A08": "At radio frequencies, current flows mostly on the surface of a conductor (the skin effect). A flat copper strap has much more surface area than a round wire.\n\nThat gives lower impedance at RF, so bonding works better. Wide and flat beats round and thin.", "T4A09": "A battery's ampere-hour (Ah) rating says how much current it can supply over time. Divide that by your equipment's average current draw to get run time in hours.\n\nExample: a 20 Ah battery with a radio averaging 4 A lasts about five hours.", "T4A10": "A hotspot is a small, low-power device that connects nearby digital radios, like DMR or D-STAR handhelds, to an internet-based digital voice or data network.\n\nIt acts as a personal gateway when no repeater is in range.", "T4A11": "In a vehicle, connect the radio's negative lead at the battery's chassis ground point. That avoids noise and voltage problems from poor body grounds.\n\nThe positive lead goes to the battery too, with fuses close to the battery.", "T4A12": "An electronic keyer helps you send Morse code by hand. Using a paddle, it creates evenly timed dots and dashes automatically.\n\nYou control the rhythm, and the keyer keeps each element precise.", "T4B01": "On SSB, too much microphone gain overdrives the transmitter. The audio becomes distorted, and the signal can spread out and interfere with nearby frequencies.\n\nSet mic gain so the ALC (automatic level control) meter stays within the range the manual recommends.", "T4B02": "Modern transceivers let you set the frequency with the keypad, typing it directly, or by turning the VFO (variable frequency oscillator) knob.\n\nThe other choices don't set frequency: CTCSS and DTMF are audio tones used with repeaters, and AFC only fine-tunes onto a signal you're already near.", "T4B03": "Squelch mutes the receiver when no signal is present. A very weak signal may not be strong enough to open it.\n\nSet the squelch so the audio stays on all the time, accepting the noise, and you'll hear even weak signals.", "T4B04": "An FM receiver works best when tuned exactly to the signal. If you're slightly off frequency, the audio becomes distorted.\n\nThat's different from SSB, where being off frequency makes the voice sound higher or lower in pitch. On FM the pitch doesn't shift; the audio just gets harsh and distorted.", "T4B05": "Scanning steps the radio through a set of frequencies or channels and pauses when it finds activity.\n\nIt's a handy way to find active repeaters or simplex conversations.", "T4B06": "On SSB, a signal slightly off frequency makes the voice sound too high or too low. RIT, Receiver Incremental Tuning (also called the clarifier), shifts only your receive frequency.\n\nYou can fix the voice without moving your transmit frequency.", "T4B07": "A DMR code plug is the configuration file loaded into a DMR radio. It contains repeater frequencies, color codes, talkgroups and contacts.\n\nYou usually create it with programming software and transfer it by cable.", "T4B08": "Different modes use different bandwidths: CW needs a few hundred hertz, SSB about 2.4 kHz, and FM much more.\n\nA filter that matches the mode lets the signal through while blocking noise and interference outside it.", "T4B09": "On DMR, groups are organized as talkgroups, each with an identification number. Select a group by entering its ID in your radio's channel settings.\n\nThen you hear and talk only to that group.", "T4B10": "An SSB voice signal is about 2.4 to 3 kHz wide. A 2,400 Hz filter lets the voice through while cutting extra noise.\n\nA narrower filter would clip the voice, and a wider one would add noise. 2,400 Hz is the sweet spot.", "T4B11": "D-STAR identifies every transmission digitally with your call sign, so the radio must have it programmed in before you transmit.\n\nThe network uses it for routing and identification.", "T5A01": "Current is the flow of electric charge, and its unit is the ampere, or amp. One amp is a lot of electrons passing a point every second.\n\nUnits to keep straight: amps for current, volts for voltage, ohms for resistance, watts for power. Amps are what an ammeter measures in series with a circuit.", "T5A02": "Power is the rate of using or producing energy, measured in watts. A 100-watt transmitter turns 100 joules of energy into RF every second.\n\nWatt-hours measure total energy used over time, like on an electric bill. Watts are the rate; watt-hours are the amount.", "T5A03": "Current is the flow of electrons through a circuit. Voltage is the push that causes the flow, and resistance is what limits it.\n\nThe plumbing picture helps: voltage is water pressure, current is the water flowing, resistance is a narrow pipe.", "T5A04": "Alternating current swings back and forth. One full swing, forward and back, is a cycle. The number of cycles per second is the frequency.\n\nHousehold power in the US is 60 cycles per second, 60 Hz. Radio signals do the same thing millions of times a second.", "T5A05": "Electrons flow because of a difference in voltage, also called electromotive force or potential difference, between two points.\n\nLike water flowing from high pressure to low, electrons move from one end of a battery through the circuit to the other. No voltage difference, no current.", "T5A06": "Frequency is measured in hertz (Hz). One hertz is one cycle per second.\n\nRadio uses multiples: kilohertz (thousands), megahertz (millions), gigahertz (billions). The 2-meter band is around 146 MHz, 146 million cycles per second.", "T5A07": "In metals, outer electrons aren't tightly bound to atoms. They drift freely and can move when a voltage is applied.\n\nThat sea of free electrons makes copper, silver and aluminum excellent conductors.", "T5A08": "Glass holds its electrons tightly, so current can't flow through it easily. That makes it a good insulator.\n\nSea water, steel and graphite all have mobile charges and conduct. Glass and ceramic insulators are common on antennas and power lines.", "T5A09": "Alternating current reverses direction regularly, flowing one way, then the other. It swings between positive and negative many times a second.\n\nCurrent that swings between positive and zero (or negative and zero) never reverses, so it's pulsing direct current, not AC. That rules out the other choices and \"All these.\" Direct current, like from a battery, flows in one direction only.", "T5A10": "Power is the rate at which electrical energy is used or produced, measured in watts.\n\nEnergy is the total amount used; power is how fast you use it. Think of a car trip: power is like speed, energy is like the distance covered.", "T5A11": "Resistance opposes the flow of any current: direct current (DC), alternating current (AC) and radio-frequency current (RF).\n\nThat's why all of the choices are correct. Coils and capacitors add more opposition to AC and RF, called reactance.", "T5B01": "Milli means one-thousandth. There are 1,000 milliamperes in one ampere.\n\nGoing from a bigger unit to a smaller one, multiply: 1.5 A × 1,000 = 1,500 mA. Smaller units, bigger numbers.", "T5B02": "Kilo means thousand. To change hertz to kilohertz, divide by 1,000.\n\n1,500,000 Hz ÷ 1,000 = 1,500 kHz. Going to a bigger unit, the number gets smaller. It's also 1.5 MHz, but that answer isn't offered.", "T5B03": "Kilo means one thousand. One kilovolt is 1,000 volts.\n\nSame prefix as kilometer (1,000 meters) and kilogram (1,000 grams).", "T5B04": "Micro means one-millionth. One microvolt is 0.000001 volt.\n\nThe trap answer, one-thousandth, is milli. Order from small to large: pico, nano, micro, milli, base unit, kilo, mega, giga.", "T5B05": "Milli means one-thousandth, so 1,000 milliwatts make one watt.\n\n500 mW ÷ 1,000 = 0.5 W, half a watt. Converting to the bigger unit, divide.", "T5B06": "There are 1,000 milliamperes in one ampere.\n\n3,000 mA ÷ 1,000 = 3 A. Moving to the bigger unit, divide by 1,000: slide the decimal three places left.", "T5B07": "There are 1,000 kilohertz in a megahertz. Going to the smaller unit, multiply.\n\n3.525 MHz × 1,000 = 3,525 kHz. That frequency is in the 80-meter band.", "T5B08": "Pico means one-trillionth; micro means one-millionth. A microfarad is a million times bigger than a picofarad.\n\nPico to nano is one step of 1,000, nano to micro is another: 1,000 × 1,000 = 1,000,000. So 1,000,000 pF ÷ 1,000,000 = 1 µF.", "T5B09": "Decibels measure ratios on a log scale. Doubling power is about 3 dB.\n\n5 W to 10 W doubles, so +3 dB. The same is true from 50 to 100 W or 500 to 1,000 W.", "T5B10": "Halving power is −3 dB. From 12 W to 6 W is one halving, and 6 W to 3 W is another.\n\nTwo halvings make −6 dB. A quarter of the power is −6 dB.", "T5B11": "Ten times the power is +10 dB. From 20 W to 200 W is a tenfold increase.\n\nKey dB values: 2x = 3 dB, 4x = 6 dB, 10x = 10 dB.", "T5B12": "To change kilohertz to megahertz, divide by 1,000.\n\n28,400 kHz ÷ 1,000 = 28.400 MHz, in the 10-meter band, inside the Technician phone segment.", "T5B13": "Giga means billion; mega means million. There are 1,000 MHz in 1 GHz.\n\n2,425 MHz ÷ 1,000 = 2.425 GHz, near Wi-Fi and microwave oven frequencies.", "T5C01": "Capacitance is the ability to store energy in an electric field, between conductive plates separated by an insulator.\n\nIts partner, inductance, stores energy in a magnetic field. Electric field, capacitance; magnetic field, inductance.", "T5C02": "Capacitance is measured in farads, named after Michael Faraday.\n\nA farad is huge, so circuits use microfarads (µF) and picofarads (pF).", "T5C03": "Inductance is the ability to store energy in a magnetic field, usually in a coil of wire.\n\nCurrent through the coil builds up a magnetic field. When the current changes, the field pushes back, resisting the change.", "T5C04": "Inductance is measured in henries, named after Joseph Henry.\n\nRadio circuits use millihenries (mH) and microhenries (µH).", "T5C05": "Impedance is total opposition to AC current, combining resistance and reactance. Like resistance, it's measured in ohms.\n\nThat's why antennas and coax are rated in ohms, like 50 ohms.", "T5C06": "Kilohertz is written kHz: lowercase k for kilo, capital H for Hertz, lowercase z.\n\nCapital K isn't standard for kilo. The capital H honors Heinrich Hertz.", "T5C07": "Megahertz is MHz: capital M for mega, capital H for Hertz, lowercase z.\n\nCase matters: lowercase m means milli, one-thousandth. MHz (a million hertz) is a billion times bigger than mHz (a thousandth of a hertz).", "T5C08": "Power equals current times voltage: P = I × E. In ham notation, E stands for voltage (electromotive force) and I for current.\n\nMemory aid: PIE. P = I × E.", "T5C09": "P = I × E. 10 amps × 13.8 volts = 138 watts.\n\n13.8 volts is the typical voltage of a car's electrical system, so this is the power for a mobile radio drawing 10 amps.", "T5C10": "P = I × E. 2.5 amps × 12 volts = 30 watts.\n\nCheck: 12 × 2 = 24, plus 12 × 0.5 = 6, total 30.", "T5C11": "Rearrange P = I × E to find current: I = P ÷ E.\n\n120 watts ÷ 12 volts = 10 amps. Memory aid: picture a triangle with P on top and I and E side by side below. Cover I, and P sits over E, so divide.", "T5C12": "Impedance is total opposition to AC current, combining resistance with reactance from coils and capacitors.\n\nIt's measured in ohms. Matching impedance between radio, coax and antenna transfers power efficiently.", "T5D01": "Ohm's law: current equals voltage divided by resistance, I = E ÷ R.\n\nMore voltage means more current; more resistance means less. Memory aid: picture a triangle with E on top and I and R side by side below. Cover I, and E sits over R, so divide.", "T5D02": "Ohm's law: voltage equals current times resistance, E = I × R.\n\nIn the triangle, cover E and I sits beside R, so multiply.", "T5D03": "Ohm's law: resistance equals voltage divided by current, R = E ÷ I.\n\nIn the triangle, cover R and E sits over I, so divide.", "T5D04": "R = E ÷ I = 90 volts ÷ 3 amps = 30 ohms.\n\nCheck: 3 amps × 30 ohms = 90 volts. It works.", "T5D05": "R = E ÷ I = 12 volts ÷ 1.5 amps = 8 ohms.\n\nCheck: 1.5 × 8 = 12. Tip: to divide by 1.5, double both numbers first: 12 ÷ 1.5 is the same as 24 ÷ 3 = 8.", "T5D06": "R = E ÷ I = 12 volts ÷ 4 amps = 3 ohms.\n\nCheck: 4 amps × 3 ohms = 12 volts.", "T5D07": "I = E ÷ R = 120 volts ÷ 80 ohms = 1.5 amps.\n\nThe trap answer, 0.667, comes from dividing the wrong way. Voltage goes on top.", "T5D08": "I = E ÷ R = 200 volts ÷ 100 ohms = 2 amps.\n\nCheck: 2 × 100 = 200.", "T5D09": "I = E ÷ R = 240 volts ÷ 24 ohms = 10 amps.\n\nCheck: 10 × 24 = 240.", "T5D10": "E = I × R = 0.5 amp × 2 ohms = 1 volt.\n\nHalf an amp through two ohms makes one volt.", "T5D11": "E = I × R = 1 amp × 10 ohms = 10 volts.\n\nWith one amp, the voltage equals the resistance.", "T5D12": "E = I × R = 2 amps × 10 ohms = 20 volts.\n\nDouble the current, double the voltage.", "T5D13": "In a series circuit, components connect end to end in one path. The same current flows through every part.\n\nVoltage divides among them, but current stays the same. Series: same current.", "T5D14": "In a parallel circuit, every component connects across the same two points, so each sees the same voltage.\n\nCurrent divides among the branches. Parallel: same voltage.", "T6A01": "A resistor is built to oppose current. It's often a small cylinder with colored bands showing its value in ohms.\n\nResistors limit current, divide voltage and set operating levels. An inductor opposes changes in current, but in a steady DC circuit it acts almost like a plain wire.", "T6A02": "A potentiometer is a resistor with a sliding contact controlled by a knob. Turning it changes the resistance between the slider and the ends.\n\nThat's how a volume control works: the knob sets how much of the audio signal passes on to the amplifier and speaker.", "T6A03": "A potentiometer is a variable resistor, so it controls resistance. Turning the knob moves a contact along a resistive track.\n\nIts name comes from \"potential,\" another word for voltage: it can tap off any fraction of the voltage across it, but what you are actually adjusting is resistance.", "T6A04": "A capacitor stores energy in an electric field between two conductive plates separated by an insulator.\n\nInductors store energy in a magnetic field. Hook: Capacitor, eleCtric; inductor, magnetic.", "T6A05": "A capacitor is two conductive surfaces separated by an insulator called a dielectric.\n\nCharge builds up on the plates, creating an electric field across the insulator. Air, ceramic, plastic and paper are common dielectrics.", "T6A06": "An inductor stores energy in a magnetic field. Current through a coil of wire creates the field.\n\nWhen current changes, the field resists the change. That's what makes inductors useful in filters and tuned circuits.", "T6A07": "An inductor is usually a coil of wire, sometimes wound on a core of iron or ferrite to increase its inductance.\n\nThe schematic symbol even looks like a coil: a series of loops.", "T6A08": "Switches are named by poles and throws. Poles are how many circuits it controls; throws are how many positions each pole can connect to.\n\nSPDT, single-pole double-throw, connects one circuit to either of two others, like each of the two switches that control one stairway light from the top and the bottom (in US stores these are called \"three-way\" switches).", "T6A09": "Component 3 in figure T-2 is a single-pole single-throw (SPST) switch. It simply opens or closes one circuit, like an ordinary on/off switch.\n\nThe symbol is a single lever that touches one contact.", "T6A10": "Nickel-metal hydride (NiMH), lithium-ion and lead-acid batteries can all be recharged many times.\n\nNiMH appears in AA packs, lithium-ion in phones and handhelds, lead-acid in cars and backup systems. All of the choices are correct.", "T6A11": "Carbon-zinc batteries are the cheap disposable cells. Trying to recharge them doesn't work and can make them leak.\n\nNickel-cadmium (NiCd) batteries are rechargeable, so don't fall for that trap.", "T6B01": "When a diode conducts, it drops a small voltage across itself, called the forward voltage drop. It varies by type.\n\nSilicon diodes drop about 0.7 volt. Germanium and Schottky diodes drop less, around 0.2 to 0.4 volt. So it's lower in some types than others.", "T6B02": "A diode allows current to flow in only one direction, like a one-way valve.\n\nCurrent flows from anode to cathode. Diodes are used in rectifiers to change AC to DC.", "T6B03": "A transistor can act as an electronic switch. A small control current or voltage turns a larger current on or off.\n\nTransistors can also amplify. Computers are built from billions of tiny transistor switches.", "T6B04": "A bipolar junction transistor is built from three regions of semiconductor material: NPN or PNP.\n\nTriodes and pentodes are vacuum tubes, not semiconductors.", "T6B05": "A field-effect transistor (FET) has three electrodes: gate, drain and source. The voltage on the gate controls current between drain and source.\n\nBipolar transistors have emitter, base and collector.", "T6B06": "Diodes are usually marked with a stripe or band near the cathode end.\n\nThat matches the bar in the schematic symbol, where the triangle points toward the cathode.", "T6B07": "An LED gives off light when forward current flows through it, from anode to cathode.\n\nReverse current doesn't flow, so the LED stays dark. LEDs are widely used as indicators.", "T6B08": "FET stands for Field Effect Transistor. An electric field from the gate voltage controls current flow.\n\nFETs draw almost no input current, which makes them useful in sensitive circuits.", "T6B09": "A diode's two electrodes are the anode and the cathode. Current flows from anode to cathode.\n\nSource and drain are FET terms, not diode terms.", "T6B10": "Power gain means the output delivers more power than the input. Only an active device, like a transistor or vacuum tube, can do that by drawing energy from a power supply.\n\nA transformer can raise voltage but lowers current, so power out never exceeds power in.", "T6B11": "Gain means an increase in signal: voltage, current or power. An amplifier can provide any of them.\n\nSo all of the choices are correct. Gain is often expressed in decibels.", "T6B12": "A bipolar junction transistor (BJT) has three electrodes: emitter, base and collector.\n\nThe base controls current between the collector and emitter. A small base current controls a much larger collector current.", "T6C01": "A schematic is an electrical diagram using standard symbols. It shows how components connect, not how they look physically.\n\nLearning schematic symbols lets you read and build circuits.", "T6C02": "Component 1 in figure T-1 is a resistor, drawn as a zigzag line.\n\nFigure T-1 key: 1 resistor, 2 transistor, 3 lamp, 4 battery.", "T6C03": "Component 2 in figure T-1 is a transistor, shown as a circle with three leads.\n\nFigure T-1 key: 1 resistor, 2 transistor, 3 lamp, 4 battery.", "T6C04": "Component 3 in figure T-1 is a lamp, a bulb-shaped symbol.\n\nFigure T-1 key: 1 resistor, 2 transistor, 3 lamp, 4 battery.", "T6C05": "Component 4 in figure T-1 is a battery, shown as alternating long and short lines.\n\nFigure T-1 key: 1 resistor, 2 transistor, 3 lamp, 4 battery.", "T6C06": "Component 6 in figure T-2 is a capacitor: two plates, one straight and one curved, with a gap.\n\nFigure T-2 key: 3 SPST switch, 4 transformer, 6 capacitor, 8 LED, 9 variable resistor.", "T6C07": "Component 8 in figure T-2 is a light-emitting diode: a diode symbol with arrows pointing outward to show light.\n\nFigure T-2 key: 3 SPST switch, 4 transformer, 6 capacitor, 8 LED, 9 variable resistor.", "T6C08": "Component 9 in figure T-2 is a variable resistor: a zigzag with an arrow through or pointing into it.\n\nFigure T-2 key: 3 SPST switch, 4 transformer, 6 capacitor, 8 LED, 9 variable resistor.", "T6C09": "Component 4 in figure T-2 is a transformer: two coils side by side with lines between them for the core.\n\nFigure T-2 key: 3 SPST switch, 4 transformer, 6 capacitor, 8 LED, 9 variable resistor.", "T6C10": "Component 3 in figure T-3 is a variable inductor: a coil with an arrow, showing it can be adjusted.\n\nFigure T-3 key: 3 variable inductor, 4 antenna.", "T6C11": "Component 4 in figure T-3 is an antenna: an upside-down triangle on a stem.\n\nFigure T-3 key: 3 variable inductor, 4 antenna.", "T6C12": "A schematic accurately shows how components connect. It doesn't show physical size, location or wire length.\n\nIt's a wiring logic diagram, not a map.", "T6D01": "A rectifier changes AC into varying DC. It uses diodes to let current flow in only one direction.\n\nThe output pulses, so filter capacitors smooth it out in a power supply.", "T6D02": "A relay is a switch operated by electricity. A small current through a coil creates a magnetic field that moves the switch contacts, closing (or on some relays opening) a circuit.\n\nRelays let small control signals switch large currents, like amplifier power.", "T6D03": "Shielded wire has a metal braid or foil around the inner conductors. The shield blocks unwanted signals from getting into the wire and keeps the wire's signals from leaking out.\n\nCoax and shielded audio cables use this principle.", "T6D04": "A meter displays an electrical quantity, like voltage or current, as a numeric value or a needle reading.\n\nDigital multimeters show numbers directly.", "T6D05": "A voltage regulator keeps a power supply's output voltage steady, even when the load or input changes.\n\nRadios need stable voltage to work properly.", "T6D06": "A transformer changes AC voltage up or down using two coils. From 120 V household power, it can produce a lower AC voltage.\n\nThe output is still AC; a rectifier converts it to DC.", "T6D07": "LEDs are commonly used as visual indicators, like power and transmit lights on radios.\n\nA Zener diode regulates voltage and doesn't light up.", "T6D08": "An inductor combined with a capacitor forms a resonant circuit. Energy swings back and forth between the inductor's magnetic field and the capacitor's electric field.\n\nThat tuned circuit picks out one frequency, the basis of radio tuning.", "T6D09": "An integrated circuit (IC) packs many transistors, diodes, resistors and other parts onto one chip.\n\nICs range from simple amplifiers to complex processors.", "T6D10": "Component 2 in figure T-1 is a transistor. Its job is to control the flow of current.\n\nIn that circuit, it switches the lamp on and off. The lamp, component 3, is what gives off light.", "T6D11": "A resonant or tuned circuit is an inductor and a capacitor together, either in series or in parallel.\n\nIt responds strongly to one frequency, which is how radios select stations.", "T7A01": "Sensitivity is how weak a signal a receiver can detect. A sensitive receiver hears stations other radios miss.\n\nIts partner is selectivity, the ability to pick one signal out from others nearby. Sensitivity hears; selectivity separates.", "T7A02": "A transceiver combines a transmitter and a receiver in one unit, sharing parts like the frequency control and power supply.\n\nNearly all modern ham radios are transceivers, from handhelds like the FT-65R to big HF base stations.", "T7A03": "A mixer takes two signals and produces new frequencies at their sum and difference. That shifts a signal from one frequency to another.\n\nExample: mix 14 MHz with 9 MHz and you get 23 MHz and 5 MHz; filters keep the one you want. Receivers use mixers to move every station to one fixed frequency where good filtering is easy. Hook: a mixer mixes, making new frequencies. An oscillator only makes one.", "T7A04": "Selectivity is a receiver's ability to separate the signal you want from others on nearby frequencies. Good filters give good selectivity.\n\nThe trap is sensitivity, which is about hearing weak signals. Hook: Selectivity Selects one signal from many. Sensitivity Senses that a signal is there at all.", "T7A05": "An oscillator generates a steady signal at a specific frequency. Oscillators are at the heart of nearly every transmitter and receiver.\n\nThe VFO, variable frequency oscillator, is the one you tune with the dial.", "T7A06": "A transverter shifts a transceiver's RF to another band. It converts on both receive and transmit.\n\nFor example, it can let a 10-meter radio operate on 2 meters or 222 MHz. Trans-verter, like trans-ceiver: it works both directions.", "T7A07": "PTT means push to talk. The PTT line is normally held open; grounding it, by pressing the mic button or a computer interface, switches the radio from receive to transmit.\n\nDigital mode interfaces use this line to key the radio automatically.", "T7A08": "Modulation is the process of adding information, like your voice, to an RF carrier signal.\n\nAM changes the carrier's strength, FM changes its frequency. Demodulation is the reverse, done in the receiver.", "T7A09": "Some VHF amplifiers have a switch labeled SSB or CW-FM. It sets the amplifier to operate properly in the selected mode.\n\nAn SSB signal's strength rises and falls with your voice, so the amplifier must amplify it faithfully (linearly) and wait a moment before switching back to receive, so it doesn't flip back between words. CW and FM signals stay at one level while on, so the amplifier can run in a simpler, more efficient way. The switch doesn't change your radio's mode; it matches the amplifier's behavior to it.", "T7A10": "An RF power amplifier takes the transceiver's output and boosts it to a higher power, such as from 10 watts to 100.\n\nA potentiometer only adjusts resistance, and changing impedance doesn't create power, so neither of those helps, and \"all these choices\" is wrong too. To raise transmitted power, add an RF power amplifier.", "T7A11": "The VFO, variable frequency oscillator, sets your operating frequency. It's what you're adjusting when you turn the tuning knob.\n\nIt controls both receive and transmit frequency.", "T7B01": "Over-deviating means your voice is swinging the FM signal too far, making it too wide and distorted.\n\nSpeak more softly or hold the mic farther away. That reduces deviation and cleans up your signal.", "T7B02": "If a broadcast AM or FM radio picks up your transmission, the usual cause is that it can't reject strong signals outside its own band.\n\nYour signal may be perfectly clean and on your own frequency; the receiver simply lacks good filtering. This is called fundamental overload.", "T7B03": "RFI, radio frequency interference, can come from fundamental overload (a strong signal swamping a receiver), harmonics (multiples of your frequency) or spurious emissions (unwanted signals from a transmitter).\n\nAll of the choices are correct.", "T7B04": "Solid-state transceivers protect their output transistors. When SWR is high, much of the power reflects back, so the radio automatically lowers its output.\n\nLow power out often means an antenna or feed line problem.", "T7B05": "When your signal interferes with a neighbor's radio or TV, the fix usually goes on their receiver: a filter at its antenna input that blocks your frequency.\n\nTheir receiver isn't rejecting strong nearby signals, and the filter helps it.", "T7B06": "When a neighbor reports interference, start at home. Make sure your station is working properly, then tune your own TV or radio to the same channel and transmit.\n\nIf your own set is clean, the problem is likely their equipment. If yours shows interference too, your station needs fixing first. That's the cooperative, fair first step the FCC expects. Hook: check your own house before knocking on theirs.", "T7B07": "A strong commercial FM broadcast station near 88 to 108 MHz can overload a 2-meter receiver. A band-reject filter (also called a notch filter) blocks that range while letting 2 meters through.\n\nA preamp would boost the interference too and make things worse. Reject the band that's causing trouble.", "T7B08": "If something in a neighbor's home is interfering with your station, every listed step is right, so the answer is all of these.\n\nWork with your neighbor to identify the offending device. Politely explain the FCC rules, which prohibit devices that cause harmful interference. And make sure your own station meets the standards you're asking of them. Cooperation first, rules second, clean house throughout.", "T7B09": "Cable TV interference is often caused by loose or poorly installed coax connectors that let RF leak in.\n\nThe first step is to check that every connector is installed properly and tight.", "T7B10": "Distorted audio through a repeater can come from several things: being off frequency, talking too loudly or too close to the mic, or a poor location with a weak or noisy path.\n\nAll of the choices are correct.", "T7B11": "Sometimes RF from your transmitter travels back down the mic cable and into the radio, distorting your voice.\n\nA clip-on ferrite choke on the mic cable blocks that RF without affecting the audio.", "T7C01": "A dummy load lets you test a transmitter without sending a signal over the air. It absorbs the power and turns it into heat.\n\nThat avoids interference while you adjust or troubleshoot.", "T7C02": "An antenna analyzer sweeps frequencies and shows where the antenna is resonant, which is usually close to where SWR is lowest.\n\nIt helps you tune an antenna for the frequency you want.", "T7C03": "A dummy load is a 50-ohm resistor that doesn't have inductance, mounted on a heat sink.\n\n50 ohms matches the transmitter output. Non-inductive means it looks like a pure resistance at RF. The heat sink gets rid of the heat.", "T7C04": "SWR, standing wave ratio, shows how well the antenna and feed line are matched. 1:1 means a perfect match, with no power reflected.\n\nUnder 2:1 is usually fine; higher means a mismatch to fix.", "T7C05": "High SWR reflects power back toward the transmitter. Solid-state output transistors can be damaged by that reflected power.\n\nSo modern radios reduce output automatically as SWR rises, to protect themselves.", "T7C06": "An SWR of 4:1 means a significant impedance mismatch between the feed line and the antenna. About a third of the power is reflected back toward the transmitter.\n\nSWR is a ratio, not decibels. A tuner or antenna adjustment is needed.", "T7C07": "Power lost in a feed line is converted into heat. Longer and thinner coax loses more, especially at higher frequencies.\n\nUsing low-loss coax keeps more power going to the antenna.", "T7C08": "A directional wattmeter measures forward and reflected power. From those two numbers, you can calculate SWR.\n\nSome meters show SWR directly. \"Iambic pentameter\" is the joke answer.", "T7C09": "Moisture getting into coax is the main way it fails. Water corrodes the shield and changes the dielectric, raising loss.\n\nWeatherproof outdoor connectors carefully.", "T7C10": "Ultraviolet light from the sun breaks down the plastic jacket on coax, causing cracks.\n\nOnce cracked, water gets in and ruins the cable. UV-resistant jackets last much longer outdoors.", "T7C11": "Foam-dielectric coax has air bubbles in its insulation, which lowers loss per foot compared with solid dielectric.\n\nThe trade-off is that foam is less rugged and can absorb moisture more easily.", "T7D01": "A voltmeter measures electric potential, also known as voltage.\n\nVoltage is the difference in electrical potential between two points.", "T7D02": "A voltmeter connects in parallel, across the component, with one probe on each side.\n\nIt measures the voltage difference between those two points.", "T7D03": "To measure current, the meter must have the current flow through it, so it goes in series. Break the circuit and insert the meter.\n\nVoltage: parallel. Current: series.", "T7D04": "An ammeter measures electric current, in amperes. Am-meter: amps meter.\n\nKeep the family straight by unit: voltmeter for volts, ammeter for amps, ohmmeter for ohms, wattmeter for watts. An ammeter connects in series, so the current flows through it.", "T7D05": "An ohmmeter has its own small battery. It pushes a small, known current through the component and measures the voltage that results.\n\nThen it uses Ohm's law, R = E ÷ I, to show resistance. That's why the circuit must be unpowered: the meter supplies its own current. Hook: the ohmmeter does Ohm's law for you.", "T7D06": "On the resistance setting, a multimeter supplies its own current. Connecting it to a live voltage source can push current into the meter and damage it.\n\nAlways select the right function before connecting.", "T7D07": "A multimeter measures several quantities: voltage, resistance and current.\n\nAmong the choices, voltage and resistance is the correct pair.", "T7D08": "Acid-core solder is for plumbing. The acid is corrosive and will eat away at electronic connections over time.\n\nUse rosin-core solder for electronics.", "T7D09": "A cold solder joint forms when the solder doesn't get hot enough or the joint moves while cooling. It looks rough, lumpy or dull.\n\nA good joint is smooth and shiny. Cold joints can fail intermittently.", "T7D10": "An ohmmeter pushes a small current through whatever it touches. A large discharged capacitor starts out empty, so it accepts current easily and the reading starts low.\n\nAs the capacitor charges, current falls, so the reading climbs over time, toward very high resistance. Picture filling a bucket: easy at first, then nothing more goes in. Rising resistance means a capacitor charging.", "T7D11": "When measuring resistance in a circuit, make sure the circuit isn't powered.\n\nAny voltage present can give false readings or damage the meter, since the ohmmeter supplies its own current.", "T8A01": "Amplitude modulation changes the strength of a signal to carry information. Single sideband is a refined form of AM.\n\nSSB removes the carrier and one of the two sidebands, keeping only what carries the voice. That makes it narrower and more efficient than plain AM.", "T8A02": "Packet radio sends data in short bursts. On VHF it usually rides on FM or PM (phase modulation), which are closely related.\n\nThe audio tones carrying the data are fed into an ordinary FM radio, so standard handhelds and mobiles work for packet.", "T8A03": "For weak-signal, long-distance contacts on VHF and UHF, operators use SSB. It packs all the power into a narrow signal, so it gets through where FM wouldn't.\n\nFM is used for local repeater chat.", "T8A04": "VHF and UHF repeaters use FM or PM. FM gives clear, noise-free audio with strong signals and is simple to build into radios.\n\nThat's why handhelds and mobiles default to FM.", "T8A05": "CW (Morse code) is the narrowest of these four, about 150 Hz, because it's just a carrier turned on and off.\n\nSSB voice is about 3 kHz, FM voice 10 to 15 kHz. Slow-scan TV sends still pictures through a voice channel, so it's about 3 kHz too. Don't confuse it with fast-scan TV at 6 MHz.", "T8A06": "Convention: upper sideband (USB) is used on 10 meters, VHF and UHF. Lower sideband (LSB) is the custom on 40, 80 and 160 meters.\n\nRule of thumb: below 10 MHz, LSB; above, USB. (60 meters is the exception: it uses USB.) Digital modes like FT8 use USB on all bands.", "T8A07": "SSB is much narrower than FM, about 3 kHz versus 10 to 15 kHz.\n\nThat lets more signals fit in a band and concentrates power, so SSB reaches farther.\n\nIt is not easier to tune: you have to set the frequency closely or voices sound too high or too low, like a cartoon duck.", "T8A08": "A typical SSB voice signal is about 3 kHz wide, just enough for intelligible speech.\n\nThat's why you need 3 kHz of room from a band edge when operating SSB.", "T8A09": "FM voice on VHF repeaters is about 10 to 15 kHz wide.\n\nThat's why repeater channels are spaced 15 or 20 kHz apart.", "T8A10": "Amateur fast-scan TV is about 6 MHz wide, like old analog broadcast TV.\n\nOnly the wide bands at 70 cm and up have room for it.", "T8A11": "A CW signal needs only about 150 Hz.\n\nIt's just a carrier switched on and off, so it's very narrow. Many CW signals fit where one SSB signal would go.", "T8A12": "FM has a capture effect: the receiver locks onto the strongest signal and suppresses weaker ones. So you hear only one station at a time.\n\nOn SSB, overlapping signals mix together, so you can hear several at once and pick out one.", "T8B01": "Satellite beacons send telemetry about the satellite's health and status: battery voltage, temperatures, solar panel currents and so on.\n\nHams decode it to monitor how the satellite is doing.", "T8B02": "Linear transponder satellites share their limited power among all users. If you transmit too much power, your signal grabs more of it and others get less.\n\nToo much uplink power blocks access by other users.", "T8B03": "Satellite tracking programs show maps of where satellites are, predict pass times with azimuth and elevation for aiming antennas, and calculate Doppler-corrected frequencies.\n\nAll of the choices are correct.", "T8B04": "Amateur satellites use several modes: SSB and CW on linear transponders, FM on single-channel satellites, and data on others.\n\nAll of the choices are correct.", "T8B05": "A satellite beacon is a transmission from the satellite containing status information, its telemetry.\n\nHearing the beacon also tells you the satellite is in range.", "T8B06": "Satellite tracking programs use Keplerian elements, a set of numbers describing the satellite's orbit.\n\nThey're updated regularly so predictions stay accurate.", "T8B07": "Doppler shift is the change in a signal's frequency due to relative motion. As a satellite approaches, its signal appears higher; as it moves away, lower.\n\nLike a passing siren. Operators adjust frequency during a pass to compensate.", "T8B08": "Satellite mode letters show uplink (you to the satellite) then downlink (satellite to you). U/V means uplink on UHF (70 cm) and downlink on VHF (2 m).\n\nV/U would be the reverse. First letter: up.", "T8B09": "Spin fading happens because a satellite and its antennas rotate. As the antennas turn, signal strength rises and falls.\n\nIt's a rhythmic fade tied to the satellite's spin.", "T8B10": "LEO means Low Earth Orbit, a few hundred kilometers up. LEO satellites orbit Earth about every 100 minutes.\n\nThey pass overhead quickly, so contacts are short. The ISS is in LEO.", "T8B11": "Anyone may receive telemetry from amateur satellites. No license is needed to listen.\n\nAmateur signals may not be encrypted to hide their meaning; only the commands sent up to control a satellite may be. That rules out the encryption-key answers. Many schools decode telemetry for science projects.", "T8B12": "With a linear transponder, your downlink signal should be about as strong as the satellite's beacon.\n\nIf you're much louder, you're using too much power and crowding others out.", "T8C01": "Radio direction finding uses directional antennas and receivers to locate a signal's source.\n\nIt's used to track down interference, jammers and lost transmitters.", "T8C02": "A hidden transmitter hunt, or fox hunt, is a game where hams find a hidden transmitter. A directional antenna is key, showing which direction the signal is strongest.\n\nIt's practice in direction finding.", "T8C03": "Contesting means making as many contacts as possible within a set time period, often under specific rules.\n\nScores are based on contacts and multipliers like states or countries.", "T8C04": "In a contest, keep it brief: send only the minimum needed, your call sign and the contest exchange.\n\nLong conversations slow everyone down.", "T8C05": "A grid locator is a letter-number code for a geographic location, like CM87 for much of the San Francisco Bay Area.\n\nEach square is 1 degree of latitude by 2 degrees of longitude, roughly 70 by 100 miles in the US. VHF and UHF contests and awards often use grid squares.", "T8C06": "IRLP connects repeaters over the internet. You access a node from your radio by sending DTMF tones (the touch-tones your keypad makes, like a phone).\n\nThe tones dial up the node you want to connect to.", "T8C07": "VoIP, Voice over Internet Protocol, sends voice over the internet as digital data.\n\nAmateur radio uses VoIP to link repeaters and stations worldwide.", "T8C08": "IRLP, the Internet Radio Linking Project, connects amateur radio systems like repeaters through the internet.\n\nA ham on one repeater can talk through another repeater across the world.", "T8C09": "EchoLink lets licensed hams connect to repeaters and other stations using a computer or smartphone, with no radio needed.\n\nThe internet carries your voice to a linked repeater. IRLP, by contrast, is reached only by radio, and D-STAR and DMR are digital radio modes that need a radio.", "T8C10": "Before using EchoLink, you must register your call sign and provide proof of license.\n\nThat keeps unlicensed people from transmitting through amateur stations.", "T8C11": "A gateway is an amateur station that connects other amateur stations to the internet, such as an EchoLink or Winlink gateway.\n\nIt bridges radio and the internet.", "T8D01": "Packet radio, IEEE 802.11 (the technical name for Wi-Fi, which hams adapt for data networks) and FT8 are all digital modes.\n\nAll of the choices are correct.", "T8D02": "FT8 is a digital mode designed to work at very low signal-to-noise ratios. It can decode signals too weak to hear.\n\nExchanges are short and timed in 15-second cycles.", "T8D03": "APRS, Automatic Packet Reporting System, sends GPS position, text messages, weather data and more.\n\nAll of the choices are correct.", "T8D04": "NTSC is the analog fast-scan color TV standard once used for US broadcast TV.\n\n\"Fast-scan\" means full-motion video like broadcast TV, as opposed to slow-scan still pictures. Hams use it for amateur television on 70 cm and up.", "T8D05": "APRS supports real-time tactical communications, with stations shown on a map.\n\nIt's useful for public service events and emergencies, tracking people and vehicles.", "T8D06": "PSK means Phase Shift Keying. Data is sent by shifting the phase of the signal.\n\nPSK31 is a popular narrow digital mode for keyboard chat.", "T8D07": "DMR, Digital Mobile Radio, uses time slots to fit two digital voice signals in one 12.5 kHz channel. The two take turns, called time-division multiplexing.\n\nThat doubles the capacity of a repeater.", "T8D08": "Packet radio transmissions include a checksum for error detection, a header with the destination call sign, and automatic repeat requests when errors are found.\n\nAll of the choices are correct.", "T8D09": "CW, continuous wave, is another name for Morse code transmission. The carrier is switched on and off in dots and dashes.\n\nIt's simple, narrow and effective with weak signals.", "T8D10": "WSJT-X software supports weak-signal modes for moonbounce (EME), propagation beacons and meteor scatter.\n\nAll of the choices are correct. FT8 is also part of WSJT-X.", "T8D11": "ARQ, Automatic Repeat reQuest, is an error correction method. When the receiver detects an error, it asks the sender to retransmit.\n\nThat ensures data arrives correctly.", "T8D12": "An amateur radio mesh network uses commercial Wi-Fi equipment with modified firmware, running on ham frequencies with more power.\n\nIt creates a high-speed data network for hams, useful in emergencies. Broadband-Hamnet and AREDN are examples.", "T9A01": "A beam antenna concentrates its signal in one direction, like a flashlight instead of a bare bulb. It also hears best in that direction.\n\nA Yagi, the familiar TV-style antenna with several elements, is the classic beam.", "T9A02": "Loading makes an antenna act electrically longer than it physically is. Inserting a coil (inductor) in the radiating element is the common method.\n\nThat's how short mobile HF antennas resonate on bands that would otherwise need much longer antennas.", "T9A03": "An antenna's polarization is described by the orientation of its electric field. A vertical antenna is vertically polarized; a horizontal one is horizontally polarized.\n\nMatching polarization between stations gives the best signal on VHF and UHF.", "T9A04": "The short flexible antenna on a handheld, often called a rubber duck, is compact but inefficient. Much of its energy is lost rather than radiated.\n\nA full-size quarter-wave antenna radiates much better.", "T9A05": "A dipole's resonant frequency depends on its length. Shortening it raises the frequency; lengthening it lowers it.\n\nShorter antenna, shorter wavelength, higher frequency. Adding coils or capacitive end loading does the opposite: the antenna acts longer and resonates lower.", "T9A06": "A Yagi has the most gain of the choices because its directors and reflector focus energy in one direction.\n\nGain comes from concentrating signal, not creating power. An isotropic antenna is an imaginary reference that radiates equally in all directions, so it has no gain.", "T9A07": "A car's metal body acts like a shield. A handheld used inside loses much of its signal strength.\n\nAn external antenna on the roof fixes that.", "T9A08": "A quarter wave on 2 meters is about 19 inches. That length makes the antenna resonant.\n\nThe band's name gives it away: a quarter of a 2-meter wave is half a meter, roughly 20 inches. (Exactly: 300 / 146 MHz is about 2.05 m; a quarter is about 0.51 m.) Real antennas are trimmed a bit shorter, about 19 inches.", "T9A09": "A 5/8-wavelength whip has more gain than a 1/4-wave. It concentrates more of the signal toward the horizon, a lower angle, not a higher one.\n\nThat helps reach distant repeaters from a vehicle.", "T9A10": "A half-wave dipole radiates strongest broadside, at right angles to the wire.\n\nIt sends very little off the ends. Picture a donut with the wire through the hole.", "T9A11": "Antenna gain is the increase in signal strength in a particular direction compared with a reference antenna.\n\nGain doesn't add power; it focuses existing power. Strength in one direction comes at the expense of others.", "T9B01": "Every outdoor connector, whatever the type, should be carefully taped or sealed against the weather.\n\nWater getting into a connector ruins the coax. All of the choices are correct.", "T9B02": "Most amateur coax is 50 ohms, matching the output impedance of most transceivers.\n\nCable TV uses 75-ohm coax, a different standard.", "T9B03": "Coax is easy to use and needs few special considerations. It can run near metal, be taped to a tower and route through walls.\n\nThat convenience, not low loss or low price, makes it the most common feed line. Open-wire line and hardline have less loss.", "T9B04": "An antenna tuner, or coupler, matches the antenna system's impedance to the transceiver's output impedance.\n\nThat lets the transmitter deliver full power safely. It doesn't change the antenna itself.", "T9B05": "Coax loss rises as frequency increases. A cable that's fine on HF may lose much of its signal at UHF.\n\nUse better, lower-loss coax for higher bands.", "T9B06": "Type N connectors are designed for frequencies above 400 MHz. They keep impedance constant and have a gasket that resists moisture, though you still tape them outdoors.\n\nPL-259 connectors are fine on HF and VHF but not ideal at UHF.", "T9B07": "PL-259 connectors, often called UHF connectors despite the name, are common at HF and VHF.\n\nThey're inexpensive and easy to install, but they lose performance at higher frequencies. They aren't watertight, and they screw on; the bayonet type is BNC.", "T9B08": "Coax loses signal from water in connectors, high SWR and extra connectors in the line. With high SWR, reflected power travels back and forth in the cable, losing some on every trip.\n\nEach adds loss, so all of the choices are correct.", "T9B09": "Erratic SWR changes usually point to a loose connection in the antenna or feed line.\n\nAs it moves, the impedance shifts, and the SWR jumps around. Check and tighten connectors.", "T9B10": "RG-213 is thicker than RG-58, with a larger center conductor and dielectric. It has less loss at a given frequency.\n\nRG-58 is thinner and lighter but loses more.", "T9B11": "Air-insulated hardline has the lowest loss. It uses rigid copper with air as most of the dielectric (the insulation between the conductors), and air wastes almost no energy.\n\nIt's expensive and hard to work with, but excellent for long runs at high frequencies.", "T9B12": "SWR, standing wave ratio, measures how well the load (antenna) is matched to the transmission line (feed line).\n\n1:1 is perfect. Higher numbers mean more reflected power."};
